/*
 * AQA GCSE Chemistry (8462) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme, diagram? }] } }
 * Topic ids unchanged so topics-config.js still matches.
 *
 * WHY THIS SHAPE. Real AQA GCSE Chemistry (8462) questions are context-led: a
 * student's investigation, a real-world application, a table of results or a
 * described graph opens each question, practicals are examined in context, and
 * maths skills run throughout. Every item here follows that pattern, written at
 * both Foundation-overlap and Higher demand.
 *
 * TARIFFS. Part tariffs follow the real mix taken from the site's own past-paper
 * data for this specification.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES. Board style: one marking point per line with (1), allow / ignore
 * / do not accept notes, max n; 6-mark extended responses use the board's three-
 * level ladder with indicative content.
 *
 * Data marked "written for this practice question" is invented but realistic and
 * every calculation has been checked.
 * Drawing canvas flag (diagram: true) enables the sketch area in practice.html.
 */

const CHEMISTRY_AQA_GCSE_PRACTICE = {
  '4.1': {
    name: 'Atomic Structure and the Periodic Table',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about the development of the model of the atom.\n\nIn 1932 James Chadwick provided experimental evidence for the existence of a particle in the nucleus that has no charge.\n\nName this particle.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• neutron (1)\nDo not accept neutral particle alone` },
      { q: `This question is about Group 0.\n\nArgon is used to fill electric light bulbs because it does not react with the hot metal filament.\n\nGive the reason why argon is unreactive. Answer in terms of electrons.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• (argon atoms have a) full / stable outer shell (of electrons) (1)\nAccept has eight electrons in its outer shell\nIgnore has a full shell unqualified\nDo not accept argon molecules / argon ions` },
      { q: `This question is about separating mixtures.\n\nSea water is a mixture of water and dissolved salts. On a ship, small amounts of drinking water can be produced from sea water. The sea water is heated, the steam is collected and cooled, and the salts are left behind.\n\nName this method of separation.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• (simple) distillation (1)\nDo not accept fractional distillation / evaporation / crystallisation / filtration` },
      { q: `This question is about electronic structure.\n\nMagnesium is in Group 2 of the periodic table. The atomic number of magnesium is 12.\n\nGive the electronic structure of a magnesium atom.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• 2,8,2 (1)\nAccept 2.8.2 or a correctly drawn diagram with 2, 8 and 2 electrons in successive shells` },
      { q: `This question is about Group 7.\n\nTable 1 shows the states of some Group 7 elements at room temperature.\n\nTable 1\nElement | State at room temperature\nfluorine | gas\nchlorine | gas\nbromine | liquid\niodine | solid\nastatine | ?\n\nPredict the state of astatine at room temperature.`, marks: 1, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `• solid (1)` },
      { q: `This question is about the size of atoms.\n\nThe radius of a sodium atom is 1.9 × 10⁻¹⁰ m. The radius of the nucleus is about 1/10 000 of the radius of the atom.\n\nCalculate the radius of the nucleus of a sodium atom.\nGive your answer in standard form.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 1.9 × 10⁻¹⁴ (m) (1)\nAccept 2 × 10⁻¹⁴ (m)\nDo not accept an answer not in standard form` },
      { q: `This question is about atoms and ions.\n\nAn aluminium ion can be represented as:\n²⁷₁₃Al³⁺\n\nGive the number of protons, neutrons and electrons in this aluminium ion.\nProtons: ______\nNeutrons: ______\nElectrons: ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• protons = 13 (1)\n• neutrons = 14 and electrons = 10 (1)\nBoth needed for the second mark\nAllow 1 mark only for 13, 14, 13 (electrons for the atom, not the ion)` },
      { q: `This question is about isotopes.\n\nCarbon-12 (¹²₆C) and carbon-14 (¹⁴₆C) are isotopes of carbon. Carbon-14 is used to find the age of wood from archaeological sites.\n\nGive one similarity and one difference between an atom of carbon-12 and an atom of carbon-14. Answer in terms of subatomic particles.\nSimilarity: ______\nDifference: ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Similarity, any one from:\n• same number of protons / both have 6 protons (1)\n• same number of electrons / both have 6 electrons (1)\nAccept same atomic number\n\nDifference:\n• carbon-14 has two more neutrons / carbon-12 has 6 neutrons and carbon-14 has 8 neutrons (1)\nAccept different mass number for difference only if linked to neutrons\nIgnore different mass alone` },
      { q: `This question is about elements, compounds and mixtures.\n\nAir contains nitrogen, oxygen, argon and carbon dioxide. Carbon dioxide has the formula CO₂.\n\nExplain why air is a mixture but carbon dioxide is a compound.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (in air) the substances / elements and compounds are not chemically combined / can be separated by physical methods (1)\n• (in carbon dioxide) carbon and oxygen (atoms) are chemically combined / bonded (in fixed proportions) (1)\nIgnore mixed together for the first mark` },
      { q: `This question is about Group 1.\n\nA teacher adds a small piece of sodium to a trough of water. Sodium hydroxide solution and hydrogen gas are produced.\n\nComplete the balanced symbol equation for the reaction.\n___Na + ___H₂O → ___NaOH + ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• H₂ (1)\n• 2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂ (balancing) (1)\nAccept multiples\nThe balancing mark depends on H₂ being correct` },
      { q: `This question is about Group 1.\n\nA teacher demonstrated the reaction of lithium with water in a large trough.\n\nGive two observations the class would see during the reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• fizzing / bubbles / effervescence (1)\n• lithium floats (1)\n• lithium moves (around on the surface) (1)\n• lithium gets smaller / disappears (1)\nIgnore gas produced / hydrogen produced (not observations)\nDo not accept melts into a ball / burns with a lilac flame (sodium / potassium)` },
      { q: `This question is about Group 1.\n\nA student added a few drops of universal indicator to the solution left after potassium reacted with water.\n\nGive the colour of the universal indicator.\nGive a reason for your answer.\nColour: ______\nReason: ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• purple (1)\nAccept blue / violet\n• (because) potassium hydroxide is formed / the solution is alkaline / contains hydroxide ions (1)\nIgnore pH 14 alone` },
      { q: `This question is about Group 7.\n\nA student added chlorine water to colourless potassium bromide solution. The solution turned orange.\n\nExplain why the solution turned orange.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• bromine is produced / displaced (1)\n• because chlorine is more reactive than bromine (1)\nAllow 1 mark for chlorine displaces bromide without reference to reactivity\nDo not accept chloride is more reactive` },
      { q: `This question is about Group 7.\n\nA student added iodine solution to sodium chloride solution. There was no reaction.\n\nExplain why there was no reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• iodine is less reactive than chlorine (1)\n• so iodine cannot displace chlorine / chloride (from sodium chloride) (1)\nAccept reverse argument: chlorine is more reactive so would stay in the compound` },
      { q: `This question is about the development of the periodic table.\n\nIn 1869 Dmitri Mendeleev published a periodic table. He left gaps in his table.\n\nExplain why Mendeleev left gaps in his periodic table.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• for elements that had not been discovered (yet) (1)\n• so that elements with similar properties were in the same group / column (1)\nAccept so that he could predict the properties of the missing elements for the second mark` },
      { q: `This question is about the development of the periodic table.\n\nIn Mendeleev's periodic table, argon (relative atomic mass 39.9) would have been placed after potassium (relative atomic mass 39.1) if the elements were in order of atomic weight. In the modern periodic table argon is placed before potassium.\n\nExplain why argon is placed before potassium in the modern periodic table.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the modern periodic table is arranged in order of atomic number / proton number (1)\n• argon has a lower atomic number than potassium (18 compared with 19) (1)\nAccept argon has a higher relative atomic mass because of the (relative abundance of) isotopes / more neutrons for the second mark\nAccept so that argon is in the same group as the other noble gases / elements with similar properties for the second mark` },
      { q: `This question is about the alpha particle scattering experiment.\n\nIn the alpha particle scattering experiment, positively charged alpha particles were fired at a very thin sheet of gold.\nMost of the alpha particles passed straight through the gold.\nA small number of alpha particles were deflected (changed direction).\n\nWhat do these two results show about the structure of the atom?`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (most passed through so) the atom is mainly empty space (1)\n• (some deflected so) the (centre / nucleus of the) atom is positively charged / positive charge is concentrated in the centre (1)\nAccept mass is concentrated in the nucleus for the second mark` },
      { q: `This question is about models of the atom.\n\nThe plum pudding model described the atom as a ball of positive charge with electrons embedded in it. The model used today is the nuclear model.\n\nGive two differences between the plum pudding model and the nuclear model.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• in the nuclear model the positive charge / mass is concentrated in a nucleus (instead of spread throughout the atom) (1)\n• in the nuclear model electrons are in shells / energy levels / orbit the nucleus (instead of being embedded) (1)\n• the nuclear model has neutrons / protons (in the nucleus) (1)\n• the nuclear model is mostly empty space (1)\nA comparison must be implied, e.g. nucleus vs no nucleus` },
      { q: `This question is about isotopes and relative atomic mass.\n\nBoron has two isotopes. Table 2 shows the abundance of each isotope in a sample of boron.\n\nTable 2\nMass number of isotope | Percentage abundance (%)\n10 | 19.9\n11 | 80.1\n\nCalculate the relative atomic mass (Aᵣ) of boron.\nGive your answer to 1 decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (10 × 19.9) + (11 × 80.1) / 100 (1)\n• = 10.8 (1)\nAn answer of 10.8 scores 2 marks\nAllow 10.801 for 1 mark if not rounded to 1 decimal place` },
      { q: `This question is about Group 0.\n\nTable 3 shows the boiling points of some Group 0 elements.\n\nTable 3\nElement | Boiling point in °C\nhelium | −269\nneon | −246\nargon | −186\nkrypton | ?\nxenon | −108\n\nDescribe the trend in boiling points of Group 0 elements.\nPredict the boiling point of krypton.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• boiling point increases going down the group / with increasing relative atomic mass (1)\nAccept boiling point gets less negative down the group\n• any value from −170 to −125 (°C) (1)\nThe actual value is −153 °C` },
      { q: `This question is about transition metals (chemistry only).\n\nPotassium manganate(VII) is purple. Manganese(IV) oxide is black and speeds up the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide without being used up. Manganese forms ions with different charges, such as Mn²⁺ and Mn⁴⁺.\n\nThe information shows that manganese has typical properties of a transition metal.\nGive two of these properties.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• forms coloured compounds (1)\n• (compounds) are (useful as) catalysts (1)\n• forms ions with different charges (1)\nIgnore high melting point / high density / hard (not shown by the information)` },
      { q: `This question is about metals and non-metals.\n\nMagnesium reacts with oxygen to form magnesium oxide. Magnesium oxide contains Mg²⁺ ions.\n\nExplain why magnesium forms ions with a 2+ charge. Answer in terms of electrons.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• a magnesium atom has two electrons in its outer shell (1)\n• which it loses (to form a stable / full outer shell) (1)\nDo not accept gains two electrons / shares electrons\nA reference to Mg²⁺ having more protons than electrons can gain the second mark` },
      { q: `This question is about separating mixtures.\n\nWine is a mixture of mainly ethanol and water. Ethanol boils at 78 °C and water boils at 100 °C. A student used fractional distillation to separate ethanol from a sample of wine.\n\nExplain how fractional distillation separates the ethanol from the water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• ethanol has a lower boiling point (than water) so it evaporates / boils first (1)\n• the ethanol vapour is condensed (and collected) (1)\nAccept water condenses in the fractionating column and returns to the flask\nIgnore references to the thermometer reading alone` },
      { q: `This question is about crystallisation.\n\nA student has a solution of copper sulfate in water. The student wants to obtain crystals of copper sulfate from the solution.\n\nDescribe how the student could produce crystals of copper sulfate from the solution.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• heat the solution (in an evaporating basin) to evaporate some of the water (1)\nAccept heat until crystals start to form / until the solution is saturated\n• leave (the solution) to cool / for the rest of the water to evaporate, so crystals form (1)\nAccept then filter off the crystals and dry them for the second mark\nDo not accept heat until all the water has evaporated / to dryness` },
      { q: `This question is about subatomic particles.\n\nAtoms contain protons, neutrons and electrons.\n\nComplete the table.\n\nParticle | Relative mass | Relative charge\nproton | 1 | ______\nneutron | ______ | 0\nelectron | very small | ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• proton: +1 (1)\n• neutron relative mass: 1 and electron relative charge: −1 (1)\nBoth needed for the second mark\nAccept + / positive for proton; − / negative for electron` },
      { q: `This question is about electronic structure.\n\nThe atomic number of aluminium is 13.\n\nDraw a diagram to show the electronic structure of an aluminium atom.\nUse a dot or a cross to represent each electron, arranged in shells (circles) around a central nucleus.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• three shells drawn, with 13 electrons in total (1)\n• 2 electrons in the first shell, 8 in the second shell and 3 in the outer shell (1)\nAccept electrons anywhere on the shell; pairing not required\nAllow 1 mark for 2,8,3 written instead of drawn` },
      { q: `This question is about Group 7.\n\nSodium chloride is produced when sodium burns in chlorine gas. Chlorine exists as diatomic molecules.\n\nComplete the balanced symbol equation for the reaction.\n___Na + ______ → ___NaCl`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• Cl₂ (1)\n• 2Na + Cl₂ → 2NaCl (1)\nAccept multiples\nThe balancing mark depends on Cl₂ being correct` },
      { q: `This question is about Group 1.\n\nPotassium reacts more vigorously with water than sodium does.\n\nExplain why potassium is more reactive than sodium. Answer in terms of electronic structure.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• potassium atoms are larger / have more shells (than sodium atoms) (1)\n• so the outer electron is further from the nucleus / there is more shielding, so there is weaker attraction between the nucleus and the outer electron (1)\n• so the outer electron is lost more easily (1)\nDo not accept potassium has more outer electrons\nIgnore references to the number of protons alone` },
      { q: `This question is about Group 7.\n\nFluorine is the most reactive element in Group 7. Reactivity decreases going down the group.\n\nExplain why the reactivity of the Group 7 elements decreases going down the group.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• going down the group the atoms are larger / have more shells (of electrons) (1)\n• so the outer shell is further from the nucleus / there is more shielding, so the attraction between the nucleus and an incoming electron is weaker (1)\n• so it is harder (for the atom) to gain an electron (1)\nDo not accept harder to lose electrons\nIgnore references to molecules or ions being larger` },
      { q: `This question is about isotopes and relative atomic mass.\n\nRubidium has two naturally occurring isotopes, rubidium-85 and rubidium-87. The relative atomic mass (Aᵣ) of rubidium is 85.5\n\nCalculate the percentage abundance of rubidium-85.\nPercentage abundance of rubidium-85 = ______ %`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• let percentage of rubidium-85 = x, so percentage of rubidium-87 = (100 − x) (1)\n• 85x + 87(100 − x) = 8550 (1)\nAccept (85x + 87(100 − x)) / 100 = 85.5\n• x = 75 (%) (1)\nAn answer of 75 (%) scores 3 marks\nAllow 2 marks for 25 (%) (abundance of rubidium-87)` },
      { q: `This question is about the alpha particle scattering experiment.\n\nIn 1909 alpha particles were fired at a thin sheet of gold foil. The results were:\n• most alpha particles passed straight through the foil\n• some alpha particles were deflected by small angles\n• a very small number of alpha particles bounced back.\n\nThese results led scientists to replace the plum pudding model with the nuclear model.\n\nExplain how these results support the nuclear model of the atom.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (most passed through so) most of the atom is empty space (1)\n• (some deflected so) the nucleus / centre of the atom is positively charged (and repels alpha particles) (1)\n• (very few bounced back so) the mass is concentrated in a very small nucleus / the nucleus is very small (1)\nEach result must be correctly linked to its conclusion\nIgnore references to electrons` },
      { q: `This question is about the development of the periodic table.\n\nIn 1871 Mendeleev left a gap below silicon for an undiscovered element he called eka-silicon. He predicted its properties. In 1886 the element germanium was discovered. Its properties closely matched Mendeleev's predictions.\n\nExplain why Mendeleev's periodic table became accepted by other scientists.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• he left gaps for undiscovered elements (1)\n• he predicted the properties of the missing elements (1)\n• (when these elements, e.g. germanium, were discovered) their properties matched / fitted the predictions (1)\nAccept he changed the order of some elements based on atomic weight so that elements with similar properties were in the same group, for 1 mark (max 3)` },
      { q: `This question is about the periodic table.\n\nElement X is in the third period of the periodic table. An atom of element X has the electronic structure 2,8,7\n\nWhich group of the periodic table is element X in?\nIs element X a metal or a non-metal?\nGive a reason for your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• Group 7 (1)\n• non-metal (1)\n• it gains an electron (to form a negative ion) / it is on the right of the periodic table / it has seven electrons in its outer shell (1)\nThe reason must match non-metal\nIgnore naming element X as chlorine` },
      { q: `This question is about separating mixtures.\n\nA student wants to obtain pure water from a sample of salty water using simple distillation. The student heats the salty water in a flask. The flask is connected to a condenser, and the distilled water is collected in a beaker.\n\nExplain how simple distillation produces pure water from salty water.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the water evaporates / boils (when heated) (1)\n• the salt does not evaporate / has a much higher boiling point so stays in the flask (1)\n• the water vapour / steam is cooled and condenses in the condenser (and is collected) (1)\nDo not accept the salt evaporates / condenses` },
      { q: `This question is about separating mixtures.\n\nRock salt is a mixture of sand and sodium chloride. Sodium chloride dissolves in water. Sand does not dissolve in water.\n\nDescribe how a student could obtain dry sodium chloride crystals from rock salt.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• crush the rock salt / grind with a pestle and mortar (1)\n• add (warm) water and stir (to dissolve the sodium chloride) (1)\n• filter (to remove the sand) (1)\n• heat the filtrate / solution to evaporate (some of) the water, then leave to crystallise (1)\nAccept dry the crystals in a warm oven / between filter papers as an alternative final step\nmax 4` },
      { q: `This question is about Group 7 displacement reactions.\n\nA student added solutions of halogens to solutions of potassium halides. Table 4 shows the results.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nTable 4\nHalogen solution added | potassium chloride | potassium bromide | potassium iodide\nchlorine water | no change | orange solution | brown solution\nbromine water | no change | no change | brown solution\niodine solution | no change | no change | no change\n\nExplain how the results show the order of reactivity of chlorine, bromine and iodine.\nWrite the ionic equation for the reaction between chlorine and bromide ions.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• chlorine displaces bromine and iodine (from their compounds) (1)\n• bromine displaces iodine but not chlorine / iodine displaces neither (1)\n• so the order of reactivity is chlorine > bromine > iodine (1)\nA more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive halogen from its salt\n• Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂ (1)\nIgnore state symbols\nAccept multiples` },
      { q: `This question is about transition metals (chemistry only).\n\nTable 5 shows some properties of chromium, a transition metal, and potassium, a Group 1 metal.\n\nTable 5\nProperty | Chromium | Potassium\nMelting point in °C | 1907 | 63.5\nDensity in g/cm³ | 7.19 | 0.86\nReaction with cold water | no reaction | reacts vigorously\nColour of compounds | many are green, orange or yellow | white\n\nCompare the properties of chromium with the properties of potassium.\nUse Table 5 and your own knowledge.`, marks: 4, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Any four from:\n• chromium has a (much) higher melting point (1)\n• chromium has a (much) higher density / potassium is less dense than water (1)\n• chromium is less reactive / does not react with water but potassium does (1)\n• chromium forms coloured compounds, potassium forms white compounds (1)\n• chromium is harder / stronger (1)\n• chromium forms ions with different charges (e.g. Cr²⁺ and Cr³⁺), potassium only forms K⁺ / 1+ ions (1)\n• chromium (compounds) can act as catalysts (1)\nEach point must be a comparison\nMere quoting of data from the table without comparison = max 2` },
      { q: `This question is about isotopes and relative atomic mass.\n\nCopper has two isotopes, ⁶³₂₉Cu and ⁶⁵₂₉Cu. Table 6 shows the abundance of each isotope in a sample of copper.\n\nTable 6\nIsotope | Percentage abundance (%)\ncopper-63 | 69.2\ncopper-65 | 30.8\n\nGive the number of neutrons in an atom of copper-65.\nCalculate the relative atomic mass (Aᵣ) of copper. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.\nExplain why the relative atomic mass of copper is not a whole number.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 36 (neutrons) (1)\n• (63 × 69.2) + (65 × 30.8) / 100 (1)\n• = 63.6 (1)\nAn answer of 63.6 scores 2 marks for the calculation\nAllow 63.616 for 1 calculation mark if not rounded\n• copper exists as (a mixture of) isotopes / atoms with different masses (1)\n• the relative atomic mass is a (weighted) mean / average of the masses of the isotopes (taking abundance into account) (1)` },
      { q: `This question is about the development of the model of the atom.\n\nScientists' ideas about the structure of atoms have changed over time as new evidence was found.\n\nDescribe how the model of the atom has changed from the early 1800s to the model used today.\nIn your answer you should include the evidence that led to each change.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed and coherent description of the development of the model in sequence, with most changes clearly linked to the evidence that caused them.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A description of several models with some links to evidence. The sequence may not always be clear.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements about one or more models, with little or no reference to evidence.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• before electrons were discovered, atoms were thought to be tiny spheres that could not be divided\n• discovery of the electron led to the plum pudding model\n• plum pudding model: a ball of positive charge with (negative) electrons embedded in it\n• alpha particle scattering experiment: alpha particles fired at thin gold foil\n• most passed through so the atom is mostly empty space\n• some deflected / a few bounced back so the positive charge and mass are concentrated in a small nucleus\n• this led to the nuclear model, replacing the plum pudding model\n• Niels Bohr: electrons orbit the nucleus at specific distances / in shells / energy levels\n• Bohr's calculations agreed with experimental observations\n• later experiments showed the positive charge of the nucleus is made of smaller particles, each with the same amount of positive charge (protons)\n• James Chadwick provided evidence for neutrons in the nucleus (about 20 years after the nucleus became accepted)` },
      { q: `This question is about Group 1.\n\nA teacher demonstrated the reactions of lithium, sodium and potassium with water. A small piece of each metal was added separately to a large trough of water containing universal indicator.\n\nDescribe what the class would observe for each metal and explain the trend in reactivity going down Group 1.\nInclude a word equation for one of the reactions.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Observations for all three metals compared, showing the trend, with a clear explanation of the trend in terms of electronic structure and a correct word equation.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Observations for at least two metals with the trend identified, and some attempt to explain the trend or a correct word equation.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple observations for at least one metal or a simple statement of the trend.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\nObservations\n• all float, fizz / produce bubbles, move on the surface and get smaller / disappear\n• the universal indicator turns purple / blue (alkaline solution formed)\n• lithium fizzes steadily / slowly\n• sodium melts into a ball and moves quickly\n• potassium reacts most vigorously, melts, and the hydrogen ignites with a lilac flame\nEquation\n• e.g. sodium + water → sodium hydroxide + hydrogen\nExplanation\n• reactivity increases going down the group\n• all have one electron in the outer shell, which is lost in the reaction\n• going down the group atoms are larger / have more shells\n• the outer electron is further from the nucleus / more shielded\n• so the attraction between the nucleus and the outer electron is weaker\n• so the outer electron is lost more easily` }
    ]
  },
  '4.2': {
    name: 'Bonding, Structure and the Properties of Matter',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about substances and their states.\n\nTable 1 shows the melting points and boiling points of four substances, A, B, C and D (data written for this practice question).\n\nSubstance | Melting point in °C | Boiling point in °C\nA | −210 | −196\nB | −95 | 69\nC | 44 | 280\nD | 801 | 1413\n\nWhich substance is a liquid at 25 °C?\n\nTick (✓) one box.\nA\nB\nC\nD`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• B (1)\nIf more than one box is ticked, award 0 marks` },
      { q: `This question is about salt.\n\nA student dissolves sodium chloride in water to make a solution.\n\nGive the state symbol for sodium chloride dissolved in water.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• (aq) (1)\nALLOW aq / aqueous\nDO NOT ACCEPT (l)` },
      { q: `This question is about covalent molecules.\n\nOxygen gas is made of O₂ molecules. The two oxygen atoms in each molecule are joined by a double covalent bond.\n\nHow many electrons are shared between the two oxygen atoms in an O₂ molecule?`, marks: 1, command: 'How many', markScheme: `• 4 (1)\nALLOW two pairs (of electrons)` },
      { q: `This question is about the bonding in different substances.\n\nMethane (CH₄) is the main gas in natural gas. Methane is a compound of two non-metals.\n\nComplete the sentence.\n\nChoose the answer from the box: covalent / ionic / metallic\n\nThe bonds between the carbon atom and the hydrogen atoms in a methane molecule are ______ bonds.`, marks: 1, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• covalent (1)` },
      { q: `This question is about particles in the air.\n\nScientists classify particles in the air by their diameter. Nanoparticles have diameters between 1 nm and 100 nm.\n\n1 nm = 1 × 10⁻⁹ m\n\nWhich diameter is the diameter of a nanoparticle?\n\nTick (✓) one box.\n6 × 10⁻⁵ m\n3 × 10⁻⁶ m\n4 × 10⁻⁷ m\n5 × 10⁻⁸ m`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• 5 × 10⁻⁸ m (1)\n(= 50 nm)\nIf more than one box is ticked, award 0 marks` },
      { q: `This question is about ionic compounds.\n\nLithium oxide is an ionic compound. It contains lithium ions (Li⁺) and oxide ions (O²⁻).\n\nGive the formula of lithium oxide.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• Li₂O (1)\nALLOW Li2O\nDO NOT ACCEPT LiO / LI₂O / Li²O` },
      { q: `This question is about covalent bonding.\n\nHydrogen chloride (HCl) is a gas. It is made of small molecules. A hydrogen atom has 1 electron and a chlorine atom has 7 electrons in its outer shell.\n\nDraw a dot and cross diagram to show the bonding in a molecule of hydrogen chloride.\n\nShow the outer shell electrons only.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• One shared pair of electrons (one dot and one cross) between H and Cl (1)\n• Six further (non-bonding) electrons on the chlorine atom, so chlorine has 8 electrons in its outer shell in total (1)\nALLOW circles drawn or omitted\nALLOW all dots or all crosses for chlorine's electrons provided the shared pair is shown as one dot and one cross\nIGNORE inner shells if correct` },
      { q: `This question is about states of matter.\n\nA student heats a beaker of solid wax. The wax melts.\n\nDescribe what happens to the particles of wax as the wax melts.\n\nAnswer in terms of energy and forces.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• The particles gain (kinetic) energy / vibrate more (1)\n• (Enough energy to) overcome the forces between the particles (so particles can move past each other) (1)\nALLOW bonds between particles for forces between particles for this mark\nIGNORE the particles expand\nDO NOT ACCEPT the particles melt` },
      { q: `This question is about carbon.\n\nDiamond is used in the tips of drills for cutting through rock.\n\nExplain why diamond is very hard.\n\nRefer to the structure and bonding of diamond in your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Each carbon atom is (covalently) bonded to four other carbon atoms / diamond has a giant covalent structure (1)\n• (Many) strong covalent bonds (that are hard to break) (1)\nDO NOT ACCEPT intermolecular forces / ionic bonds (max 1)\nIGNORE layers` },
      { q: `This question is about magnesium oxide.\n\nMagnesium burns in oxygen to produce magnesium oxide. Magnesium oxide is an ionic compound.\n\nThe electronic structure of a magnesium atom is 2,8,2. The electronic structure of an oxygen atom is 2,6.\n\nDescribe what happens to the electrons when a magnesium atom reacts with an oxygen atom.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (Each) magnesium atom loses two electrons (1)\n• (Each) oxygen atom gains two electrons (1)\nALLOW 2 electrons are transferred from magnesium to oxygen for 2 marks\nDO NOT ACCEPT sharing of electrons (0 marks)` },
      { q: `This question is about sodium chloride.\n\nA student tested whether sodium chloride conducts electricity. Solid sodium chloride did not conduct. Sodium chloride solution did conduct.\n\nExplain why sodium chloride solution conducts electricity but solid sodium chloride does not.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• In the solution the ions can move (and carry charge) (1)\n• In the solid the ions are held in fixed positions / in a lattice / cannot move (1)\nDO NOT ACCEPT electrons moving (max 1)\nIGNORE free / delocalised (unless referring to electrons)` },
      { q: `This question is about fuels.\n\nMethane, CH₄, has a boiling point of −162 °C.\n\nExplain why methane has a low boiling point.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (Methane is made of) small molecules with weak (intermolecular) forces between the molecules (1)\n• (So) little energy is needed to overcome these forces (1)\nDO NOT ACCEPT covalent bonds break / weak covalent bonds (max 1)` },
      { q: `This question is about metals.\n\nCopper is used to make electrical wires.\n\nExplain why copper is a good conductor of electricity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (Copper has) delocalised electrons (1)\n• (Which) move through the (whole) structure / carry charge (through the metal) (1)\nALLOW free electrons / sea of electrons\nDO NOT ACCEPT ions moving (max 1)` },
      { q: `This question is about polymers.\n\nEthene (C₂H₄) is a gas at room temperature. Poly(ethene) is a polymer made from ethene. Poly(ethene) is a solid at room temperature.\n\nExplain why poly(ethene) is a solid at room temperature.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Poly(ethene) molecules are very large / are long chains (1)\n• (So) the intermolecular forces (between poly(ethene) molecules) are (relatively) strong (1)\nALLOW stronger forces between molecules than in ethene\nDO NOT ACCEPT strong covalent bonds between molecules` },
      { q: `This question is about nanoparticles.\n\nZinc oxide nanoparticles are used in some sunscreens. A student models a zinc oxide nanoparticle as a cube with sides of 10 nm.\n\nCalculate the surface area to volume ratio of the cube.\n\nGive the unit.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Surface area = 6 × 10 × 10 = 600 (nm²) and volume = 10 × 10 × 10 = 1000 (nm³) (1)\n• (ratio = 600 ÷ 1000 =) 0.6 nm⁻¹ / 0.6 per nm (1)\nALLOW 0.6 / nm\nALLOW 3 : 5 or 0.6 : 1 for the second mark if no unit is given\nIf no unit, max 1 unless given as a ratio` },
      { q: `This question is about models of particles.\n\nThe particle model of states of matter shows particles as small solid spheres.\n\nGive two limitations of the particle model.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• There are no forces (shown between the particles) (1)\n• All particles are shown as spheres (1)\n• The spheres / particles are solid / particles are not really solid (1)\nALLOW particles shown as the same size / do not show the true size of particles (1)\nALLOW does not show the space between particles to scale (1)\nALLOW does not show (the movement of) particles\nmax 2` },
      { q: `This question is about the halogens.\n\nTable 2 shows the boiling points of chlorine and bromine.\n\nHalogen | Formula | Boiling point in °C\nChlorine | Cl₂ | −34\nBromine | Br₂ | 59\n\nExplain why bromine has a higher boiling point than chlorine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Bromine molecules are larger (than chlorine molecules) / have a greater Mr (1)\n• (So) there are stronger intermolecular forces (which need more energy to overcome) (1)\nDO NOT ACCEPT stronger covalent bonds / bonds break (max 1)` },
      { q: `This question is about carbon nanotubes.\n\nCarbon nanotubes are cylindrical fullerenes. Carbon nanotubes are added to the frames of some tennis rackets.\n\nGive two properties of carbon nanotubes that make them useful for reinforcing tennis racket frames.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• High tensile strength / strong (1)\n• Low density / lightweight (1)\n• High length to diameter ratio / very long compared with width (1)\nIGNORE hard / flexible\nIGNORE conducts electricity / heat` },
      { q: `This question is about sunscreens.\n\nSome sunscreens contain nanoparticles of titanium dioxide. Older sunscreens contain larger particles of titanium dioxide.\n\nGive one advantage and one disadvantage of using nanoparticles of titanium dioxide in sunscreens.\n\nAdvantage: ______\nDisadvantage: ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Advantage, any one from:\n• Better protection from ultraviolet / UV radiation (1)\n• Better skin coverage / less (white) residue on the skin / transparent on the skin (1)\nDisadvantage, any one from:\n• Possible / unknown effects on health (1)\n• Nanoparticles may be absorbed into the skin / body (cells) (1)\n• May be harmful to the environment (when washed off) (1)\nIGNORE cost` },
      { q: `This question is about sand.\n\nSand is mainly silicon dioxide (silica). Silicon dioxide has a melting point of 1710 °C. Silicon dioxide has a giant covalent structure.\n\nExplain why silicon dioxide has a high melting point.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (Many) strong covalent bonds (between atoms) (1)\n• (Which need) a lot of energy to break (1)\nDO NOT ACCEPT intermolecular forces / ionic bonds (0 marks)\nIGNORE giant structure (given in the question)` },
      { q: `This question is about ammonia.\n\nAmmonia (NH₃) is made of small molecules. A nitrogen atom has 5 electrons in its outer shell. A hydrogen atom has 1 electron.\n\nDraw a dot and cross diagram to show the bonding in a molecule of ammonia.\n\nShow the outer shell electrons only.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• Three shared pairs (each one dot and one cross) between nitrogen and three hydrogen atoms (1)\n• One non-bonding (lone) pair of electrons on nitrogen, so nitrogen has 8 electrons in its outer shell in total (1)\nALLOW circles drawn or omitted\nIGNORE inner shells if correct` },
      { q: `This question is about metals.\n\nAluminium is a metal. It is used to make drinks cans.\n\nDescribe the structure and bonding in aluminium.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (Giant structure of) positive ions arranged in a regular pattern / in layers (1)\n• (Strong electrostatic attraction between the positive ions and) delocalised electrons (1)\nALLOW sea of electrons / free electrons\nDO NOT ACCEPT atoms for ions in the first marking point\nDO NOT ACCEPT ionic / covalent bonding (max 1)` },
      { q: `This question is about sodium chloride.\n\nSodium chloride is made of sodium ions and chloride ions. The electronic structure of a sodium atom is 2,8,1. The electronic structure of a chlorine atom is 2,8,7.\n\nGive the electronic structure of a sodium ion and of a chloride ion.\n\nSodium ion: ______\nChloride ion: ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• Sodium ion: 2,8 (1)\n• Chloride ion: 2,8,8 (1)\nALLOW [2,8]⁺ and [2,8,8]⁻\nIGNORE charges` },
      { q: `This question is about air pollution.\n\nFine particles (PM2.5) in the air can have a diameter of 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m. A nanoparticle of soot has a diameter of 2.5 × 10⁻⁸ m.\n\nHow many times larger is the diameter of the fine particle than the diameter of the nanoparticle?`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (2.5 × 10⁻⁶) ÷ (2.5 × 10⁻⁸) (1)\n• = 100 (times) (1)\nALLOW 1 × 10² / 10²\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `This question is about methane.\n\nA teacher uses a ball and stick model to represent a molecule of methane (CH₄). The model has one black ball for carbon and four white balls for hydrogen, joined by plastic sticks.\n\nGive two limitations of using a ball and stick model to represent a methane molecule.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• Bonds are not physical sticks / bonds are shown as solid sticks (1)\n• Does not show the electrons (in the bonds) / does not show which atom the electrons come from (1)\n• Atoms are not the size shown / balls are not to scale / bond lengths not to scale (1)\n• Atoms are not coloured / are not solid balls (1)\n• Does not show that atoms / the molecule move (1)\nmax 2` },
      { q: `This question is about carbon.\n\nBuckminsterfullerene (C₆₀) melts / changes state at a much lower temperature than diamond.\n\nExplain why C₆₀ changes state at a much lower temperature than diamond.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• C₆₀ is made of (simple) molecules with weak intermolecular forces (between them), which need little energy to overcome (1)\n• Diamond is giant covalent, so many strong covalent bonds must be broken (which need a lot of energy) (1)\nDO NOT ACCEPT covalent bonds in C₆₀ break (for first mark)` },
      { q: `This question is about metals.\n\nTungsten is a metal used to make the filaments in some light bulbs. The filament gets very hot when the bulb is switched on. Tungsten has a melting point of 3422 °C.\n\nExplain why tungsten has a high melting point.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Strong (electrostatic) attraction between positive (metal) ions and delocalised electrons / strong metallic bonds (1)\n• (Which need) a lot of energy to overcome (1)\nDO NOT ACCEPT intermolecular forces / covalent bonds / ionic bonds (0 marks)` },
      { q: `This question is about nanoparticles.\n\nA student uses two cubes to model particles of silver (data written for this practice question).\n\nCube A has sides of 20 nm.\nCube B has sides of 2 nm.\n\nCalculate how many times larger the surface area to volume ratio of cube B is than that of cube A.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Cube A: surface area = 6 × 20² = 2400 nm² and volume = 20³ = 8000 nm³, so ratio = 0.3 (nm⁻¹) (1)\n• Cube B: surface area = 6 × 2² = 24 nm² and volume = 2³ = 8 nm³, so ratio = 3 (nm⁻¹) (1)\n• 3 ÷ 0.3 = 10 (times) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 3 marks\nALLOW ratios given as 3 : 10 and 3 : 1` },
      { q: `This question is about ionic compounds.\n\nTable 3 shows the melting points of two ionic compounds.\n\nCompound | Ions present | Melting point in °C\nSodium chloride | Na⁺ and Cl⁻ | 801\nMagnesium oxide | Mg²⁺ and O²⁻ | 2852\n\nExplain why magnesium oxide has a higher melting point than sodium chloride.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Both have giant (ionic) lattices with strong electrostatic forces of attraction between oppositely charged ions (1)\n• Magnesium oxide has ions with a greater charge / 2+ and 2− ions (compared with 1+ and 1−) (1)\n• (So) the attraction between ions is stronger so more energy is needed to overcome the forces (1)\nDO NOT ACCEPT intermolecular forces / covalent bonds / molecules (max 1)` },
      { q: `This question is about magnesium oxide.\n\nMagnesium oxide is an ionic compound. The electronic structure of magnesium is 2,8,2. The electronic structure of oxygen is 2,6.\n\nDraw dot and cross diagrams to show the ions in magnesium oxide.\n\nShow the outer shell electrons only. Give the charge on each ion.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• Magnesium ion shown with no electrons in the outer shell / with eight electrons in the second shell (1)\n• Oxide ion shown with eight electrons in the outer shell: six of one symbol and two of the other (1)\n• Charges: 2+ on magnesium ion and 2− on oxide ion (1)\nALLOW brackets omitted\nALLOW Mg²⁺ and O²⁻ written as symbols\nDO NOT ACCEPT shared electrons / overlapping shells (max 1)` },
      { q: `This question is about carbon.\n\nGraphite is used in pencils. When a pencil is moved across paper, layers of graphite rub off onto the paper. Diamond is used in cutting tools.\n\nExplain why graphite is soft but diamond is hard.\n\nAnswer in terms of structure and bonding.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Graphite has layers (of hexagonal rings) with each carbon atom bonded to three others (1)\n• There are no covalent bonds between the layers / only weak forces between the layers, so the layers can slide over each other (1)\n• In diamond each carbon atom forms four (strong) covalent bonds in a rigid giant structure (so atoms cannot slide) (1)\nDO NOT ACCEPT weak covalent bonds between layers\nDO NOT ACCEPT ionic / metallic bonds (max 2)` },
      { q: `This question is about identifying substances from their properties.\n\nTable 4 shows information about four substances, E, F, G and H (data written for this practice question).\n\nSubstance | Melting point in °C | Conducts electricity when solid? | Conducts electricity when molten?\nE | 1083 | yes | yes\nF | −114 | no | no\nG | 1610 | no | no\nH | 770 | no | yes\n\nWhich substance has:\n• a giant covalent structure\n• simple molecules\n• a giant ionic structure?\n\nGive the letter for each.`, marks: 3, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• Giant covalent: G (1)\n• Simple molecules: F (1)\n• Giant ionic: H (1)\nIf a letter is used more than once, mark each incorrect use as wrong` },
      { q: `This question is about alloys.\n\nAircraft bodies are made from an alloy of aluminium called duralumin. Duralumin contains aluminium with small amounts of copper and magnesium. Pure aluminium is too soft to use.\n\nExplain why duralumin is harder than pure aluminium.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• In pure aluminium the atoms are in layers which can slide over each other (1)\n• In duralumin there are atoms of different sizes / copper and magnesium atoms are a different size from aluminium atoms (1)\n• (So) the layers are distorted, making it more difficult for the layers to slide (over each other) (1)\nALLOW ions for atoms throughout\nDO NOT ACCEPT molecules (max 2)` },
      { q: `This question is about nitrogen.\n\nAir is about 78% nitrogen. Nitrogen molecules (N₂) contain a triple covalent bond, which is one of the strongest covalent bonds known. Nitrogen boils at −196 °C.\n\nExplain why nitrogen is a gas at room temperature even though the bond in an N₂ molecule is very strong.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Nitrogen is made of small molecules (1)\n• The intermolecular forces (between N₂ molecules) are weak (1)\n• (When nitrogen boils) only the intermolecular forces are overcome, not the covalent bonds / little energy is needed to overcome intermolecular forces (1)\nDO NOT ACCEPT the triple bond breaks when nitrogen boils (max 2)` },
      { q: `This question is about graphene.\n\nGraphene is a single layer of graphite. Scientists are developing graphene for use in flexible electronic screens.\n\nDescribe the structure and bonding of graphene.\n\nExplain why graphene conducts electricity and is very strong.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• A single layer of carbon atoms (one atom thick) arranged in hexagonal rings (1)\n• Each carbon atom is covalently bonded to three other carbon atoms (1)\n• Each carbon atom has one delocalised electron, which can move through the layer (so graphene conducts electricity) (1)\n• Strong covalent bonds (between carbon atoms) make graphene very strong (1)\nDO NOT ACCEPT ions / intermolecular forces (max 3)` },
      { q: `This question is about silver nanoparticles.\n\nSome socks contain nanoparticles of silver. Silver kills bacteria that cause bad smells. A manufacturer models each silver nanoparticle as a cube with sides of 40 nm.\n\nCalculate the surface area to volume ratio of the cube.\n\nExplain why the manufacturer uses nanoparticles of silver rather than larger particles of silver.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Calculation:\n• Surface area = 6 × 40 × 40 = 9600 (nm²) and volume = 40 × 40 × 40 = 64 000 (nm³) (1)\n• Ratio = 9600 ÷ 64 000 = 0.15 (nm⁻¹) (1)\nALLOW 3 : 20\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks\nExplanation:\n• Nanoparticles have a (much) higher surface area to volume ratio (1)\n• So smaller quantities / less silver is needed (to be effective / to kill bacteria) (1)\nALLOW more silver atoms exposed at the surface / more silver in contact with bacteria for the third mark` },
      { q: `This question is about sodium chloride.\n\nSodium reacts with chlorine to produce sodium chloride.\n\n2Na(s) + Cl₂(g) → 2NaCl(s)\n\nThe electronic structure of sodium is 2,8,1. The electronic structure of chlorine is 2,8,7.\n\nDescribe how sodium atoms and chlorine atoms form sodium chloride.\n\nAnswer in terms of electrons.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (Each) sodium atom loses one electron (1)\n• (Each) chlorine atom gains one electron (1)\n• Forming Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions / ions with the stable electronic structure of a noble gas / 2,8 and 2,8,8 (1)\n• The oppositely charged ions are held together by strong electrostatic forces of attraction (in a giant lattice) (1)\nDO NOT ACCEPT sharing electrons / covalent bond (max 2)\nIGNORE chloride atom` },
      { q: `This question is about oxides.\n\nTable 5 shows information about carbon dioxide and silicon dioxide.\n\nOxide | Formula | Temperature at which the solid changes state in °C\nCarbon dioxide | CO₂ | −78\nSilicon dioxide | SiO₂ | 1710\n\nCarbon and silicon are both in Group 4 of the periodic table.\n\nExplain why the solid of silicon dioxide changes state at a much higher temperature than solid carbon dioxide.\n\nAnswer in terms of structure and bonding.`, marks: 5, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Carbon dioxide:\n• Is made of small molecules (1)\n• With weak intermolecular forces (between molecules) (1)\n• Which need little energy to overcome (1)\nSilicon dioxide:\n• Has a giant covalent structure / is a lattice of atoms joined by covalent bonds (1)\n• (Many) strong covalent bonds must be broken, which needs a lot of energy (1)\nDO NOT ACCEPT covalent bonds break in carbon dioxide (max 4)\nDO NOT ACCEPT silicon dioxide is ionic (max 3)` },
      { q: `This question is about two forms of carbon.\n\nDiamond and graphite are both made of carbon atoms only.\n\nTable 6 shows some properties of diamond and graphite.\n\nProperty | Diamond | Graphite\nHardness | very hard | soft and slippery\nMelting point | very high | very high\nConducts electricity? | no | yes\n\nCompare the structure and bonding of diamond and graphite.\n\nUse your answer to explain the properties in Table 6.`, marks: 6, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed and coherent comparison of structure and bonding, linked logically to explain all three properties.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A comparison of structure and/or bonding with some properties explained. The logic may be inconsistent at times.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements about structure, bonding or properties, with little linking.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\nStructure and bonding\n• Both are giant covalent structures / macromolecules\n• In diamond each carbon atom forms four covalent bonds\n• In graphite each carbon atom forms three covalent bonds\n• Graphite has layers of hexagonal rings\n• There are no covalent bonds between the layers in graphite / only weak forces between layers\n• Graphite has one delocalised electron per carbon atom; diamond has no delocalised electrons\nProperties\n• Both have very high melting points because many strong covalent bonds must be broken, needing a lot of energy\n• Diamond is hard because it is a rigid structure with each atom held by four strong bonds\n• Graphite is soft / slippery because the layers can slide over each other\n• Graphite conducts because delocalised electrons can move through the structure / between layers\n• Diamond does not conduct because all outer electrons are used in bonds / there are no delocalised electrons\nDo not credit intermolecular forces broken on melting, or ions / metallic bonding` },
      { q: `This question is about sunscreens.\n\nSunscreens protect skin from ultraviolet (UV) radiation. Table 7 shows information about two sunscreens, P and Q, that both contain titanium dioxide (data written for this practice question).\n\nProperty | Sunscreen P | Sunscreen Q\nDiameter of titanium dioxide particles | 20 nm | 500 nm\nPercentage of UV blocked | 97 | 92\nAppearance on skin | clear | white layer\nMass of titanium dioxide needed per 100 g of sunscreen in g | 5 | 15\nCost per 100 g in £ | 9.50 | 6.00\n\nEvaluate the use of sunscreen P compared with sunscreen Q.\n\nUse Table 7 and your own knowledge.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed and coherent evaluation that considers a range of advantages and disadvantages, uses data from the table, applies knowledge of nanoparticles, and reaches a justified conclusion.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): An attempt to describe some advantages and/or disadvantages, using some data or knowledge. A conclusion may be given but may not be justified.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements about advantages or disadvantages, with little use of data. No conclusion or a conclusion with no reason.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• P uses nanoparticles (1–100 nm); Q uses fine / larger particles\n• P blocks more UV (97% vs 92%), so gives better protection / lower risk of sunburn and skin cancer\n• P is clear on the skin, so people may be more willing to use it\n• P needs less titanium dioxide (5 g vs 15 g per 100 g) because nanoparticles have a much higher surface area to volume ratio\n• P costs more (£9.50 vs £6.00 per 100 g)\n• Nanoparticles may be absorbed through the skin / into cells / the bloodstream\n• The long-term health effects of nanoparticles are not yet known\n• Nanoparticles washed off may harm the environment / aquatic life\n• The effect of the larger particles in Q is better understood\nConclusion: e.g. P is better because it blocks more UV and is clear, but there are unknown risks; or Q is safer / cheaper but gives less protection. Any justified conclusion` }
    ]
  },
  '4.3': {
    name: 'Quantitative Chemistry',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about magnesium oxide.\n\nA student burned 2.4 g of magnesium ribbon. The magnesium reacted with 1.6 g of oxygen from the air. Magnesium oxide was the only product.\n\nWhat mass of magnesium oxide was produced?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `• 4.0 g (1)\nAllow: 4 g` },
      { q: `This question is about carbon dioxide.\n\nCarbon dioxide is dissolved in water to make fizzy drinks. The formula of carbon dioxide is CO₂.\n\nRelative atomic masses (Aᵣ): C = 12   O = 16\n\nCalculate the relative formula mass (Mᵣ) of carbon dioxide.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 44 (1)\nIgnore: units` },
      { q: `A student made a solution by dissolving 5.0 g of copper sulfate in water. The total volume of the solution was 0.50 dm³.\n\nWhich unit could the student use for the concentration of this solution?\n\nTick (✓) one box.\n\n☐ cm³\n☐ g\n☐ g/dm³\n☐ g/mol`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• g/dm³ only ticked (1)\nIf more than one box is ticked, 0 marks.` },
      { q: `A student measured the volume of gas produced in a reaction four times. The student calculated the mean and then used the range of the results.\n\nComplete the sentence. Choose the answer from the box.\n\naccuracy        resolution        uncertainty\n\nThe range of a set of measurements about the mean is used to estimate the ______.`, marks: 1, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• uncertainty (1)` },
      { q: `This question is about copper.\n\nA student has a sample of 1 mole of copper atoms.\n\nHow many atoms are in 1 mole of copper?\n\nTick (✓) one box.\n\n☐ 6.02 × 10⁻²³\n☐ 63.5\n☐ 6.02 × 10²³\n☐ 63.5 × 10²³`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• 6.02 × 10²³ only ticked (1)\nIf more than one box is ticked, 0 marks.` },
      { q: `Hydrogen gas is produced when zinc reacts with dilute sulfuric acid.\n\nWhat is the volume of 1 mole of hydrogen gas at room temperature and pressure (RTP)?\n\nGive the unit.`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `• 24 dm³ (1)\nAllow: 24 000 cm³ / 24 litres\nUnit required for the mark` },
      { q: `This question is about fuels.\n\nPropane (C₃H₈) is used as a fuel in camping stoves. Propane burns completely in oxygen to produce carbon dioxide and water.\n\nBalance the equation for the reaction.\n\nC₃H₈ + ___ O₂ ⟶ ___ CO₂ + ___ H₂O`, marks: 2, command: 'Balance', markScheme: `• 3CO₂ and 4H₂O (1)\n• 5O₂ (1)\nC₃H₈ + 5O₂ ⟶ 3CO₂ + 4H₂O\nAllow: correct multiples for 1 mark only` },
      { q: `A student heated a piece of magnesium ribbon in a crucible with a lid. The student lifted the lid from time to time to let air in.\n\nThe student weighed the crucible and its contents before and after heating. The mass of the crucible and contents had increased.\n\nExplain why the mass increased.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• magnesium reacts with oxygen (from the air) (1)\n• (so) the mass of oxygen / the oxygen atoms is added to the product / magnesium oxide is heavier than the magnesium (1)\nAllow: oxygen is a reactant that is a gas, so its mass was not included in the starting mass\nDo not accept: magnesium gains mass from the flame / heat` },
      { q: `This question is about fertilisers.\n\nAmmonium sulfate, (NH₄)₂SO₄, is used as a fertiliser.\n\nRelative atomic masses (Aᵣ): H = 1   N = 14   O = 16   S = 32\n\nCalculate the relative formula mass (Mᵣ) of ammonium sulfate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (2 × 14) + (8 × 1) + 32 + (4 × 16) (1)\n• = 132 (1)\nAllow: 2 × 18 + 32 + 64 for first mark\nAn answer of 132 scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `Ammonium nitrate, NH₄NO₃, is another fertiliser. Farmers compare fertilisers by the percentage by mass of nitrogen they contain.\n\nRelative formula mass (Mᵣ) of NH₄NO₃ = 80\nRelative atomic mass (Aᵣ): N = 14\n\nCalculate the percentage by mass of nitrogen in ammonium nitrate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (2 × 14) / 80 × 100 (1)\n• = 35 (%) (1)\nAn answer of 35 (%) scores 2 marks\nAn answer of 17.5 (%) (only one N counted) scores 1 mark` },
      { q: `A student made a solution of sodium hydroxide.\n\nThe student dissolved 12.5 g of sodium hydroxide in water and made the solution up to 250 cm³.\n\nCalculate the concentration of the sodium hydroxide solution in g/dm³.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 250 cm³ = 0.250 dm³ (1)\n• 12.5 / 0.250 = 50 (g/dm³) (1)\nAn answer of 50 (g/dm³) scores 2 marks\nAn answer of 0.05 scores 1 mark (no conversion of volume)` },
      { q: `A sports drink contains glucose at a concentration of 40 g/dm³.\n\nA bottle contains 200 cm³ of the drink.\n\nCalculate the mass of glucose in the bottle.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 200 cm³ = 0.200 dm³ (1)\n• 40 × 0.200 = 8.0 (g) (1)\nAn answer of 8 (g) scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student made a solution by dissolving 10 g of potassium chloride in 100 cm³ of water.\n\nThe student then added another 100 cm³ of water to the solution and stirred it.\n\nExplain what happens to the concentration of the solution when the extra water is added.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the concentration decreases / halves (1)\n• (because) the mass of solute stays the same but the volume (of solution) increases (1)\nAllow: same mass of potassium chloride in a larger volume\nAllow: concentration falls from 100 g/dm³ to 50 g/dm³ for 2 marks` },
      { q: `This question is about carbon dioxide. (Higher)\n\nA cylinder of carbon dioxide used in a restaurant drinks machine releases 11 g of carbon dioxide.\n\nRelative formula mass (Mᵣ) of CO₂ = 44\n\nCalculate the number of moles of carbon dioxide released.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• moles = mass / Mᵣ = 11 / 44 (1)\n• = 0.25 (mol) (1)\nAn answer of 0.25 (mol) scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `A beaker contains 9.0 g of water, H₂O.\n\nRelative formula mass (Mᵣ) of H₂O = 18\nThe Avogadro constant = 6.02 × 10²³ per mole\n\nCalculate the number of water molecules in the beaker.\n\nGive your answer in standard form.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 9.0 / 18 = 0.50 (mol) (1)\n• 0.50 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 3.01 × 10²³ (1)\nAn answer of 3.01 × 10²³ scores 2 marks\nAllow: 3.0 × 10²³\nAllow ecf from incorrect number of moles for the second mark` },
      { q: `A student investigated the reaction between marble chips and hydrochloric acid. The student collected the carbon dioxide produced in a gas syringe for 2 minutes and repeated the experiment four times.\n\nTable 1 shows the results (data written for this practice question).\n\nTable 1\nRun | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4\nVolume of gas (cm³) | 46 | 50 | 48 | 44\n\nCalculate the mean volume of gas and the uncertainty in the mean.\n\nMean = ______ cm³ ± ______ cm³`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• mean = 47 (cm³) (1)\n• uncertainty = ± 3 (cm³) (1)\nUncertainty is half the range: (50 − 44) / 2 = 3\nAllow ecf for uncertainty from an incorrect mean only if half the range is used correctly` },
      { q: `This question is about magnesium and hydrochloric acid. (Higher)\n\nA student added a 5 cm piece of magnesium ribbon to 50 cm³ of dilute hydrochloric acid. The magnesium reacted completely and some acid was left over.\n\nThe student then repeated the experiment with the same piece of magnesium but 100 cm³ of the same acid.\n\nExplain why the volume of hydrogen produced was the same in both experiments.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• magnesium is the limiting reactant / the acid is in excess (1)\n• (so) the amount of product depends on the amount of magnesium, which was the same / all the magnesium was used up in both experiments (1)\nAllow: adding more acid does not change the amount of magnesium that can react` },
      { q: `This question is about making copper sulfate crystals. (Chemistry only)\n\nA student calculated that the maximum mass of copper sulfate crystals that could be made in an experiment was 5.0 g. The student actually obtained 3.8 g of crystals.\n\nCalculate the percentage yield.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (3.8 / 5.0) × 100 (1)\n• = 76 (%) (1)\nAn answer of 76 (%) scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `In the experiment to make copper sulfate crystals, the student obtained less than the maximum theoretical mass of product. (Chemistry only)\n\nGive two reasons why the yield of a product is less than 100%.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• the reaction may not go to completion / the reaction is reversible (1)\n• some of the product may be lost when it is separated from the reaction mixture / during filtering / when transferring between containers (1)\n• some of the reactants may react in ways different to the expected reaction / side reactions (1)\nIgnore: human error / spillage unqualified\nAllow: crystals left in the solution / not all the solution crystallised` },
      { q: `This question is about making hydrogen. (Chemistry only)\n\nHydrogen is made in industry by reacting methane with steam.\n\nCH₄ + H₂O ⟶ CO + 3H₂\n\nRelative formula masses (Mᵣ): CH₄ = 16   H₂O = 18   CO = 28   H₂ = 2\n\nCalculate the percentage atom economy for making hydrogen by this reaction.\n\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (3 × 2) / (16 + 18) × 100 (1)\n• = 17.6 (%) (1)\nAllow: 6 / 34 × 100\nAn answer of 17.6 (%) scores 2 marks\nAn answer of 5.88 (%) (only one H₂ counted) scores 1 mark` },
      { q: `A chemical company is choosing between two reactions to make the same product. Reaction A has an atom economy of 100%. Reaction B has an atom economy of 48%. (Chemistry only)\n\nGive two reasons why the company might choose the reaction with the higher atom economy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• less waste (product) is produced (1)\n• more of the starting materials end up as the useful / desired product (1)\n• better use of (finite) raw materials / more sustainable (1)\n• cheaper / more profitable because no money spent disposing of waste or buying extra reactants (1)\nIgnore: faster reaction / higher yield` },
      { q: `This question is about hydrochloric acid. (Chemistry only, Higher)\n\nA student had a solution of hydrochloric acid with a concentration of 0.200 mol/dm³.\n\nRelative formula mass (Mᵣ) of HCl = 36.5\n\nCalculate the concentration of the hydrochloric acid in g/dm³.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 0.200 × 36.5 (1)\n• = 7.3 (g/dm³) (1)\nAn answer of 7.3 (g/dm³) scores 2 marks\nAllow: 7.30` },
      { q: `A student carried out a titration to find the volume of sulfuric acid needed to neutralise 25.0 cm³ of potassium hydroxide solution. (Chemistry only)\n\nTable 2 shows the results (data written for this practice question).\n\nTable 2\nTitration | Rough | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4\nVolume of acid (cm³) | 23.40 | 22.85 | 22.95 | 23.30 | 22.90\n\nConcordant results are within 0.10 cm³ of each other.\n\nCalculate the mean volume of sulfuric acid added. Use only the concordant results.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• uses titrations 1, 2 and 4 only: (22.85 + 22.95 + 22.90) / 3 (1)\n• = 22.90 (cm³) (1)\nAllow: 22.9 (cm³)\nAn answer of 22.90 scores 2 marks\nAn answer using all four numbered titrations (23.00) scores 1 mark` },
      { q: `This question is about oxygen. (Chemistry only, Higher)\n\nA hospital uses cylinders of oxygen gas. A small sample contains 4.0 g of oxygen, O₂.\n\nRelative formula mass (Mᵣ) of O₂ = 32\nThe volume of one mole of any gas at room temperature and pressure is 24 dm³.\n\nCalculate the volume of the oxygen sample at room temperature and pressure.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 4.0 / 32 = 0.125 (mol) (1)\n• 0.125 × 24 = 3.0 (dm³) (1)\nAn answer of 3 (dm³) scores 2 marks\nAllow: 3000 cm³ if unit given` },
      { q: `Methane burns in oxygen. (Chemistry only, Higher)\n\nCH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) ⟶ CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l)\n\nA student burned 50 cm³ of methane in excess oxygen. All volumes were measured at room temperature and pressure.\n\nDetermine the volume of oxygen that reacted and the volume of carbon dioxide produced.`, marks: 2, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `• oxygen: 100 cm³ (1)\n• carbon dioxide: 50 cm³ (1)\nEqual amounts in moles of gases occupy the same volume at the same temperature and pressure, so volumes are in the ratio 1 : 2 : 1` },
      { q: `A student poured 50 cm³ of lead nitrate solution and 50 cm³ of potassium iodide solution into a beaker on a balance. A yellow precipitate of lead iodide formed. The reading on the balance did not change.\n\nExplain why the mass did not change during this reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• no atoms are lost or made / atoms are rearranged (1)\n• no gas is produced / no substance escapes from the beaker / the precipitate stays in the beaker (1)\nAllow: mass is conserved / total mass of products equals total mass of reactants for 1 mark\nDo not accept: the precipitate has no mass` },
      { q: `A student investigated the mass of carbon dioxide lost when marble chips react with acid in an open flask. The total mass lost in each experiment was about 0.5 g.\n\nThe student could use either a balance with a resolution of 0.1 g or a balance with a resolution of 0.01 g.\n\nExplain which balance the student should use.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the balance with a resolution of 0.01 g (1)\n• (because) it measures the small mass change more precisely / gives a smaller (percentage) uncertainty in the mass change (1)\nAllow: it can detect smaller changes in mass\nNo mark for choice of balance alone without a reason` },
      { q: `This question is about limestone. (Higher)\n\nLimestone is mainly calcium carbonate. When calcium carbonate is heated strongly it decomposes to form calcium oxide.\n\nCaCO₃ ⟶ CaO + CO₂\n\nRelative formula masses (Mᵣ): CaCO₃ = 100   CaO = 56\n\nCalculate the mass of calcium oxide produced when 25 g of calcium carbonate is completely decomposed.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• moles CaCO₃ = 25 / 100 = 0.25 (mol) (1)\n• moles CaO = 0.25 (mol) (1:1 ratio) (1)\n• mass CaO = 0.25 × 56 = 14 (g) (1)\nAn answer of 14 (g) scores 3 marks\nAllow alternative method: 100 g ⟶ 56 g so 25 g ⟶ 14 g` },
      { q: `A technician made sodium hydroxide solution for a class practical. (Higher)\n\nThe technician dissolved 4.0 g of sodium hydroxide, NaOH, in water and made the solution up to 250 cm³.\n\nRelative formula mass (Mᵣ) of NaOH = 40\n\nCalculate the concentration of the solution in mol/dm³.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• moles = 4.0 / 40 = 0.10 (mol) (1)\n• volume = 250 / 1000 = 0.250 (dm³) (1)\n• concentration = 0.10 / 0.250 = 0.40 (mol/dm³) (1)\nAn answer of 0.4 (mol/dm³) scores 3 marks\nAlternative: 4.0 / 0.250 = 16 g/dm³, then 16 / 40 = 0.40 mol/dm³` },
      { q: `This question is about extracting iron. (Chemistry only)\n\nIron is extracted from iron(III) oxide in a blast furnace.\n\nFe₂O₃ + 3CO ⟶ 2Fe + 3CO₂\n\nRelative atomic mass (Aᵣ): Fe = 56\nRelative formula masses (Mᵣ): Fe₂O₃ = 160   CO = 28   CO₂ = 44\n\nCalculate the percentage atom economy for producing iron in this reaction.\n\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• total Mᵣ of reactants = 160 + (3 × 28) = 244 (1)\n• (2 × 56) / 244 × 100 (1)\n• = 45.9 (%) (1)\nAllow: total Mᵣ of products = 112 + 132 = 244 for the first mark\nAn answer of 45.9 (%) scores 3 marks\nAllow 1 mark for 45.90... or 46 (%) not given to 3 significant figures, provided working is correct` },
      { q: `A student reacted 5.0 g of calcium carbonate with excess hydrochloric acid. (Chemistry only, Higher)\n\nCaCO₃(s) + 2HCl(aq) ⟶ CaCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g)\n\nRelative formula mass (Mᵣ) of CaCO₃ = 100\nThe volume of one mole of any gas at room temperature and pressure is 24 dm³.\n\nCalculate the volume of carbon dioxide produced at room temperature and pressure.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• moles CaCO₃ = 5.0 / 100 = 0.050 (mol) (1)\n• moles CO₂ = 0.050 (mol) (1)\n• volume = 0.050 × 24 = 1.2 (dm³) (1)\nAn answer of 1.2 dm³ or 1200 cm³ scores 3 marks` },
      { q: `A student heated 2.47 g of copper carbonate, CuCO₃, in an open boiling tube. The copper carbonate decomposed to copper oxide and carbon dioxide.\n\nThe student weighed the tube and contents, heated it again, and repeated until the mass stayed the same. Table 3 shows the mass of solid after each heating (data written for this practice question).\n\nTable 3\nHeating | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4\nMass of solid (g) | 1.72 | 1.60 | 1.59 | 1.59\n\nExplain why the mass of solid decreased, and why the student kept heating until the mass stayed the same.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• carbon dioxide (gas) is produced (1)\n• which escapes from the (open) tube so its mass is not measured (1)\n• heating to constant mass shows that the reaction is complete / all the copper carbonate has decomposed (1)\nAllow: the mass stops changing when no more carbon dioxide is given off\nDo not accept: mass is destroyed / atoms are lost` },
      { q: `This question is about titration. (Chemistry only, Higher)\n\nA student found that 20.00 cm³ of hydrochloric acid neutralised 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution of concentration 0.100 mol/dm³.\n\nNaOH + HCl ⟶ NaCl + H₂O\n\nCalculate the concentration of the hydrochloric acid in mol/dm³.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• moles NaOH = 0.100 × 25.0 / 1000 = 0.00250 (mol) (1)\n• moles HCl = 0.00250 (mol) (1:1 ratio) (1)\n• concentration HCl = 0.00250 / (20.00 / 1000) = 0.125 (mol/dm³) (1)\nAn answer of 0.125 (mol/dm³) scores 3 marks\nAllow ecf at each step` },
      { q: `A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 8.8 g. (Higher)\n\nRelative formula mass (Mᵣ) of CO₂ = 44\nThe Avogadro constant = 6.02 × 10²³ per mole\n\nCalculate the number of oxygen atoms in the sample.\n\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• moles CO₂ = 8.8 / 44 = 0.20 (mol) (1)\n• molecules = 0.20 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 1.204 × 10²³ (1)\n• oxygen atoms = 2 × 1.204 × 10²³ = 2.41 × 10²³ (1)\nAn answer of 2.41 × 10²³ scores 3 marks\nAn answer of 1.20 × 10²³ scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `This question is about magnesium oxide. (Higher)\n\nA student heated 0.36 g of magnesium in a crucible until it had all reacted with oxygen. The mass of magnesium oxide produced was 0.60 g.\n\nRelative atomic mass (Aᵣ): Mg = 24\nRelative formula masses (Mᵣ): O₂ = 32   MgO = 40\n\nUse the masses to balance the equation for the reaction. You must show your working.\n\n___ Mg + ___ O₂ ⟶ ___ MgO`, marks: 4, command: 'Balance', markScheme: `• mass of oxygen = 0.60 − 0.36 = 0.24 (g) (1)\n• moles: Mg = 0.36 / 24 = 0.015 ; O₂ = 0.24 / 32 = 0.0075 ; MgO = 0.60 / 40 = 0.015 (1)\n• divides by smallest (0.0075) to give ratio 2 : 1 : 2 (1)\n• 2Mg + O₂ ⟶ 2MgO (1)\nA correctly balanced equation without working scores 1 mark only` },
      { q: `A student added 0.60 g of magnesium ribbon to 50.0 cm³ of hydrochloric acid of concentration 0.80 mol/dm³. (Chemistry only, Higher)\n\nMg(s) + 2HCl(aq) ⟶ MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g)\n\nRelative atomic mass (Aᵣ): Mg = 24\nThe volume of one mole of any gas at room temperature and pressure is 24 dm³.\n\nDetermine which reactant is the limiting reactant. Calculate the maximum volume of hydrogen gas produced at room temperature and pressure.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• moles Mg = 0.60 / 24 = 0.025 (mol) and moles HCl = 0.80 × 50.0 / 1000 = 0.040 (mol) (1)\n• 0.025 mol Mg needs 0.050 mol HCl, so hydrochloric acid is the limiting reactant (1)\n• moles H₂ = 0.040 / 2 = 0.020 (mol) (1)\n• volume = 0.020 × 24 = 0.48 (dm³) (1)\nAllow: 480 cm³\nAn answer of 0.60 dm³ (Mg used as limiting) scores 2 marks if moles were correctly calculated` },
      { q: `A student made magnesium chloride by reacting magnesium oxide with excess hydrochloric acid. (Chemistry only, Higher)\n\nMgO + 2HCl ⟶ MgCl₂ + H₂O\n\nThe student used 2.0 g of magnesium oxide and obtained 3.8 g of magnesium chloride.\n\nRelative formula masses (Mᵣ): MgO = 40   MgCl₂ = 95\n\nCalculate the percentage yield of magnesium chloride.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• moles MgO = 2.0 / 40 = 0.050 (mol) (1)\n• moles MgCl₂ = 0.050 (mol) (1)\n• maximum (theoretical) mass MgCl₂ = 0.050 × 95 = 4.75 (g) (1)\n• percentage yield = 3.8 / 4.75 × 100 = 80 (%) (1)\nAn answer of 80 (%) scores 4 marks\nAllow ecf at each step` },
      { q: `This question is about the concentration of a drain cleaner. (Chemistry only, Higher)\n\nA drain cleaner contains sodium hydroxide solution. A student titrated 25.0 cm³ of the drain cleaner solution with sulfuric acid of concentration 0.150 mol/dm³. The mean titre was 21.60 cm³.\n\nH₂SO₄ + 2NaOH ⟶ Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O\n\nRelative formula mass (Mᵣ) of NaOH = 40\n\nCalculate the concentration of sodium hydroxide in the drain cleaner solution in g/dm³.\n\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• moles H₂SO₄ = 0.150 × 21.60 / 1000 = 0.00324 (mol) (1)\n• moles NaOH = 2 × 0.00324 = 0.00648 (mol) (1)\n• concentration NaOH = 0.00648 / (25.0 / 1000) = 0.2592 (mol/dm³) (1)\n• concentration in g/dm³ = 0.2592 × 40 = 10.368 (1)\n• = 10.4 (g/dm³) to 3 significant figures (1)\nAn answer of 10.4 (g/dm³) scores 5 marks\nAllow ecf at each step\nAn answer of 5.18 (g/dm³) (1:2 ratio not used) scores 3 marks if given to 3 significant figures` },
      { q: `A student is given dilute hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide solution of concentration 0.100 mol/dm³. (Chemistry only)\n\nThe student also has a burette, a pipette, a conical flask, a white tile and phenolphthalein indicator.\n\nDescribe how the student could use titration to find the volume of hydrochloric acid that exactly neutralises 25.0 cm³ of the sodium hydroxide solution.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed and coherent plan covering all key steps. The steps are in a logical sequence that could be followed by another person to obtain valid results.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): The key steps are identified and the plan is mostly logically sequenced. It may not be possible to follow it to obtain valid results.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements are made. The response may lack a logical structure and would not lead to valid results.\n\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• use a pipette (and pipette filler) to measure 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution\n• transfer the sodium hydroxide solution to a conical flask\n• add a few drops of phenolphthalein indicator\n• place the flask on a white tile\n• fill the burette with hydrochloric acid (using a funnel; remove the funnel)\n• record the initial burette reading (read at eye level, bottom of the meniscus)\n• add acid from the burette to the flask while swirling\n• add the acid drop by drop near the end point\n• stop when the indicator changes from pink to colourless\n• record the final burette reading and calculate the titre\n• do a rough titration first\n• repeat until concordant results (within 0.10 cm³) are obtained\n• calculate the mean of the concordant titres` },
      { q: `Ethanol can be produced by two different processes. (Chemistry only)\n\nProcess 1: fermentation of sugar from sugar cane\nC₆H₁₂O₆ ⟶ 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂\n\nProcess 2: hydration of ethene from crude oil\nC₂H₄ + H₂O ⟶ C₂H₅OH\n\nTable 4 gives information about the two processes (data written for this practice question).\n\nTable 4\nFeature | Process 1 | Process 2\nAtom economy (%) | 51.1 | 100\nPercentage yield (%) | 15 | 95\nTemperature (°C) | 35 | 300\nPressure | atmospheric | 70 times atmospheric\nType of process | batch | continuous\nPurity of ethanol produced | about 15% in water | almost pure\n\nEvaluate the two processes for producing ethanol.\n\nUse Table 4 and your own knowledge.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A judgement, strongly linked and logically supported by a sufficient range of correct reasons, is given.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some logically linked reasons are given. There may also be a simple judgement.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Relevant points are made. They are not logically linked.\n\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\nProcess 1 (fermentation)\n• uses a renewable raw material (sugar cane)\n• low temperature and atmospheric pressure, so less energy needed / cheaper equipment\n• lower atom economy (51.1%): carbon dioxide is a waste product, and it is a greenhouse gas\n• low percentage yield (15%), so less product from the starting materials\n• batch process, so slower / not continuous production\n• ethanol produced is dilute and must be separated by fractional distillation, which needs energy\n• sugar cane uses land that could grow food\nProcess 2 (hydration of ethene)\n• 100% atom economy: no waste product, all atoms end up in the ethanol\n• high percentage yield (95%)\n• continuous process, so large quantities produced quickly\n• almost pure ethanol, so little purification needed\n• uses ethene from crude oil, which is a finite / non-renewable resource\n• high temperature and pressure need a lot of energy / expensive equipment\nJudgement\n• for a Level 3 answer a justified judgement is required, e.g. Process 2 is more efficient (higher atom economy and yield) but Process 1 is more sustainable in the long term because the raw material is renewable` }
    ]
  },
  '4.4': {
    name: 'Chemical Changes',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about the reactivity of metals.\nA teacher added small pieces of three Group 1 metals to separate troughs of water.\nTable 1 shows the observations (data written for this practice question).\nLithium: fizzes steadily, moves slowly on the surface\nPotassium: melts into a ball, moves very quickly, burns with a lilac flame\nSodium: melts into a ball, fizzes rapidly, moves quickly on the surface\n\nWhich metal is the most reactive? Use Table 1.\nTick one box.\nLithium\nPotassium\nSodium`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `Potassium\nif more than one box ticked, do not award the mark` },
      { q: `This question is about metals and their extraction.\nGold is found in the Earth as the metal itself. Most other metals are found as compounds in ores.\n\nGive one reason why gold is found in the Earth as the metal itself.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `gold is (very) unreactive\nallow gold does not react (easily) with other substances / with oxygen\nignore gold is low in the reactivity series unless qualified\ndo not accept gold is unreactive because it is a noble gas` },
      { q: `A student added a small piece of magnesium ribbon to dilute hydrochloric acid in a test tube.\nThe mixture fizzed and the magnesium disappeared.\n\nName the gas produced in this reaction.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `hydrogen\nallow H₂\ndo not accept H` },
      { q: `A titration measures the volumes of an acid and an alkali that neutralise each other.\nA student used a pipette to transfer sodium hydroxide solution into a conical flask and then added a few drops of indicator.\nThe student added dilute hydrochloric acid from a burette until the solution was just neutral.\n\nComplete the sentence.\nChoose the answer from the box: 1 / 7 / 13\n\nThe pH of the solution in the conical flask at the end point was ______.`, marks: 1, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `7` },
      { q: `This question is about the extraction of aluminium.\nAluminium is manufactured by the electrolysis of a molten mixture of aluminium oxide and cryolite.\nAluminium oxide has a melting point of about 2050 °C.\n\nGive one reason why cryolite is mixed with the aluminium oxide.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `to lower the melting point (of the mixture / electrolyte)\nallow so less energy is needed / to reduce energy costs\nallow so the mixture melts at a lower temperature\nignore to make the ions move / as a catalyst` },
      { q: `This question is about neutralisation.\nA student tested four solutions with universal indicator. The student recorded the colour of each solution (data written for this practice question).\nSolution A: purple\nSolution B: green\nSolution C: red\nSolution D: yellow\n\nWhich solution, A, B, C or D, is the most acidic?`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `C` },
      { q: `This question is about reactions of acids.\nZinc reacts with dilute sulfuric acid.\n\nComplete the word equation for the reaction.\nzinc + sulfuric acid → ______ + ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `zinc sulfate (1)\nhydrogen (1)\neither order\ndo not accept zinc sulphide` },
      { q: `This question is about displacement reactions.\nA student put an iron nail into blue copper sulfate solution and left it for 30 minutes.\nIron is more reactive than copper.\n\nGive two observations the student would make.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• brown / pink / orange solid forms (on the nail)\nallow nail becomes coated with copper\n• (blue) colour of solution fades\nallow solution turns (pale) green\n• nail gets smaller / thinner\nignore fizzing / bubbles\nignore temperature change` },
      { q: `This question is about displacement reactions.\nMagnesium reacts with copper sulfate solution. The ionic equation for the reaction is:\nMg(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) → Mg²⁺(aq) + Cu(s)\n\nWhich species is oxidised in this reaction?\nExplain your answer in terms of electrons.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `magnesium / Mg (1)\n(because) it loses (two) electrons (1)\nallow Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻ for the second mark\nsecond mark dependent on first mark` },
      { q: `This question is about the extraction of metals.\nIron is extracted in a blast furnace by heating iron oxide with carbon.\nThe word equation is:\niron oxide + carbon → iron + carbon dioxide\n\nExplain why iron can be extracted from iron oxide by heating with carbon.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `iron is less reactive than carbon (1)\nallow carbon is more reactive than iron\n(so) carbon removes oxygen from iron oxide / displaces iron (1)\nallow iron oxide is reduced (by carbon)\nallow carbon is oxidised` },
      { q: `This question is about the reactivity series.\nPart of the reactivity series, in order of decreasing reactivity, is:\npotassium, sodium, calcium, magnesium, aluminium, carbon, zinc, iron, hydrogen, copper\n\nExplain why aluminium cannot be extracted from aluminium oxide by heating with carbon.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `aluminium is more reactive than carbon (1)\nallow aluminium is above carbon in the reactivity series\n(so) carbon cannot reduce aluminium oxide / remove oxygen from aluminium oxide (1)\nallow carbon cannot displace aluminium\nallow (so) electrolysis must be used for 1 mark if first marking point not given` },
      { q: `Copper can be extracted by heating copper oxide with carbon.\nThe equation for the reaction is:\n___ CuO + C → ___ Cu + CO₂\n\nBalance the equation.\nName the substance that is reduced in this reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Balance', markScheme: `2CuO + C → 2Cu + CO₂ (1)\ncopper oxide / CuO (1)\nallow copper(II) oxide` },
      { q: `This question is about making a salt.\nA student made copper sulfate crystals.\nMethod:\n1. Warm 50 cm³ of dilute sulfuric acid in a beaker.\n2. Add copper oxide powder to the acid, stirring, until some copper oxide remains unreacted.\n3. Filter the mixture.\n4. Heat the filtrate to make crystals.\n\nGive one reason why the student added copper oxide until some remained unreacted in step 2.\nGive one reason for filtering the mixture in step 3.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `step 2: to make sure all the (sulfuric) acid has reacted / is used up (1)\nallow copper oxide is in excess\nallow so no acid remains in the solution\nstep 3: to remove the excess / unreacted copper oxide (1)\nallow to remove the (insoluble) solid\nignore to remove impurities unqualified` },
      { q: `A student made a solution of zinc chloride by reacting zinc carbonate with hydrochloric acid.\nThe student then filtered off the excess zinc carbonate.\n\nDescribe how the student should obtain dry crystals of zinc chloride from the zinc chloride solution.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `heat (the solution) to evaporate some of the water / until crystals start to form (1)\nallow heat using a water bath / electric heater\ndo not accept heat to dryness / evaporate all the water\nleave to cool / crystallise, then filter (off the crystals) and dry (between filter paper / in a warm oven) (1)` },
      { q: `Potassium nitrate is used in fertilisers.\nPotassium nitrate can be made by reacting an acid with an alkali.\n\nName the acid and the alkali used to make potassium nitrate.\nAcid ______\nAlkali ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `acid: nitric acid (1)\nallow HNO₃\nalkali: potassium hydroxide (1)\nallow KOH\nallow potassium carbonate for alkali` },
      { q: `This question is about pH and neutralisation.\nA student added sodium hydroxide solution, 2 cm³ at a time, to 25 cm³ of dilute hydrochloric acid.\nThe student measured the pH of the mixture after each addition using a pH probe.\nThe student continued adding sodium hydroxide solution well past the point of neutralisation.\n\nDescribe how the pH of the mixture changed during the experiment.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `pH starts low / below 7 / about 1 and increases (1)\n(passes through) 7 at neutralisation and ends above 7 / at about 13 (1)\nallow a rapid increase around pH 7\nmax 1 if no reference to pH values or neutral` },
      { q: `This question is about strong and weak acids.\nA student measured the pH of two acid solutions with the same concentration (data written for this practice question).\nHydrochloric acid, 0.10 mol/dm³: pH 1.0\nEthanoic acid, 0.10 mol/dm³: pH 2.9\n\nExplain why the hydrochloric acid has a lower pH than the ethanoic acid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `hydrochloric acid is a strong acid / completely ionised (in aqueous solution) and ethanoic acid is a weak acid / only partially ionised (1)\n(so) hydrochloric acid has a higher concentration of hydrogen ions / H⁺(aq) (1)\nignore dissociated for ionised\ndo not accept hydrochloric acid is more concentrated` },
      { q: `This question is about pH.\nThe pH of a sample of acid rain was 4.\nThe pH of a sample of lemon juice was 2.\n\nHow many times greater is the hydrogen ion concentration in the lemon juice than in the acid rain?\nGive a reason for your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `100 (times) (1)\neach decrease of one pH unit increases the hydrogen ion concentration by a factor of 10 (1)\nallow 10 × 10 = 100\nallow 10² for 100` },
      { q: `This question is about acids.\nA bottle in a school laboratory is labelled 'dilute ethanoic acid'. Ethanoic acid is a weak acid.\n\nExplain the difference between the terms dilute and weak when describing an acid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `dilute means there is a small amount of acid in a given volume (of solution) / low concentration (1)\nweak means the acid is only partially ionised (in aqueous solution) (1)\nallow weak means few of the acid molecules release H⁺ ions\ndo not accept weak means low concentration` },
      { q: `This question is about electrolysis.\nA teacher electrolysed lead bromide.\nThe teacher heated solid lead bromide until it melted and then switched on the power supply.\n\nExplain why solid lead bromide does not conduct electricity but molten lead bromide does.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `in the solid the ions cannot move / are in fixed positions (1)\nwhen molten the ions are free to move (and carry charge / current) (1)\ndo not accept electrons move for either mark\nmax 1 if reference to molecules or atoms moving` },
      { q: `Molten potassium chloride can be electrolysed using inert electrodes.\n\nName the product formed at the positive electrode and the product formed at the negative electrode.\nPositive electrode ______\nNegative electrode ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `positive electrode: chlorine (1)\nallow Cl₂\ndo not accept chloride / Cl\nnegative electrode: potassium (1)\nallow K` },
      { q: `This question is about the electrolysis of aqueous solutions.\nA student electrolysed sodium chloride solution using inert electrodes.\n\nName the product formed at the negative electrode.\nGive one reason why sodium is not formed at the negative electrode.`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `hydrogen (1)\nallow H₂\nsodium is more reactive than hydrogen (1)\nallow hydrogen is less reactive than sodium` },
      { q: `This question is about the electrolysis of sodium sulfate solution.\nOxygen is produced at the positive electrode.\n\nComplete the half equation for the reaction at the positive electrode.\n___ OH⁻ → O₂ + ___ H₂O + ___ e⁻`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `4OH⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻\nall three numbers correct (2)\nany two numbers correct (1)\nallow multiples` },
      { q: `This question is about the extraction of aluminium.\nAluminium is extracted by electrolysis of a molten mixture of aluminium oxide and cryolite at about 950 °C.\nThe positive electrodes are made of carbon.\n\nExplain why the positive electrodes must be replaced regularly.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `oxygen is produced at the positive electrode (1)\n(the oxygen) reacts with the carbon (at the high temperature) to form carbon dioxide (so the electrode wears away / burns away) (1)\nallow carbon + oxygen → carbon dioxide for second mark\nignore carbon monoxide` },
      { q: `A student investigated the electrolysis of copper chloride solution using inert electrodes.\nA gas was produced at the positive electrode.\n\nDescribe a test to identify the gas produced at the positive electrode.\nGive the result of the test.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `(hold) damp / moist (blue) litmus paper (in the gas) (1)\nbleaches / turns white (1)\nallow blue litmus turns red then white\nresult mark dependent on correct test\nignore smell` },
      { q: `This question is about titrations.\nA student carried out a titration to find the volume of dilute sulfuric acid needed to neutralise 25.0 cm³ of potassium hydroxide solution.\nThe student added the acid from a burette.\n\nGive two ways the student could make sure the volume of acid added is accurate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• add the acid dropwise near the end point\n• swirl the flask (while adding the acid)\n• read the burette at eye level\n• read from the bottom of the meniscus\n• use a white tile (under the flask) to see the colour change clearly\n• rinse the burette with the acid (before filling)\n• make sure there are no air bubbles in the burette tip\nignore repeat the titration / calculate a mean` },
      { q: `A student did a titration four times.\nTable 2 shows the results (data written for this practice question).\nTitration 1 (rough): 24.60 cm³\nTitration 2: 23.95 cm³\nTitration 3: 24.25 cm³\nTitration 4: 24.05 cm³\nConcordant results are within 0.10 cm³ of each other.\n\nCalculate the mean titre using only the concordant results.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(23.95 + 24.05) ÷ 2 (1)\n= 24.00 (cm³) (1)\nallow 24.0 (cm³)\nan answer of 24.08 or 24.21 scores 0` },
      { q: `This question is about the reactivity of metals.\nA student added pieces of three metals, X, Y and Z, to solutions of each other's salts.\nTable 3 shows the results (data written for this practice question).\nMetal X in a solution of the salt of Y: reaction\nMetal X in a solution of the salt of Z: reaction\nMetal Y in a solution of the salt of X: no reaction\nMetal Y in a solution of the salt of Z: no reaction\nMetal Z in a solution of the salt of X: no reaction\nMetal Z in a solution of the salt of Y: reaction\n\nPut the metals X, Y and Z in order of reactivity.\nExplain your answer. Use Table 3.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `order (most reactive first): X, Z, Y (1)\nX displaces both Y and Z (so X is most reactive) (1)\nZ displaces Y but not X / Y displaces neither metal (so Y is least reactive) (1)\nallow a more reactive metal displaces a less reactive metal from a solution of its salt for 1 mark if no reference to the data` },
      { q: `This question is about oxidation and reduction.\nZinc reacts with copper sulfate solution.\nThe ionic equation is:\nZn(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) → Zn²⁺(aq) + Cu(s)\n\nExplain why this reaction is both an oxidation and a reduction.\nYou should include half equations in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ (1)\nallow Zn − 2e⁻ → Zn²⁺\nCu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu (1)\nzinc (atoms) lose electrons so are oxidised and copper ions gain electrons so are reduced (1)\nallow oxidation is loss of electrons and reduction is gain of electrons if linked to the correct species` },
      { q: `A student did a titration to find the concentration of a sodium hydroxide solution.\n25.0 cm³ of the sodium hydroxide solution was neutralised by 20.00 cm³ of sulfuric acid of concentration 0.100 mol/dm³.\nThe equation for the reaction is:\nH₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O\n\nCalculate the concentration of the sodium hydroxide solution in mol/dm³.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `moles H₂SO₄ = 0.100 × 20.00 / 1000 = 0.00200 (1)\nmoles NaOH = 0.00200 × 2 = 0.00400 (1)\nconcentration = 0.00400 ÷ (25.0 / 1000) = 0.160 (mol/dm³) (1)\nallow ecf from incorrect number of moles\nan answer of 0.0800 scores 2 (ratio not used)\ncorrect answer with no working scores 3` },
      { q: `This question is about the electrolysis of aqueous solutions.\nA student electrolysed potassium bromide solution using inert electrodes.\n\nPredict the products at the positive electrode and at the negative electrode.\nExplain your prediction for the negative electrode.`, marks: 3, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `positive electrode: bromine (1)\nnegative electrode: hydrogen (1)\n(because) potassium is more reactive than hydrogen (1)\nallow hydrogen ions (from water) are discharged instead of potassium ions\nthird mark dependent on hydrogen at negative electrode` },
      { q: `Aluminium is extracted by the electrolysis of aluminium oxide.\nThe equation for the overall reaction is:\n2Al₂O₃ → 4Al + 3O₂\nRelative atomic masses (Ar): O = 16 Al = 27\n\nCalculate the maximum mass of aluminium that can be produced from 51 kg of aluminium oxide.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Mr Al₂O₃ = 102 (1)\nmoles Al₂O₃ = 51 000 ÷ 102 = 500 and moles Al = 500 × 2 = 1000 (1)\nmass Al = 1000 × 27 = 27 000 g = 27 (kg) (1)\nallow alternative method: 102 kg Al₂O₃ gives 54 kg Al, so 51 kg gives 27 kg\nallow ecf from incorrect Mr\ncorrect answer with no working scores 3` },
      { q: `This question is about making salts.\nA student added zinc carbonate powder, one spatula at a time, to 50 cm³ of dilute hydrochloric acid.\nAt first the mixture fizzed. After several spatulas were added, the fizzing stopped and solid remained in the beaker.\n\nExplain these observations.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `fizzing is caused by carbon dioxide (being produced) (1)\nthe fizzing stops because all the (hydrochloric) acid has been used up / has reacted (1)\nthe solid remaining is (excess / unreacted) zinc carbonate (because there is no acid left to react with it) (1)\nignore hydrogen for first mark only if carbon dioxide also given\ndo not accept zinc chloride is the solid` },
      { q: `This question is about the electrolysis of copper chloride solution.\nA student set up an electrolysis cell with two inert carbon electrodes in copper chloride solution, connected to a 6 V d.c. power supply.\nAfter 10 minutes the negative electrode was coated with a pink-brown solid and bubbles had formed at the positive electrode.\n\nExplain these observations in terms of the ions in the solution.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `copper ions / Cu²⁺ are attracted to the negative electrode (1)\n(where they) gain electrons to form copper (atoms) (1)\nallow Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu\nchloride ions / Cl⁻ are attracted to the positive electrode and lose electrons to form chlorine (gas) (1)\nallow 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻\ndo not accept chlorine ions` },
      { q: `A student did a titration to find the concentration of a potassium hydroxide solution.\n25.0 cm³ of the potassium hydroxide solution was exactly neutralised by 22.40 cm³ of hydrochloric acid of concentration 0.150 mol/dm³.\nThe equation for the reaction is:\nKOH + HCl → KCl + H₂O\nRelative atomic masses (Ar): H = 1 O = 16 K = 39\n\nCalculate the concentration of the potassium hydroxide solution in g/dm³.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `moles HCl = 0.150 × 22.40 / 1000 = 0.00336 (1)\nmoles KOH = 0.00336 and concentration KOH = 0.00336 ÷ 0.0250 = 0.1344 (mol/dm³) (1)\nMr KOH = 56 and 0.1344 × 56 = 7.5264 (g/dm³) (1)\n= 7.53 (g/dm³) to 3 significant figures (1)\nallow ecf at each step\ncorrect answer to 3 significant figures with no working scores 4` },
      { q: `This question is about the extraction of aluminium.\nAluminium is produced by electrolysis of a molten mixture of aluminium oxide and cryolite.\nAluminium ions (Al³⁺) and oxide ions (O²⁻) are present in the molten mixture.\n\nWrite a half equation for the reaction at each electrode.\nExplain which reaction is a reduction.`, marks: 4, command: 'Write', markScheme: `negative electrode: Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al (1)\npositive electrode: 2O²⁻ → O₂ + 4e⁻ (1)\nallow 2O²⁻ − 4e⁻ → O₂\nallow multiples\nthe reaction at the negative electrode is reduction (1)\nbecause aluminium ions gain electrons (1)\nallow reduction is gain of electrons linked to Al³⁺ for the fourth mark` },
      { q: `This question is about the electrolysis of aqueous solutions.\nA student electrolysed two different aqueous solutions using inert electrodes.\n\nPredict the product at each electrode for:\n• copper sulfate solution\n• sodium iodide solution.\nGive one reason for each prediction at the positive electrode.`, marks: 4, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `copper sulfate: copper at the negative electrode and oxygen at the positive electrode (1)\nreason: (sulfate ions are not discharged so) hydroxide ions (from water) are discharged / no halide ions are present (1)\nsodium iodide: hydrogen at the negative electrode and iodine at the positive electrode (1)\nreason: iodide ions / halide ions are present (so the halogen is produced) (1)` },
      { q: `A student did a titration to find the concentration of a sodium hydroxide solution.\nThe student used 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution in each titration and sulfuric acid of concentration 0.0500 mol/dm³ in the burette.\nTable 4 shows the results (data written for this practice question).\nTitration 1 (rough): 23.10 cm³\nTitration 2: 22.30 cm³\nTitration 3: 22.60 cm³\nTitration 4: 22.20 cm³\nConcordant results are within 0.10 cm³ of each other.\nThe equation for the reaction is:\nH₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O\n\nCalculate the concentration of the sodium hydroxide solution in mol/dm³.\nUse only the concordant results.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mean titre = (22.30 + 22.20) ÷ 2 = 22.25 (cm³) (1)\nmoles H₂SO₄ = 0.0500 × 22.25 / 1000 = 0.0011125 (1)\nmoles NaOH = 0.0011125 × 2 = 0.002225 (1)\nconcentration = 0.002225 ÷ 0.0250 = 0.089 (1)\n= 0.0890 (mol/dm³) to 3 significant figures (1)\nallow ecf at each step from an incorrect mean titre\nan answer of 0.0445 scores 4 if ratio not used\ncorrect answer to 3 significant figures with no working scores 5` },
      { q: `This question is about making a soluble salt.\nCopper sulfate is a soluble salt. It can be made by reacting copper oxide, an insoluble base, with dilute sulfuric acid.\nA student is provided with: copper oxide powder, dilute sulfuric acid, a beaker, a Bunsen burner, tripod and gauze, a glass rod, a filter funnel and filter paper, and an evaporating basin.\n\nDescribe a method the student could use to make pure, dry crystals of copper sulfate.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A coherent method is described with relevant detail, in a logical sequence, which would produce pure, dry crystals of copper sulfate. Includes the use of excess base, removal of the excess and controlled crystallisation.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): The bulk of a method is described with mostly relevant detail, which may not be in a completely logical sequence or may have a step missing.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements are made but may not be linked to a valid method.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• measure a volume of sulfuric acid (e.g. 50 cm³) into a beaker\n• warm the acid (gently, do not boil)\n• add copper oxide a spatula at a time, stirring with the glass rod\n• continue until copper oxide is in excess / some solid remains (so all acid has reacted)\n• filter to remove the excess copper oxide\n• pour the (blue) filtrate into an evaporating basin\n• heat gently to evaporate some of the water / until crystals start to form at the edge\n• do not heat to dryness\n• leave to cool so that crystals form\n• filter / pour off the remaining solution to collect the crystals\n• dry the crystals between filter papers / in a warm oven / leave in a warm place\n• safety: wear eye protection, avoid spitting when heating` },
      { q: `This question is about the extraction of metals.\nTable 5 gives information about two metals (data written for this practice question).\nIron: found as iron oxide (Fe₂O₃); extracted by heating with carbon at about 1500 °C\nAluminium: found as aluminium oxide (Al₂O₃); melting point of Al₂O₃ is 2050 °C; extracted by electrolysis at about 950 °C using carbon electrodes\nCarbon is above iron and below aluminium in the reactivity series.\n\nExplain why iron and aluminium are extracted by different methods, and why extracting aluminium is expensive.\nYou should refer to oxidation and reduction in your answer.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed and coherent explanation is given which links the reactivity of each metal to the method of extraction, refers correctly to oxidation and reduction, and gives logically linked reasons for the cost of extracting aluminium.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): An explanation is given of either the choice of method or the cost of extraction, with some correct reference to oxidation or reduction. The logic may be unclear in places.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements are made about the methods or the cost, which may not be linked.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\nchoice of method\n• iron is less reactive than carbon so carbon can displace iron from iron oxide\n• iron oxide is reduced / loses oxygen\n• carbon is oxidised / gains oxygen (to form carbon dioxide)\n• aluminium is more reactive than carbon so carbon cannot reduce aluminium oxide\n• so aluminium must be extracted by electrolysis\n• aluminium ions gain electrons at the negative electrode / are reduced: Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al\n• oxide ions lose electrons at the positive electrode / are oxidised to oxygen\ncost of extracting aluminium\n• aluminium oxide must be molten so the ions can move\n• a large amount of energy is needed to melt the mixture / keep it at 950 °C\n• cryolite is used to lower the operating temperature (from above 2050 °C), which reduces energy costs, but it is still high\n• a large amount of electricity (electrical energy) is needed for electrolysis\n• oxygen reacts with the carbon positive electrodes to form carbon dioxide\n• so the positive electrodes wear away and must be replaced regularly` }
    ]
  },
  '4.5': {
    name: 'Energy Changes',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about energy changes.\nWhen a student added magnesium ribbon to dilute hydrochloric acid, the test tube became warm.\n\nComplete the sentence.\nChoose the answer from the box: absorbs, transfers, stores.\n\nAn exothermic reaction ______ energy to the surroundings.`, marks: 1, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `transfers` },
      { q: `Calcium oxide is made by heating calcium carbonate strongly in a lime kiln.\nCaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂\nThe reaction only continues while energy is supplied by heating.\n\nWhat type of reaction is the decomposition of calcium carbonate?\nTick one box.\nCombustion\nEndothermic\nExothermic\nNeutralisation`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `Endothermic\nif more than one box ticked, do not award the mark` },
      { q: `A student investigated the temperature change when sodium hydroxide solution reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid.\nThe student carried out the reaction in a polystyrene cup with a lid.\n\nGive one reason why the student used a polystyrene cup instead of a glass beaker.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `polystyrene is a (good thermal) insulator\nallow reduces energy / heat loss (to the surroundings)\nignore keeps the heat in unqualified\ndo not accept prevents all heat loss` },
      { q: `Chemical reactions can only happen when particles collide with enough energy.\n\nWhat is the name for the minimum amount of energy that particles must have to react?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `activation energy` },
      { q: `This question is about chemical cells and batteries.\nA torch contains a battery made of 4 cells connected in series. Each cell has a voltage of 1.5 V.\n\nWhat is the voltage of the battery?\nTick one box.\n0.375 V\n1.5 V\n5.5 V\n6.0 V`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `6.0 V\nif more than one box ticked, do not award the mark` },
      { q: `Some buses are powered by hydrogen fuel cells.\nIn a hydrogen fuel cell, hydrogen is oxidised to produce a potential difference.\n\nName the only chemical product of a hydrogen fuel cell.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `water\nallow H₂O\nallow steam / water vapour` },
      { q: `Instant cold packs are used to treat sports injuries.\nA cold pack contains solid ammonium nitrate and a separate bag of water. When the pack is squeezed, the bag breaks and the ammonium nitrate dissolves in the water. The pack becomes cold.\n\nExplain why the cold pack becomes cold.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(dissolving ammonium nitrate) is endothermic (1)\n(so) energy is taken in from the surroundings\nallow heat is absorbed from the surroundings / the pack / the skin (1)\nignore cold is released` },
      { q: `A student dissolved potassium chloride in water in a polystyrene cup.\nStarting temperature of the water = 21.4 °C\nLowest temperature of the solution = 15.8 °C\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the temperature change.\nGive the type of reaction shown by this result.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(temperature change =) 5.6 (°C) decrease\nallow −5.6 (°C) (1)\nendothermic (1)\nallow ecf from an increase giving exothermic` },
      { q: `A student investigated how the temperature change of a reaction depends on the mass of zinc powder added to 25 cm³ of copper sulfate solution.\nThe student added different masses of zinc powder and measured the highest temperature reached each time.\n\nGive the independent variable and the dependent variable in this investigation.\nIndependent variable: ______\nDependent variable: ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `independent: mass of zinc (powder) (1)\ndependent: temperature change\nallow highest temperature (reached) (1)` },
      { q: `A student investigated the temperature change when different metals were added to dilute hydrochloric acid.\nMethod:\n1. Measure 20 cm³ of dilute hydrochloric acid into a polystyrene cup.\n2. Record the temperature of the acid.\n3. Add a sample of metal to the acid and stir.\n4. Record the highest temperature reached.\n5. Repeat with different metals.\n\nGive two variables the student should control.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• volume of acid\n• concentration of acid\n• mass of metal\nallow amount of metal\n• surface area / size of pieces of metal\nallow use powder for all metals\n• starting temperature of acid\n• (type of) container / insulation\nignore amount of acid\ndo not accept type of metal` },
      { q: `A student carried out a neutralisation reaction in a polystyrene cup.\nThe student did not put a lid on the cup.\n\nSuggest how putting a lid on the cup would improve the investigation.\nGive a reason for your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `reduces energy / heat loss (to the surroundings)\nallow less heat lost by evaporation / convection (1)\n(so) the temperature change measured is closer to the true value\nallow (results) more accurate (1)\nignore more reliable / precise` },
      { q: `A student measured temperature changes using a thermometer with a resolution of 1 °C.\nThe temperature changes measured were between 3 °C and 6 °C.\n\nSuggest a better piece of equipment to measure the temperature.\nGive a reason for your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `digital thermometer\nallow temperature probe / data logger / thermometer reading to 0.1 °C (1)\n(because it has a) higher resolution\nallow smaller increments / more precise / smaller (percentage) uncertainty (1)\nignore more accurate unqualified` },
      { q: `A student repeated an experiment to measure the temperature rise when sodium hydrogencarbonate reacted with dilute ethanoic acid.\nThe student's results were 7.4 °C, 7.0 °C and 7.2 °C.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the mean temperature change and its uncertainty.\nMean temperature change = ______ ± ______ °C`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(mean =) 7.2 (°C) (1)\n(uncertainty =) ± 0.2 (°C)\nallow half the range: (7.4 − 7.0) ÷ 2 (1)` },
      { q: `A reaction profile for a reaction is described:\n• the energy of the reactants is 120 kJ\n• the highest point on the curve is at 310 kJ\n• the energy of the products is 40 kJ.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nDetermine the activation energy and the overall energy change for this reaction.\nActivation energy = ______ kJ\nOverall energy change = ______ kJ`, marks: 2, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `(activation energy =) 190 (kJ) (1)\n(overall energy change =) −80 (kJ)\nallow 80 (kJ) released (1)\ndo not accept +80` },
      { q: `Methane is used as a fuel for cooking. The combustion of methane is exothermic.\nCH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O\n\nExplain why the combustion of methane is exothermic.\nAnswer in terms of bond breaking and bond making.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `energy is needed to break bonds and energy is released when bonds are made\nallow bond breaking is endothermic and bond making is exothermic (1)\nmore energy is released making new bonds than is needed to break the bonds (in the reactants) (1)\ndo not accept energy is released breaking bonds` },
      { q: `Calcium carbonate decomposes when heated. This reaction is endothermic.\nA student drew a reaction profile for an exothermic reaction.\n\nDescribe two ways a reaction profile for the decomposition of calcium carbonate would be different from the reaction profile for an exothermic reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `the products are at a higher energy (level) than the reactants (1)\nthe overall energy change arrow points upwards\nallow energy change is positive / energy is taken in (1)\nallow activation energy (from reactants) is larger for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Hydrogen reacts with chlorine to produce hydrogen chloride.\nH₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl\nEnergy needed to break the bonds in the reactants = 679 kJ/mol\nEnergy released when the bonds in the products are made = 864 kJ/mol\n\nCalculate the overall energy change for the reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `679 − 864 (1)\n−185 (kJ/mol) (1)\nallow 185 (kJ/mol) released for 2 marks\nan answer of +185 scores 1 mark` },
      { q: `Methane burns in oxygen.\nCH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O\nOne molecule of methane has four C–H bonds. One molecule of oxygen has one O=O bond.\nBond energies: C–H = 413 kJ/mol; O=O = 498 kJ/mol\n\nCalculate the energy needed to break all the bonds in 1 mole of methane and 2 moles of oxygen.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(4 × 413) + (2 × 498)\nallow 1652 + 996 (1)\n2648 (kJ) (1)\nan answer of 2648 scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `This question is about chemical cells.\nA student made a simple cell by placing two different metals into an electrolyte and connecting them to a voltmeter.\n\nGive two factors that affect the voltage of the cell.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `(the type of) metals used as electrodes\nallow the difference in reactivity of the metals (1)\n(the type of) electrolyte used\nallow concentration of electrolyte (1)\nignore size of electrodes / temperature` },
      { q: `A student made cells using copper and a second metal, X, Y or Z, dipped in sodium chloride solution.\nVoltage of cell with copper and metal X = 0.6 V\nVoltage of cell with copper and metal Y = 2.1 V\nVoltage of cell with copper and metal Z = 1.0 V\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nUse the results to put metals X, Y and Z in order of reactivity.\nGive a reason for your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Use', markScheme: `Y, Z, X (most reactive first) (1)\nthe bigger the difference in reactivity (from copper) the greater the voltage\nallow the more reactive the metal the higher the voltage (1)` },
      { q: `A smoke alarm uses a 9 V battery. The battery contains six cells. Each cell produces 1.5 V.\n\nExplain how six cells produce a voltage of 9 V.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the cells are connected in series (1)\n(so) the voltages add together\nallow 6 × 1.5 = 9 (V) (1)` },
      { q: `Alkaline batteries are non-rechargeable.\nA TV remote control uses alkaline batteries. After several months the batteries stop working.\n\nExplain why the alkaline batteries stop working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the chemical reactions stop (1)\nbecause one of the reactants has been used up (1)\nignore battery is flat / runs out of charge / electricity` },
      { q: `Hydrogen fuel cells are used to power some cars. Hydrogen reacts with oxygen in the fuel cell.\n\nComplete the balanced symbol equation for the overall reaction in a hydrogen fuel cell.\n___H₂ + O₂ → ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O\nH₂O as the product (1)\ncorrect balancing (1)\nallow multiples\nallow correct state symbols but ignore them if absent` },
      { q: `In a hydrogen fuel cell with an acid electrolyte, oxygen gains electrons at the positive electrode.\n\nComplete the half equation for the reaction at the positive electrode.\nO₂ + 4H⁺ + ______ → ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `4e⁻ (1)\n2H₂O (1)\nO₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O\nallow e for e⁻` },
      { q: `A delivery company is replacing its petrol vans. One option is vans powered by hydrogen fuel cells.\n\nGive two advantages of using hydrogen fuel cells compared with rechargeable lithium-ion batteries to power the vans.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• fuel cells do not need recharging / can be refuelled quickly\nallow refuelling takes less time than recharging\n• vans can travel further before refuelling\n• fuel cells do not lose efficiency as quickly / are not worn out by repeated recharging\n• fuel cells have fewer toxic materials to dispose of\nallow batteries contain toxic (metal) compounds\n• fewer stages of manufacture / disposal\n• only product is water\nignore no pollution unqualified` },
      { q: `Hydrogen fuel cells produce only water when they are working.\nMost hydrogen used in the UK is made from natural gas.\n\nGive two disadvantages of using hydrogen fuel cells to power cars.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• hydrogen is a gas so it is difficult to store\nallow takes up a large volume / needs to be stored under high pressure\n• hydrogen is flammable / explosive\n• producing hydrogen (from natural gas or by electrolysis) uses fossil fuels / releases carbon dioxide\nallow hydrogen production requires energy\n• there are few hydrogen filling stations\n• fuel cells are expensive (to make)` },
      { q: `Some ready meals are sold in self-heating cans. When a button on the can is pressed, water mixes with calcium oxide in a separate compartment.\ncalcium oxide + water → calcium hydroxide\n\nExplain why this reaction is suitable for heating the food in the can.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the reaction is exothermic (1)\n(so) it transfers energy to the surroundings / food\nallow the temperature of the food increases (1)\nallow reactants are cheap / easy to store / no flame needed for 1 mark if exothermic not stated` },
      { q: `Ammonia is produced from nitrogen and hydrogen in the Haber process.\nN₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃\nBond energies: N≡N = 945 kJ/mol; H–H = 436 kJ/mol; N–H = 391 kJ/mol\nEach ammonia molecule contains three N–H bonds.\n\nCalculate the overall energy change for the forward reaction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(bonds broken =) 945 + (3 × 436) = 2253 (kJ/mol) (1)\n(bonds made =) 6 × 391 = 2346 (kJ/mol) (1)\n(2253 − 2346 =) −93 (kJ/mol) (1)\nallow ecf from incorrect totals\nan answer of −93 scores 3 marks\nan answer of +93 scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `Hydrogen burns in oxygen to produce water.\n2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O\nH–H = 436 kJ/mol; O=O = 498 kJ/mol; O–H = 464 kJ/mol\nEach water molecule contains two O–H bonds.\n\nCalculate the overall energy change for the reaction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(bonds broken =) (2 × 436) + 498 = 1370 (kJ/mol) (1)\n(bonds made =) 4 × 464 = 1856 (kJ/mol) (1)\n(1370 − 1856 =) −486 (kJ/mol) (1)\nallow ecf from incorrect totals\nan answer of −486 scores 3 marks\nan answer of +486 scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `Hydrogen reacts with bromine to produce hydrogen bromide.\nH₂ + Br₂ → 2HBr\nThe overall energy change for the reaction is −103 kJ/mol.\nH–H = 436 kJ/mol; Br–Br = 193 kJ/mol\n\nCalculate the bond energy of the H–Br bond.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(bonds broken =) 436 + 193 = 629 (kJ/mol) (1)\n(energy released making bonds =) 629 + 103 = 732 (kJ/mol) (1)\n(H–Br = 732 ÷ 2 =) 366 (kJ/mol) (1)\nallow ecf\nan answer of 366 scores 3 marks\nan answer of 263 (from 629 − 103 then ÷ 2) scores 1 mark` },
      { q: `A student measured the temperature rise when 0.5 g of magnesium powder was added to 25 cm³ of copper sulfate solution.\nThe student did the experiment four times.\nTemperature rise in °C: 12.3, 12.7, 14.9, 12.5\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the mean temperature rise.\nDo not include the anomalous result.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `14.9 identified as anomalous / not included (1)\n(12.3 + 12.7 + 12.5) ÷ 3 (1)\n12.5 (°C) (1)\nan answer of 12.5 scores 3 marks\nan answer of 13.1 scores 1 mark (anomalous result included)` },
      { q: `Mobile phones use rechargeable lithium-ion batteries.\nWhen the phone is used, chemical reactions in the battery produce a voltage until the battery is flat.\n\nExplain how a rechargeable battery can be recharged, and why a non-rechargeable battery cannot be reused.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `an external electrical current is supplied\nallow connect to a charger / mains (1)\n(which) reverses the chemical reactions\nallow reforms the reactants (1)\nin a non-rechargeable battery the reactions cannot be reversed / are irreversible\nallow (once) a reactant is used up it cannot be reformed (1)` },
      { q: `A fuel cell car needs 1.43 × 10⁵ kJ of energy for a journey.\nThe fuel cell releases 286 kJ of energy for each mole of hydrogen used.\n(data written for this practice question)\nRelative formula mass (Mr) of hydrogen, H₂ = 2\n\nCalculate the mass of hydrogen needed for the journey.\nGive your answer in kg.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(moles =) 143 000 ÷ 286 = 500 (mol) (1)\n(mass =) 500 × 2 = 1000 (g) (1)\n1.0 (kg) (1)\nallow ecf from incorrect moles\nan answer of 1 / 1.0 kg scores 3 marks\nan answer of 1000 scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `Table 1 shows the voltages of cells made from different pairs of metals in the same electrolyte.\nmagnesium and copper: 2.71 V\nzinc and copper: 1.10 V\niron and copper: 0.78 V\nIn each cell, copper is the positive electrode.\n\nPredict the voltage of a cell made from magnesium and zinc in the same electrolyte.\nExplain your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `1.61 (V)\nallow 1.6 (V) (1)\n(because) 2.71 − 1.10\nallow difference between the magnesium–copper and zinc–copper voltages (1)\nmagnesium and zinc are closer in reactivity (than magnesium and copper) so the voltage is smaller\nallow the smaller the difference in reactivity the smaller the voltage (1)` },
      { q: `Ethene reacts with hydrogen to produce ethane.\nC₂H₄ + H₂ → C₂H₆\nAn ethene molecule contains one C=C bond and four C–H bonds.\nAn ethane molecule contains one C–C bond and six C–H bonds.\nBond energies: C=C = 612 kJ/mol; C–C = 347 kJ/mol; C–H = 413 kJ/mol; H–H = 436 kJ/mol\n\nCalculate the overall energy change for the reaction.\nGive whether the reaction is exothermic or endothermic.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(bonds broken =) 612 + (4 × 413) + 436 = 2700 (kJ/mol) (1)\n(bonds made =) 347 + (6 × 413) = 2825 (kJ/mol) (1)\n(2700 − 2825 =) −125 (kJ/mol) (1)\nexothermic\nallow ecf from sign of calculated answer (1)\nalternative method: C–H bonds cancel: (612 + 436) − (347 + 2 × 413) = −125 scores the first three marks` },
      { q: `A student investigated the reaction between dilute hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide solution.\nThe student added the acid 5 cm³ at a time to 25 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution in a polystyrene cup and recorded the temperature after each addition.\nVolume of acid added in cm³ / Temperature in °C\n0 / 19.0\n5 / 22.4\n10 / 25.2\n15 / 27.4\n20 / 28.6\n25 / 28.4\n30 / 27.6\n35 / 26.9\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nExplain the changes in temperature in terms of the energy transfers taking place.\nYou should use data from the table in your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `temperature increases (from 19.0 °C) up to 20 cm³ / 28.6 °C (1)\nbecause neutralisation is exothermic / energy is released to the surroundings (1)\nat about 20 cm³ all the sodium hydroxide has reacted / reaction is complete\nallow maximum temperature at neutralisation (1)\nafter 20 cm³ temperature decreases because no more energy is released and the extra (cooler) acid absorbs energy\nallow energy is lost to the surroundings (1)` },
      { q: `Propane burns in oxygen. The reaction is exothermic.\nC₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O\nThe energy of the reactants is higher than the energy of the products. The activation energy is small compared with the overall energy change.\n\nDraw a reaction profile for this reaction.\nLabel:\n• the reactants and products\n• the activation energy\n• the overall energy change.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `products line drawn lower than reactants line on an energy (y) axis (1)\ncurve rising from reactants to a peak then falling to products (1)\nactivation energy shown by an arrow from the reactants line up to the peak (1)\noverall energy change shown by an arrow from the reactants level down to the products level\nallow double-headed arrow or line between the levels for the overall energy change (1)\nactivation energy must be shown as smaller than the overall energy change for full marks` },
      { q: `Propane is used as a fuel in camping stoves.\nC₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O\nA propane molecule contains two C–C bonds and eight C–H bonds.\nEach carbon dioxide molecule contains two C=O bonds. Each water molecule contains two O–H bonds.\nBond energies: C–C = 347 kJ/mol; C–H = 413 kJ/mol; O=O = 498 kJ/mol; C=O = 805 kJ/mol; O–H = 464 kJ/mol\n\nCalculate the overall energy change for the combustion of propane.\nExplain what your answer shows about the reaction.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(bonds broken =) (2 × 347) + (8 × 413) + (5 × 498) = 6488 (kJ/mol) (1)\n(bonds made =) (6 × 805) + (8 × 464) = 8542 (kJ/mol) (1)\n(6488 − 8542 =) −2054 (kJ/mol) (1)\nallow ecf from incorrect totals\nthe reaction is exothermic (1)\nbecause more energy is released making bonds than is needed to break bonds\nallow because the energy change is negative (1)` },
      { q: `A student wanted to investigate how the temperature change of a displacement reaction depends on the metal used.\nThe student was given magnesium powder, zinc powder, iron powder and copper sulfate solution.\nThe student also had access to normal laboratory equipment.\n\nPlan an investigation to find how the temperature change depends on the metal used.\nYour plan should give valid results.`, marks: 6, command: 'Plan', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): a coherent method is described with relevant detail, in a logical sequence, that would lead to valid results, including control variables and how the temperature change is determined.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): the bulk of a method is described with mostly relevant detail; some steps may be out of order or a control variable missing.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements are made about relevant steps; the response may lack a logical structure.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• measure a fixed volume of copper sulfate solution (e.g. 25 cm³) using a measuring cylinder\n• into a polystyrene cup (in a beaker for stability)\n• measure the starting temperature with a thermometer\n• weigh a fixed mass of one metal powder (e.g. 0.5 g) on a balance\n• add the metal and stir\n• put a lid on the cup\n• record the highest temperature reached\n• calculate the temperature change (highest − starting temperature)\n• repeat with each of the other metals\n• use the same concentration of copper sulfate solution\n• use the same starting temperature\n• use the same particle size of metal / all powders\n• use excess metal or the same amount in moles\n• repeat each experiment and calculate a mean\n• use a fresh cup / rinse the cup between experiments` },
      { q: `A city council wants to replace its diesel buses. It is considering buses powered by hydrogen fuel cells and buses powered by rechargeable lithium-ion batteries.\nTable 2 shows information about the two types of bus.\n(data written for this practice question)\nFeature / Hydrogen fuel cell bus / Lithium-ion battery bus\nDistance travelled before refuelling or recharging in km / 450 / 250\nTime to refuel or recharge / 10 minutes / 4 hours\nCost of the bus in £ / 500 000 / 350 000\nNumber of refuelling or charging points in the city / 1 / 25\nProducts released when bus is running / water / none\n\nEvaluate the use of hydrogen fuel cells compared with rechargeable lithium-ion batteries to power the buses.\nUse Table 2 and your own knowledge.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): a detailed and coherent evaluation is given that considers a range of advantages and disadvantages of both, uses data from the table and own knowledge, and reaches a justified conclusion.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): a comparison of fuel cells and batteries is made using some data and some own knowledge; a conclusion may be stated but not fully justified.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements are made about fuel cells or batteries; little use of data.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\nfuel cell buses:\n• travel further (450 km compared with 250 km) before refuelling\n• refuel much faster (10 minutes compared with 4 hours) so spend more time in service\n• only product is water / no carbon dioxide released when running\n• no recharging so do not lose capacity over many cycles\n• fewer toxic materials to dispose of at end of life\n• cost more (£150 000 more per bus)\n• only one refuelling point so more infrastructure needed\n• hydrogen is a flammable gas, difficult to store, needs high pressure / large volume\n• hydrogen is mostly made from natural gas (releasing CO₂) or by electrolysis using electricity\nbattery buses:\n• cheaper to buy and many charging points already available\n• no products released when running\n• long recharge time; shorter range\n• batteries contain toxic metal compounds; limited number of recharge cycles\n• electricity for recharging may be generated from fossil fuels\nconclusion:\n• a justified judgement, e.g. fuel cells are better for long routes / if more refuelling points are built; batteries are better while infrastructure is lacking or for cost` }
    ]
  },
  '4.6': {
    name: 'The Rate and Extent of Chemical Change',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about the rate of a reaction.\n\nA student added marble chips (calcium carbonate) to dilute hydrochloric acid in a conical flask. Bubbles of gas were produced.\n\nName the gas produced.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• carbon dioxide (1)\nallow CO₂\ndo not accept carbon monoxide / hydrogen` },
      { q: `Ammonia is made from nitrogen and hydrogen. The equation for the reaction is:\n\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)\n\nWhat does the symbol ⇌ show about this reaction?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `• the reaction is reversible (1)\nallow the reaction can go in both directions / products can react to form the reactants again\nignore equilibrium unqualified` },
      { q: `A student investigated the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide solution. The student added 0.50 g of manganese(IV) oxide to the hydrogen peroxide solution. Oxygen gas was produced quickly.\n\nManganese(IV) oxide is a catalyst for this reaction.\n\nWhat is the mass of manganese(IV) oxide at the end of the reaction?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `• 0.50 g (1)\nallow 0.5 g\nallow the same mass as at the start` },
      { q: `This question is about a reversible reaction.\n\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)\n\nThe forward reaction is exothermic. When 2 moles of ammonia are formed, 92 kJ of energy is transferred to the surroundings.\n\nHow much energy is taken in from the surroundings when 2 moles of ammonia decompose into nitrogen and hydrogen?`, marks: 1, command: 'How', markScheme: `• 92 kJ (1)\nallow the same amount (of energy) as the forward reaction gives out\nignore sign` },
      { q: `A student investigated how the concentration of sodium thiosulfate solution affects the rate of its reaction with hydrochloric acid.\n\nThe student put a conical flask on a piece of paper marked with a black cross. The student timed how long it took for the cross to disappear as a yellow precipitate formed.\n\nWhat is the dependent variable in this investigation?\n\nTick one box.\nthe concentration of sodium thiosulfate solution\nthe size of the cross\nthe time taken for the cross to disappear\nthe volume of hydrochloric acid`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• the time taken for the cross to disappear (1)\nif more than one box ticked, award 0` },
      { q: `A student measured the volume of hydrogen gas produced when magnesium reacted with dilute sulfuric acid. The volume of gas was measured in cm³ and the time was measured in seconds.\n\nWhich is the unit for the mean rate of this reaction?\n\nTick one box.\ncm³\ncm³/s\ns/cm³\ng/s`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• cm³/s (1)\nif more than one box ticked, award 0` },
      { q: `A student added magnesium ribbon to dilute hydrochloric acid. The hydrogen gas produced was collected in a gas syringe.\n\n36 cm³ of hydrogen gas was collected in the first 40 seconds.\n\nCalculate the mean rate of reaction in the first 40 seconds.\n\nMean rate = ______ cm³/s`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• mean rate = 36 ÷ 40 (1)\n• = 0.9 (cm³/s) (1)\nallow 0.90\nan answer of 0.9 scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student put a conical flask containing marble chips and dilute hydrochloric acid on a balance. Carbon dioxide gas escaped from the flask.\n\nThe mass of the flask and contents decreased by 1.20 g in 150 seconds.\n\nCalculate the mean rate of reaction in g/s.\n\nMean rate = ______ g/s`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• mean rate = 1.20 ÷ 150 (1)\n• = 0.0080 (g/s) (1)\nallow 0.008 / 8.0 × 10⁻³\nan answer of 0.008 scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student compared the rate of reaction of calcium carbonate with dilute hydrochloric acid using large marble chips and using powdered calcium carbonate.\n\nThe same mass of calcium carbonate and the same volume and concentration of acid were used each time. The powdered calcium carbonate reacted faster.\n\nExplain why the powdered calcium carbonate reacted faster.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• powder has a larger surface area (to volume ratio) (1)\n• (so) more frequent collisions (between acid particles and calcium carbonate) (1)\nallow more collisions per second / per unit time\ndo not accept 'more collisions' unqualified for the second mark\ndo not accept particles move faster` },
      { q: `This question is about the reaction between magnesium and hydrochloric acid.\n\nA student found that magnesium reacted faster with 2.0 mol/dm³ hydrochloric acid than with 1.0 mol/dm³ hydrochloric acid.\n\nExplain why the rate of reaction is greater with the more concentrated acid.\n\nUse collision theory in your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (in the more concentrated acid there are) more (acid) particles in the same volume (1)\nallow particles are closer together\n• (so) collisions are more frequent (1)\nallow more collisions per second\ndo not accept particles have more energy / move faster` },
      { q: `Hydrogen and iodine react in the gas phase to produce hydrogen iodide.\n\nH₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g)\n\nA scientist increased the pressure of the gases while keeping the temperature constant. The rate of reaction increased.\n\nExplain why increasing the pressure increases the rate of this reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (at higher pressure there are) more (gas) particles in the same volume (1)\nallow particles closer together\n• (so) more frequent collisions (1)\nallow more collisions per second\ndo not accept particles have more energy` },
      { q: `Hydrogen peroxide decomposes slowly to produce water and oxygen.\n\n2H₂O₂(aq) → 2H₂O(l) + O₂(g)\n\nWhen a small amount of manganese(IV) oxide is added, the hydrogen peroxide decomposes much faster.\n\nExplain how a catalyst increases the rate of a reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (a catalyst) provides a different (reaction) pathway (1)\n• with a lower activation energy (1)\nallow (so) more particles / collisions have energy greater than or equal to the activation energy for 1 mark if pathway not mentioned\ndo not accept catalyst gives particles more energy` },
      { q: `This question is about collision theory.\n\nWhen hydrochloric acid is added to zinc granules, the zinc reacts and hydrogen gas is produced. Not every collision between an acid particle and a zinc atom causes a reaction.\n\nGive the two conditions needed for a collision to result in a reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• the particles must collide (1)\n• with (at least) the minimum amount of energy / with energy equal to or greater than the activation energy (1)\nallow with enough energy\nignore correct orientation` },
      { q: `A student investigated how the concentration of sodium thiosulfate solution affects the rate of its reaction with dilute hydrochloric acid. The student used the time taken for a black cross under the flask to disappear.\n\nThe independent variable was the concentration of sodium thiosulfate solution.\n\nGive two control variables in this investigation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• volume of sodium thiosulfate solution (1)\n• volume of hydrochloric acid (1)\n• concentration of hydrochloric acid (1)\n• temperature (of the solutions) (1)\n• (size / type of) the cross / the same cross / same paper (1)\n• same size / shape of flask (1)\nignore amount for volume\nignore same equipment unqualified` },
      { q: `A student investigated the reaction between sodium thiosulfate solution and hydrochloric acid. A yellow precipitate of sulfur formed and made the solution cloudy. The student recorded the time when they could no longer see a black cross under the flask.\n\nThe student's partner repeated the experiment and recorded a different time.\n\nSuggest why the two students recorded different times, and suggest how the method could be improved.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• (deciding when) the cross disappears depends on the person's eyesight / is subjective (1)\nallow different people judge the end point differently\n• use a light sensor / light meter (and data logger) (to measure the cloudiness) (1)\nallow use a colorimeter\nallow the same person judges the end point every time` },
      { q: `A student repeated a rate experiment four times. Table 1 shows the results.\n\nTable 1\nRun 1: 42 s\nRun 2: 45 s\nRun 3: 39 s\nRun 4: 58 s\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the mean time.\n\nDo not include the anomalous result in your calculation.\n\nMean time = ______ s`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (42 + 45 + 39) ÷ 3 (1)\n• = 42 (s) (1)\nan answer of 42 scores 2 marks\nan answer of 46 (all four results used) scores 1 mark` },
      { q: `This question is about a reversible reaction.\n\nhydrated copper sulfate ⇌ anhydrous copper sulfate + water\n(blue)                              (white)\n\nThe forward reaction is endothermic. A student heated blue hydrated copper sulfate until it turned white. The student let the white solid cool, then added a few drops of water.\n\nDescribe two observations the student would make when water is added to the white solid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (white solid) turns blue (1)\n• the solid / test tube gets hot / warm (1)\nallow steam / fizzing / hissing for second mark\nallow temperature increases` },
      { q: `This question is about equilibrium.\n\nA student sealed a mixture of nitrogen dioxide and dinitrogen tetroxide in a glass tube. After some time the colour of the mixture stopped changing.\n\n2NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g)\n\nThe reaction had reached equilibrium.\n\nExplain what is meant by equilibrium for a reversible reaction in a closed system.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the forward and reverse reactions occur at the same rate (1)\ndo not accept the reactions stop\n• (so) the amounts / concentrations of reactants and products do not change (1)\ndo not accept the concentrations of reactants and products are equal` },
      { q: `The reaction between iron(III) ions and thiocyanate ions is reversible.\n\nFe³⁺(aq) + SCN⁻(aq) ⇌ FeSCN²⁺(aq)\nyellow       colourless     red\n\nThe equilibrium mixture is orange. A student added more Fe³⁺ ions to the equilibrium mixture. The mixture became a darker red.\n\nExplain why the mixture became a darker red.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (position of) equilibrium shifts to the right / more FeSCN²⁺ is produced (1)\nallow forward reaction is favoured\n• to reduce the concentration of Fe³⁺ (ions) / to oppose the increase in concentration of Fe³⁺ (1)\nallow (system) counteracts the change` },
      { q: `Sulfur trioxide is made from sulfur dioxide and oxygen.\n\n2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)\n\nExplain why increasing the pressure increases the yield of sulfur trioxide at equilibrium.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (increasing pressure shifts the position of equilibrium) towards the side with fewer molecules (of gas) (1)\nallow to reduce the pressure\n• there are 2 molecules / moles on the right and 3 on the left (so equilibrium shifts right) (1)\nallow fewer molecules on the product side` },
      { q: `Ammonia is made by the reaction of nitrogen with hydrogen.\n\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)\n\nThe forward reaction is exothermic.\n\nExplain what happens to the yield of ammonia at equilibrium if the temperature is increased.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• yield (of ammonia) decreases (1)\n• (because) equilibrium shifts in the direction of the endothermic reaction / reverse reaction is favoured (1)\nallow equilibrium shifts to the left to reduce the temperature\nsecond mark depends on first mark` },
      { q: `A student measured the volume of carbon dioxide produced when calcium carbonate reacted with hydrochloric acid. The student plotted a graph of volume of gas (cm³) against time (s). The line was a curve that became less steep with time.\n\nTo find the rate at 30 s, the student drew a tangent to the curve at 30 s. The tangent passed through the points (10 s, 22 cm³) and (70 s, 58 cm³).\n\nCalculate the rate of reaction at 30 s.\n\nRate = ______ cm³/s`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• gradient = (58 − 22) ÷ (70 − 10) or 36 ÷ 60 (1)\n• = 0.60 (cm³/s) (1)\nallow 0.6\nan answer of 0.6 scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student added 0.24 g of magnesium ribbon to excess dilute hydrochloric acid. All the magnesium reacted in 40 seconds.\n\nRelative atomic mass (Aᵣ): Mg = 24\n\nCalculate the mean rate of reaction in mol/s.\n\nMean rate = ______ mol/s`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• moles of Mg = 0.24 ÷ 24 = 0.010 (mol) (1)\n• mean rate = 0.010 ÷ 40 = 2.5 × 10⁻⁴ (mol/s) (1)\nallow 0.00025\nan answer of 2.5 × 10⁻⁴ scores 2 marks\nallow ecf from incorrect moles` },
      { q: `A student reacted 2.0 g of marble chips with excess hydrochloric acid and measured the volume of carbon dioxide produced. The student then repeated the experiment with 2.0 g of powdered marble.\n\nThe reaction with powder was faster, but the total volume of carbon dioxide collected was 480 cm³ in both experiments.\n\nExplain why the total volume of carbon dioxide collected was the same in both experiments.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the same mass / amount of calcium carbonate was used (1)\n• (calcium carbonate was the limiting reactant because) the acid was in excess / all the calcium carbonate reacted (1)\nallow surface area affects only the rate, not the amount of product, for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Hydrogen reacts with iodine to produce hydrogen iodide.\n\nH₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g)\n\nA scientist increased the pressure of an equilibrium mixture of these gases at constant temperature.\n\nExplain why the yield of hydrogen iodide did not change.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• there are the same number of (gas) molecules / moles on each side of the equation (1)\nallow 2 molecules on the left and 2 on the right\n• (so) changing pressure does not favour either the forward or the reverse reaction / position of equilibrium does not shift (1)` },
      { q: `A student investigated the reaction between sodium thiosulfate solution and hydrochloric acid.\n\nNa₂S₂O₃(aq) + 2HCl(aq) → 2NaCl(aq) + SO₂(g) + S(s) + H₂O(l)\n\nSulfur dioxide is a toxic gas that can trigger asthma attacks.\n\nGive one hazard of this investigation and a precaution to reduce the risk from this hazard.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `hazard (1) with linked precaution (1) eg:\n• sulfur dioxide is toxic / irritates lungs (1) – work in a well-ventilated room / use a fume cupboard / pour solution away quickly after the experiment (1)\nor\n• hydrochloric acid is an irritant / corrosive (1) – wear eye protection / goggles (1)\nallow use low concentrations of acid for precaution\nignore wear gloves / lab coat` },
      { q: `A student heated some white ammonium chloride in the bottom of a test tube. The white solid disappeared from the bottom of the tube. A white solid then formed near the cool top of the tube.\n\nammonium chloride ⇌ ammonia + hydrogen chloride\n\nExplain these observations.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (on heating) ammonium chloride decomposes into (the gases) ammonia and hydrogen chloride (1)\n• (at the cooler top) the reaction is reversed / ammonia and hydrogen chloride react to form ammonium chloride again (1)\nallow the reaction is reversible for 1 mark if neither point given` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of temperature on the rate of reaction between magnesium ribbon and dilute hydrochloric acid. The reaction was faster at 50 °C than at 20 °C.\n\nExplain why increasing the temperature increases the rate of reaction.\n\nUse collision theory in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• particles have more (kinetic) energy / move faster (1)\n• (so) collisions are more frequent (1)\nallow more collisions per second\n• more of the collisions have energy equal to or greater than the activation energy / more collisions are successful (1)\nallow more particles have enough energy to react\ndo not accept 'more collisions' unqualified for the second mark` },
      { q: `A student added zinc to dilute sulfuric acid and collected the hydrogen gas in an inverted measuring cylinder.\n\n0.12 dm³ of hydrogen gas was collected in 2.0 minutes.\n\nCalculate the mean rate of reaction in cm³/s.\n\nMean rate = ______ cm³/s`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 0.12 dm³ = 120 cm³ (1)\n• 2.0 minutes = 120 s (1)\n• mean rate = 120 ÷ 120 = 1.0 (cm³/s) (1)\nallow 1\nan answer of 1.0 scores 3 marks\nan answer of 0.06 (cm³ not converted) or 60 (minutes not converted) scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student investigated how the concentration of hydrochloric acid affects the rate of reaction with calcium carbonate. The student measured the time taken to collect 30 cm³ of carbon dioxide.\n\nTable 2\n1.0 mol/dm³ acid: 20 s to collect 30 cm³\n0.50 mol/dm³ acid: 52 s to collect 30 cm³\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nHow many times greater is the mean rate of reaction with 1.0 mol/dm³ acid than with 0.50 mol/dm³ acid?\n\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• mean rate with 1.0 mol/dm³ = 30 ÷ 20 = 1.5 (cm³/s) (1)\n• mean rate with 0.50 mol/dm³ = 30 ÷ 52 = 0.577 (cm³/s) (1)\n• 1.5 ÷ 0.577 = 2.6 (times) (1)\nallow 52 ÷ 20 = 2.6 for 3 marks\nan answer of 2.6 scores 3 marks` },
      { q: `A student tested three metal oxides, A, B and C, as possible catalysts for the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide. The student added 0.50 g of each metal oxide to 25 cm³ of hydrogen peroxide solution and measured the volume of oxygen collected in 60 seconds.\n\nTable 3\nNo metal oxide: 2 cm³\nMetal oxide A: 3 cm³\nMetal oxide B: 46 cm³\nMetal oxide C: 18 cm³\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nThe student concluded that metal oxide B was the best catalyst.\n\nGive one reason for this conclusion. Describe how the student could show that metal oxide B was not used up in the reaction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• B produced the largest volume of oxygen in 60 s / gave the fastest rate (1)\n• filter (the mixture) to separate B and dry it (1)\n• weigh B and show the mass is still 0.50 g (1)\nallow mass is unchanged / same as at the start\nallow show that B still catalyses a fresh sample of hydrogen peroxide (at the same rate) for third mark` },
      { q: `Nitrogen dioxide (NO₂) is a brown gas. Dinitrogen tetroxide (N₂O₄) is a colourless gas.\n\n2NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g)\nbrown        colourless\n\nA student sealed an equilibrium mixture of the two gases in a gas syringe. The student pushed the plunger in quickly to increase the pressure, keeping the temperature constant. After a few seconds the mixture became a paler brown than before.\n\nExplain why the mixture became paler.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (increasing pressure) shifts the position of equilibrium to the right / towards N₂O₄ (1)\n• (because) there are fewer molecules on the right / 1 molecule on the right and 2 on the left (1)\n• (so) the amount / concentration of NO₂ (the brown gas) decreases (1)\nallow more colourless N₂O₄ is produced` },
      { q: `This question is about energy changes in reactions.\n\nThe reaction between hydrogen peroxide and a catalyst is exothermic.\n\nDraw a reaction profile for an exothermic reaction.\n\nShow the reaction profile without a catalyst and with a catalyst. Label the activation energy for the reaction without a catalyst.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• products shown at a lower energy than reactants (1)\n• two curves rising above reactants then falling to products, with the catalysed curve having a lower peak (1)\n• activation energy shown as an arrow / line from the reactant energy level to the top of the uncatalysed curve (1)\nignore labelling of axes unless incorrect\ndo not accept activation energy arrow from products` },
      { q: `Gas P reacts to form gas Q in a reversible reaction.\n\n2P(g) ⇌ Q(g)\n\nTable 4 shows the percentage yield of Q at equilibrium at different temperatures. The pressure was the same each time.\n\nTable 4\n200 °C: 72%\n300 °C: 55%\n400 °C: 36%\n500 °C: 20%\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nIs the forward reaction exothermic or endothermic?\n\nExplain your answer. Use Table 4.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• exothermic (1)\n• (because) the yield of Q decreases as temperature increases (1)\n• (increasing temperature) shifts the position of equilibrium in the direction of the endothermic reaction, which is the reverse reaction / to the left (1)\nmarks 2 and 3 can be awarded if exothermic not given` },
      { q: `A student reacted marble chips with dilute hydrochloric acid in a conical flask on a balance. The student recorded the total mass of carbon dioxide lost from the flask.\n\nTable 5\nTime 0 s: 0.00 g\nTime 30 s: 0.66 g\nTime 60 s: 1.08 g\nTime 90 s: 1.32 g\nTime 120 s: 1.44 g\nTime 150 s: 1.44 g\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nThe mean rate of reaction between 0 s and 30 s was 0.022 g/s.\n\nCalculate the mean rate of reaction between 60 s and 90 s.\n\nExplain why the rate of reaction decreases as the reaction continues.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `calculation:\n• (1.32 − 1.08) ÷ 30 or 0.24 ÷ 30 (1)\n• = 0.0080 (g/s) (1)\nallow 0.008 / 8.0 × 10⁻³\nexplanation:\n• the concentration of acid decreases / the acid (or marble) is being used up (1)\nallow fewer reactant particles\n• (so) collisions become less frequent (1)\nallow fewer collisions per second\ndo not accept fewer collisions unqualified` },
      { q: `A student wants to investigate how the concentration of hydrochloric acid affects the rate of its reaction with magnesium ribbon.\n\nMg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g)\n\nThe student is given: magnesium ribbon, hydrochloric acid of five different concentrations, a conical flask with a bung and delivery tube, a gas syringe and a stopwatch.\n\nDescribe a method the student could use to measure the rate of reaction for each concentration of acid.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• measure a set volume of acid (eg 50 cm³) with a measuring cylinder into the conical flask (1)\n• add a set length / mass of magnesium ribbon and immediately put the bung in and start the stopwatch (1)\n• measure the volume of hydrogen in the gas syringe at regular time intervals / every 10 s or measure the time to collect a set volume of gas (1)\n• repeat with each of the other concentrations, keeping the volume of acid, length of magnesium and temperature the same (1)\nallow collect the gas in an upturned measuring cylinder full of water\nallow calculate rate = volume ÷ time` },
      { q: `Methanol is manufactured from carbon monoxide and hydrogen.\n\nCO(g) + 2H₂(g) ⇌ CH₃OH(g)\n\nThe forward reaction is exothermic.\n\nExplain the effect on the yield of methanol at equilibrium of:\n• increasing the pressure\n• increasing the temperature.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `pressure:\n• yield increases (1)\n• (because) equilibrium shifts to the side with fewer molecules / 1 molecule on the right and 3 on the left (1)\ntemperature:\n• yield decreases (1)\n• (because) equilibrium shifts in the endothermic direction / the reverse reaction is endothermic and is favoured (1)\nexplanation marks depend on the correct effect` },
      { q: `A student investigated how the concentration of sodium thiosulfate solution affects the rate of its reaction with hydrochloric acid. The student timed how long it took for a cross under the flask to disappear.\n\nThe student calculated the rate of reaction using:\n\nrate = 1000 ÷ time\n\nTable 6\nConcentration 10 g/dm³: time 125 s, rate 8.0\nConcentration 20 g/dm³: time 63 s, rate 15.9\nConcentration 30 g/dm³: time 42 s, rate X\nConcentration 40 g/dm³: time 31 s, rate 32.3\nConcentration 50 g/dm³: time 25 s, rate Y\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate values X and Y. Give your answers to 1 decimal place.\n\nDescribe the relationship between the concentration of sodium thiosulfate and the rate of reaction.\n\nExplain this relationship in terms of particles.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• X = 1000 ÷ 42 = 23.8 (1)\n• Y = 1000 ÷ 25 = 40.0 (1)\nallow 40\n• rate is (directly) proportional to concentration (1)\nallow as concentration doubles the rate doubles\nallow as concentration increases the rate increases for this mark only if proportional not stated\n• (higher concentration means) more particles in the same volume (1)\n• (so) more frequent collisions (1)\nallow more collisions per second` },
      { q: `A student wants to investigate how temperature affects the rate of reaction between sodium thiosulfate solution and dilute hydrochloric acid.\n\nNa₂S₂O₃(aq) + 2HCl(aq) → 2NaCl(aq) + SO₂(g) + S(s) + H₂O(l)\n\nAs the reaction happens, a yellow precipitate of sulfur forms and the mixture becomes cloudy.\n\nPlan an investigation to find out how temperature affects the rate of this reaction.\n\nYour plan should give valid results.`, marks: 6, command: 'Plan', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): a coherent method is described with relevant detail, which demonstrates a broad understanding of the relevant scientific techniques and procedures. The steps in the method are logically ordered. The method would lead to the production of valid results.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): the bulk of a method is described with mostly relevant detail, which demonstrates a reasonable understanding of the relevant scientific techniques and procedures. The method may not be in a completely logical sequence and may be missing some detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements are made which demonstrate some understanding of some of the relevant scientific techniques and procedures. The response may lack a logical structure and would not lead to the production of valid results.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• measure a set volume of sodium thiosulfate solution (eg 50 cm³) with a measuring cylinder\n• heat the solution in a water bath to the chosen temperature\n• measure the temperature with a thermometer\n• measure a set volume of hydrochloric acid (eg 5 cm³) of fixed concentration\n• place the flask on a piece of paper marked with a cross\n• add the acid to the flask, swirl and start a stopwatch\n• look down through the solution and stop the stopwatch when the cross can no longer be seen\n• repeat at a range of at least five temperatures (eg 20 °C to 60 °C)\n• keep the volumes and concentrations of both solutions and the cross the same\n• repeat each temperature and calculate a mean time\n• calculate rate as 1 ÷ time or 1000 ÷ time\n• use a well-ventilated room because sulfur dioxide is produced\nallow use of a light sensor / data logger instead of the cross` },
      { q: `Ethanol can be manufactured by reacting ethene with steam.\n\nC₂H₄(g) + H₂O(g) ⇌ C₂H₅OH(g)\n\nThe forward reaction is exothermic. A phosphoric acid catalyst is used.\n\nTable 7 shows the percentage yield of ethanol at equilibrium under different conditions.\n\nTable 7\n200 °C, 30 atm: 36%\n200 °C, 70 atm: 52%\n300 °C, 30 atm: 14%\n300 °C, 70 atm: 24%\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nThe company uses a temperature of 300 °C and a pressure of 70 atm.\n\nExplain why the company uses these conditions and a catalyst.\n\nUse Table 7 and your knowledge of rate and equilibrium in your answer.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): a detailed and coherent explanation is given of the effects of temperature and pressure on both yield and rate, with use of the data, and links these to the choice of conditions. The response makes logical links between clearly identified, relevant points.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): an explanation is given of the effect of temperature and/or pressure on yield or rate, with some reference to the data or to the choice of conditions. The logic may be unclear and links may not be clearly identified.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements are made about the effect of temperature, pressure or the catalyst. The response may fail to make logical links between the points raised.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\ntemperature\n• the forward reaction is exothermic, so a higher temperature shifts equilibrium to the left (endothermic direction)\n• Table 7 shows the yield is lower at 300 °C than at 200 °C (eg 24% compared with 52% at 70 atm)\n• but a higher temperature increases the rate (particles have more energy, more frequent and more successful collisions)\n• 300 °C is a compromise between an acceptable yield and a fast enough rate\npressure\n• there are 2 molecules of gas on the left and 1 on the right\n• higher pressure shifts equilibrium to the right, so yield increases (eg 14% to 24% at 300 °C)\n• higher pressure also increases the rate (more particles in the same volume, more frequent collisions)\n• very high pressures are expensive / need stronger equipment and are more dangerous, so 70 atm is a compromise\ncatalyst\n• the catalyst increases the rate by providing a pathway with a lower activation energy\n• the catalyst does not change the yield at equilibrium\n• so equilibrium is reached faster at a lower temperature than would otherwise be needed / saves energy and cost\nother\n• unreacted ethene and steam can be recycled` }
    ]
  },
  '4.7': {
    name: 'Organic Chemistry',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about crude oil.\nCrude oil is a finite resource found in rocks. It was formed over millions of years.\n\nWhat is crude oil mainly formed from?\nTick (✓) one box.\nThe remains of ancient biomass, mainly plankton\nThe remains of volcanic rocks\nGases from the early atmosphere\nSalts dissolved in sea water`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `The remains of ancient biomass, mainly plankton (1)` },
      { q: `This question is about alkanes.\nThe alkanes are a homologous series of hydrocarbons. Pentane is an alkane with 5 carbon atoms in each molecule.\n\nGive the molecular formula of pentane.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `C₅H₁₂ (1)` },
      { q: `A student added a few drops of orange bromine water to a sample of hex-1-ene, an alkene. The student shook the mixture.\n\nComplete the sentence. Choose the answer from the box: colourless / green / purple\nThe bromine water turned ______.`, marks: 1, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `colourless (1)\nignore clear` },
      { q: `This question is about carboxylic acids.\nVinegar is a solution of ethanoic acid in water.\n\nWhich group of atoms is the functional group of all carboxylic acids?\nTick (✓) one box.\n–C=C–\n–COOH\n–OH\n–NH₂`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `–COOH (1)` },
      { q: `This question is about natural polymers.\nDNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) is a large molecule found in the cells of living organisms. DNA encodes the genetic instructions for the development and functioning of organisms.\n\nComplete the sentence. Choose the answer from the box: amino acids / glucose / nucleotides\nDNA is a polymer made from four different monomers called ______.`, marks: 1, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `nucleotides (1)` },
      { q: `A student added solid sodium carbonate to a solution of ethanoic acid in a test tube. The mixture fizzed.\n\nName the gas produced.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `carbon dioxide (1)\nallow CO₂\ndo not accept hydrogen` },
      { q: `This question is about hydrocarbons.\nEthane has the formula C₂H₆. Each carbon atom in ethane forms four single covalent bonds.\n\nExplain why ethane is described as a saturated hydrocarbon.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `hydrocarbon: contains (atoms of) hydrogen and carbon only (1)\nsaturated: (contains) only single (C–C) bonds / no (C=C) double bonds (1)\nallow cannot have any more hydrogen atoms added\nignore contains no double bonds between carbon and hydrogen` },
      { q: `Crude oil is separated into fractions by fractional distillation.\nTable 1 shows information about some of the fractions. (data written for this practice question)\nFraction | Boiling range in °C | Number of carbon atoms in each molecule\nLiquefied petroleum gases | below 25 | 1–4\nPetrol | 40–100 | 5–10\nKerosene | 150–250 | 10–16\nDiesel oil | 250–350 | 14–20\nFuel oil | above 350 | 20–40\n\nWhich fraction in Table 1 has the highest viscosity?\nGive a reason for your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `fuel oil (1)\n(because it has) the largest molecules / the longest (carbon) chains / the most carbon atoms (in each molecule) (1)\nallow highest boiling point(s)\nthe reason mark is dependent on the correct fraction being chosen` },
      { q: `A teacher demonstrated the flammability of three alkanes. The teacher put a small volume of each alkane on a separate watch glass and held a lighted splint near it.\nThe results were:\nhexane (C₆H₁₄) – caught fire immediately\ndecane (C₁₀H₂₂) – caught fire after a few seconds\nhexadecane (C₁₆H₃₄) – did not catch fire\n\nDescribe the pattern shown by these results.\nRefer to the size of the molecules in your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `flammability decreases (1)\nas the number of carbon atoms / chain length / size of the molecule increases (1)\nallow converse: smaller molecules are more flammable for 2 marks\nallow hexane is the most flammable and hexadecane the least for 1 mark if no other mark awarded` },
      { q: `Natural gas is mainly methane. Methane is used as a fuel in gas boilers. In a boiler with a good supply of air, methane undergoes complete combustion.\n\nComplete the word equation for the complete combustion of methane.\nmethane + oxygen ⟶ ______ + ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `carbon dioxide (1)\nwater (1)\neither order\nallow correct formulae (CO₂, H₂O)\ndo not accept carbon monoxide` },
      { q: `Propane (C₃H₈) is used as a fuel for camping stoves. Propane burns completely in oxygen.\n\nBalance the equation for the complete combustion of propane.\nC₃H₈ + ___O₂ ⟶ ___CO₂ + ___H₂O`, marks: 2, command: 'Balance', markScheme: `3 CO₂ and 4 H₂O (1)\n5 O₂ (1)\nC₃H₈ + 5O₂ ⟶ 3CO₂ + 4H₂O\nallow correct multiples, e.g. 2C₃H₈ + 10O₂ ⟶ 6CO₂ + 8H₂O, for 2 marks` },
      { q: `This question is about cracking. Large hydrocarbon molecules from crude oil are broken down into smaller, more useful molecules.\nOne method of cracking passes the vapour of the large hydrocarbons over a hot catalyst.\n\nGive two other conditions or methods that can be used to crack hydrocarbons.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• high temperature (1)\nallow a temperature in the range 450–900 °C\n• mixing the hydrocarbon (vapour) with steam (steam cracking) (1)\n• vaporising the hydrocarbon (before cracking) (1)\nignore heat on its own unless qualified\nignore pressure` },
      { q: `Decane is cracked to produce octane and one other product.\nThe equation for the reaction is:\nC₁₀H₂₂ ⟶ C₈H₁₈ + ______\n\nComplete the equation.\nName the other product.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `C₂H₄ (1)\nethene (1)\nallow H₂C=CH₂ / CH₂=CH₂ / CH₂CH₂` },
      { q: `This question is about reactions of alkenes.\nPropene (C₃H₆) reacts with hydrogen to produce a saturated hydrocarbon. The reaction is used to convert unsaturated hydrocarbons into saturated ones.\n\nName the product of the reaction between propene and hydrogen.\nGive the name of the type of substance that must be present for this reaction to happen at a useful rate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `propane (1)\nallow C₃H₈\ncatalyst (1)\nallow nickel` },
      { q: `Propene (C₃H₆) reacts with bromine at room temperature. One molecule of propene reacts with one molecule of bromine (Br₂). Only one product is formed.\n\nGive the molecular formula of the product.\nName the type of reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `C₃H₆Br₂ (1)\nallow CH₃CHBrCH₂Br / 1,2-dibromopropane\naddition (1)\nignore bromination\ndo not accept displacement / substitution` },
      { q: `A student put a small piece of sodium into a beaker of ethanol.\nThe reaction produces hydrogen gas and a solution of sodium ethoxide.\n\nGive two observations the student would make during the reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• bubbles / fizzing / effervescence (1)\n• the sodium gets smaller / disappears (1)\n• the beaker / mixture gets warmer (1)\nignore gas produced / hydrogen produced\nignore sodium dissolves unless qualified as disappears\ndo not accept flame / burns / explodes` },
      { q: `Ethanol (C₂H₅OH) can be oxidised by warming it with acidified potassium dichromate solution.\n\nName the organic product of this reaction.\nGive the formula of the functional group in this product.`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `ethanoic acid (1)\nallow CH₃COOH\n–COOH (1)\nallow carboxyl / carboxylic acid group drawn out with C=O and O–H` },
      { q: `Esters are used in perfumes and food flavourings because they have pleasant smells.\nA student made an ester by warming a mixture of ethanol and ethanoic acid with a few drops of another acid.\n\nName the ester formed.\nGive the purpose of the few drops of acid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `ethyl ethanoate (1)\nallow CH₃COOC₂H₅ / CH₃COOCH₂CH₃\nto act as a catalyst (1)\nallow to speed up the reaction\nignore sulfuric acid on its own` },
      { q: `A student measured the pH of two acid solutions. Both solutions had the same concentration.\nhydrochloric acid – pH 1\nethanoic acid – pH 3\n\nExplain why the ethanoic acid solution has a higher pH than the hydrochloric acid solution.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `ethanoic acid is a weak acid / hydrochloric acid is a strong acid (1)\nethanoic acid is only partially ionised (in aqueous solution) so the concentration of hydrogen ions / H⁺(aq) is lower (1)\nallow converse for hydrochloric acid: completely ionised so higher concentration of H⁺\nignore dissociated for ionised\ndo not accept ethanoic acid is more dilute / less concentrated` },
      { q: `Butanol (C₄H₉OH) can be used as a fuel for cars.\nRelative atomic masses (Aᵣ): H = 1  C = 12  O = 16\n\nCalculate the relative formula mass (Mᵣ) of butanol.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(4 × 12) + (10 × 1) + 16 (1)\nallow 48 + 10 + 16\n= 74 (1)\nan answer of 74 scores 2 marks\nallow 73 for 1 mark (9 hydrogen atoms used)` },
      { q: `Ethene molecules join together to form poly(ethene). Ethene is shown as: two carbon atoms joined by a double bond, with two hydrogen atoms bonded to each carbon atom (H₂C=CH₂).\n\nDraw the displayed structure of the repeating unit of poly(ethene).`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `two carbon atoms joined by a single bond, each with two hydrogen atoms (1)\nbrackets drawn around the unit with bond lines extending through both brackets and n written after the bracket (1)\n–[–CH₂–CH₂–]ₙ–\ndo not accept a C=C double bond in the repeating unit for the first mark` },
      { q: `Polyesters are made by condensation polymerisation. One polyester is made from ethane-1,2-diol and a dicarboxylic acid.\n\nName the small molecule that is also produced when this polyester is formed.\nGive the number of functional groups that each monomer molecule must have.`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `water (1)\nallow H₂O\ntwo (1)\nallow each monomer has a functional group at each end` },
      { q: `Glycine is the simplest amino acid. Its structure can be written as H₂N–CH₂–COOH.\n\nName the two functional groups in a glycine molecule.`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `amine / amino (group) / –NH₂ (1)\ncarboxylic acid / carboxyl (group) / –COOH (1)\nignore acid group on its own\ndo not accept amide / ammonia` },
      { q: `This question is about natural polymers. Many important polymers are made in living organisms.\n\nDraw one line from each polymer to the monomer it is made from.\nPolymers: Cellulose; Protein; Starch\nMonomers: Amino acids; Ethene; Nucleotides; Sugars`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw lines', markScheme: `Cellulose – Sugars\nProtein – Amino acids\nStarch – Sugars\nall three correct (2)\none or two correct (1)\nmore than one line from a polymer: all lines from that polymer are wrong` },
      { q: `Scientists described the structure of DNA in 1953 using X-ray images of DNA crystals.\n\nDescribe the structure of a DNA molecule.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `two polymer chains / strands (1)\n(arranged / twisted) in the form of a double helix (1)\nallow made of (four different) nucleotide monomers for 1 mark if no other mark awarded\nignore spiral / ladder unless double helix is stated` },
      { q: `A home brewer makes ethanol by fermenting a sugar solution. The brewer adds yeast to the solution in a container.\n\nGive two conditions the brewer should use so that fermentation produces ethanol.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• warm temperature (1)\nallow a temperature in the range 25–35 °C\n• anaerobic / no air / no oxygen (1)\nallow fit an airlock\n• in aqueous solution (1)\nignore yeast (given in the question)\nignore high temperature` },
      { q: `Poly(ethene) is made from ethene monomers (C₂H₄). One sample of poly(ethene) contains chains with 3500 repeating units each.\nRelative atomic masses (Aᵣ): H = 1  C = 12\n\nCalculate the relative formula mass (Mᵣ) of one chain of this poly(ethene).\nIgnore the atoms at the ends of each chain.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Mᵣ of repeating unit = (2 × 12) + (4 × 1) = 28 (1)\n28 × 3500 = 98 000 (1)\nan answer of 98 000 scores 2 marks\nallow 9.8 × 10⁴` },
      { q: `Oil companies compare the amount of each fraction obtained from crude oil with the amount customers want.\nTable 2 shows the supply and demand for some fractions from one refinery, as percentages of the crude oil processed. (data written for this practice question)\nFraction | Supply in % | Demand in %\nLiquefied petroleum gases | 4 | 5\nPetrol | 18 | 30\nKerosene | 13 | 15\nDiesel oil | 20 | 26\nFuel oil and bitumen | 45 | 24\n\nExplain why the oil company cracks the fuel oil fraction.\nUse Table 2.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `supply of fuel oil (45%) is greater than demand (24%) (1)\nsupply of petrol (18%) is less than demand (30%)\nallow supply of the smaller fractions (petrol / diesel / kerosene / gases) is less than demand (1)\ncracking breaks the large molecules into smaller, more useful molecules (such as petrol) (1)\nallow cracking also produces alkenes which are used to make polymers / as a feedstock\nat least one comparison must use data from Table 2 for full marks` },
      { q: `A scientist distilled a 160 g sample of crude oil. The mass of the petrol fraction collected was 37.0 g.\n\nCalculate the percentage by mass of the sample that was collected as the petrol fraction.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(37.0 ÷ 160) × 100 (1)\n= 23.125 (1)\n= 23.1 (%) (1)\nan answer of 23.1 scores 3 marks\nan answer of 23.125 / 23.13 / 23 scores 2 marks\nthe 3 significant figures mark can be awarded for any answer correctly rounded to 3 significant figures from an incorrect calculation` },
      { q: `Poly(chloroethene) is used to make window frames and water pipes. It is made from chloroethene monomers.\nA chloroethene molecule has two carbon atoms joined by a double bond. One carbon atom is bonded to two hydrogen atoms. The other carbon atom is bonded to one hydrogen atom and one chlorine atom (H₂C=CHCl).\n\nComplete the equation for the formation of poly(chloroethene) from n molecules of chloroethene.\nShow the displayed structure of the repeating unit of the polymer.\nn H₂C=CHCl ⟶ ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', diagram: true, markScheme: `two carbon atoms joined by a single bond (1)\ncorrect atoms attached: two H on one carbon, one H and one Cl on the other carbon (1)\nbrackets with bond lines extending through both brackets and n after the bracket (1)\n–[–CH₂–CHCl–]ₙ–\nallow the Cl on either carbon atom\ndo not accept a C=C double bond in the polymer for the first mark` },
      { q: `A student investigated fermentation of a sugar solution using yeast. The student kept the mixture at 30 °C. The equation for the reaction is:\nC₆H₁₂O₆ ⟶ 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂\n\nExplain why the student did not use a temperature of 10 °C or a temperature of 60 °C.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `at 10 °C the reaction / fermentation is (too) slow (1)\nbecause the enzymes in yeast are less active / yeast is less active\nallow particles have less energy / fewer successful collisions\nat 60 °C the enzymes (in the yeast) are denatured (1)\nallow the yeast is killed\nso fermentation stops / slows down / less ethanol is produced (1)\nignore 30 °C is the optimum temperature unless explained` },
      { q: `Ethanol can be made industrially by reacting ethene with steam.\n\nWrite a balanced symbol equation for this reaction.\nGive two conditions used for this reaction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Write', markScheme: `C₂H₄ + H₂O ⟶ C₂H₅OH (1)\nallow CH₂=CH₂ + H₂O ⟶ CH₃CH₂OH\nallow ⇌ for ⟶\nany two from:\n• catalyst (1)\nallow phosphoric acid\n• high temperature (1)\nallow a value in the range 250–350 °C\n• high pressure (1)\nallow a value in the range 50–80 atmospheres\nignore steam (given in the question)` },
      { q: `A teacher gave a student two unlabelled bottles. One bottle contained hexane (C₆H₁₄). The other bottle contained hexene (C₆H₁₂). Both liquids are colourless.\n\nDescribe a chemical test the student could use to identify which bottle contains hexene.\nGive the result for each liquid.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `add bromine water (and shake) (1)\nhexene: (orange to) colourless / decolourises (1)\nhexane: stays orange / no (colour) change (1)\nthe result marks are dependent on the use of bromine water\nallow bromine solution\nignore clear for colourless\ndo not accept goes clear for hexane` },
      { q: `Limescale is calcium carbonate. Some descaling products for kettles contain ethanoic acid. A student added ethanoic acid to sodium carbonate to model what happens.\n\nName the salt formed when ethanoic acid reacts with sodium carbonate.\nComplete and balance the equation for the reaction.\n___CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ ⟶ ___CH₃COONa + ______ + ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `sodium ethanoate (1)\nH₂O and CO₂ (1)\neither order\n2 (in front of CH₃COOH) and 2 (in front of CH₃COONa) (1)\n2CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ ⟶ 2CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂\nallow sodium acetate` },
      { q: `Octane (C₈H₁₈) is one of the alkanes in petrol. The equation for the complete combustion of octane is:\n2C₈H₁₈ + 25O₂ ⟶ 16CO₂ + 18H₂O\nRelative atomic masses (Aᵣ): H = 1  C = 12  O = 16\n\nA car engine burns 5.70 g of octane completely.\nCalculate the mass of carbon dioxide produced.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Mᵣ of C₈H₁₈ = 114 (1)\nmoles of octane = 5.70 ÷ 114 = 0.0500 (mol) (1)\nmoles of CO₂ = 0.0500 × 8 = 0.400 (mol) (1)\nallow 0.0500 × 16 ÷ 2\nmass of CO₂ = 0.400 × 44 = 17.6 (g) (1)\nan answer of 17.6 (g) scores 4 marks\nallow ecf from an incorrect Mᵣ or mole ratio\nalternative method: Mᵣ values 114 and 44 (1); 228 g octane ⟶ 704 g CO₂ (1); 5.70 × 704 ÷ 228 (1); = 17.6 g (1)` },
      { q: `Proteins and poly(ethene) are both polymers.\nProteins are made in living organisms from amino acids. Poly(ethene) is made from ethene.\n\nCompare the formation of a protein with the formation of poly(ethene).`, marks: 4, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `any four from:\n• both form long chain molecules from many small (monomer) molecules joined together (1)\n• proteins are formed by condensation polymerisation; poly(ethene) by addition polymerisation (1)\n• in forming a protein a small molecule / water is also produced; in forming poly(ethene) the polymer is the only product (1)\n• amino acids have two different functional groups (amine and carboxylic acid) / ethene has a C=C double bond (1)\n• a protein is made from different (types of) amino acid monomers; poly(ethene) is made from one type of monomer (1)\n• amino acids react to form polypeptides (which make up proteins) (1)\nto gain 4 marks there must be at least one valid difference` },
      { q: `In fractional distillation, crude oil is heated until it vaporises and the vapour passes into a tall column. The column is hot at the bottom and cooler at the top.\nBitumen is collected near the bottom of the column. Liquefied petroleum gases are collected at the top.\n\nExplain why bitumen is collected near the bottom of the column and liquefied petroleum gases are collected at the top.\nRefer to the size of the molecules and the forces between molecules in your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `bitumen has large / long-chain molecules (and LPG has small molecules) (1)\nlarger molecules have stronger intermolecular forces (1)\nallow converse for small molecules\ndo not accept stronger covalent bonds / breaking bonds\nso bitumen has a (very) high boiling point (1)\nso it condenses (as soon as it enters the column) where the temperature is high / near the bottom (1)\nallow LPG has a low boiling point so does not condense / stays as a gas and rises to the top` },
      { q: `A student made ethanol by fermenting a solution of glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆). The equation for the reaction is:\nC₆H₁₂O₆ ⟶ 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂\nRelative atomic masses (Aᵣ): H = 1  C = 12  O = 16\nThe student used 45.0 g of glucose. The student obtained 18.4 g of ethanol.\n\nCalculate the percentage yield of ethanol.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Mᵣ C₆H₁₂O₆ = 180 (1)\nmoles of glucose = 45.0 ÷ 180 = 0.250 (mol) (1)\nmoles of ethanol = 0.250 × 2 = 0.500 (mol) (1)\nmaximum (theoretical) mass of ethanol = 0.500 × 46 = 23.0 (g) (1)\npercentage yield = (18.4 ÷ 23.0) × 100 = 80.0 (%) (1)\nan answer of 80 (%) scores 5 marks\nallow ecf at each step\nalternative: 180 g glucose ⟶ 92 g ethanol (2), so 45.0 g ⟶ 23.0 g (2), yield = 80.0% (1)` },
      { q: `Crude oil is a mixture of a very large number of compounds. Most of the compounds are hydrocarbons called alkanes.\nThe fractions produced from crude oil are used as fuels and as feedstock for the petrochemical industry.\n\nDescribe how crude oil is separated into fractions by fractional distillation.\nIn your answer you should describe how the properties of the fractions change from the top to the bottom of the column.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed and coherent description of the process of fractional distillation, with a clear link between where fractions are collected and their boiling points, AND correct description of how at least two properties change down the column.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A description of the main steps of fractional distillation AND either a link to boiling point OR some correct description of how properties change.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements about heating crude oil or about the properties of fractions, with little logical sequence.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\nProcess\n• crude oil is heated / vaporised (to about 350 °C or more)\n• the vapour enters the (fractionating) column\n• there is a temperature gradient: hot at the bottom, cooler at the top\n• vapours rise up the column\n• each fraction condenses at the level where the temperature falls below its boiling point\n• fractions are collected / drawn off at different heights\n• gases (with the lowest boiling points) leave at the top\n• the residue (e.g. bitumen) is collected at the bottom\n• a fraction is a mixture of hydrocarbons with a similar number of carbon atoms / similar boiling points\nProperties (top to bottom of column)\n• molecules become larger / chain length increases / more carbon atoms\n• boiling point increases\n• viscosity increases\n• flammability decreases / harder to ignite\nUses\n• fuels include petrol, diesel oil, kerosene, heavy fuel oil and liquefied petroleum gases\n• fractions are used as feedstock to make solvents, lubricants, polymers and detergents` },
      { q: `A teacher has four unlabelled bottles, A, B, C and D. Each bottle contains one colourless liquid. The liquids are:\n• cyclohexane (an alkane)\n• cyclohexene (an alkene)\n• ethanol (an alcohol)\n• ethanoic acid (a carboxylic acid)\nThe teacher has the following reagents: bromine water, sodium carbonate and small pieces of sodium.\n\nPlan a method to identify the liquid in each bottle using only these reagents.\nGive the results you would expect for each liquid.`, marks: 6, command: 'Plan', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A coherent, logically ordered plan that would allow all four liquids to be identified, with correct expected results for each test used.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A plan that would identify at least two of the liquids, with mostly correct expected results; the sequence may not be fully logical.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): One or more relevant tests or results given, but with little logical sequence; may not lead to identification of any liquid.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• use a small sample of each liquid in a separate test tube, a fresh sample for each test\nTest with bromine water\n• add bromine water to each liquid and shake\n• cyclohexene: orange to colourless\n• the other three: no change / stays orange\nTest with sodium carbonate\n• add sodium carbonate to each remaining liquid\n• ethanoic acid: fizzes / bubbles of carbon dioxide\n• (gas turns limewater milky)\n• cyclohexane and ethanol: no fizzing\nTest with sodium\n• add a small piece of sodium to the two remaining liquids\n• ethanol: fizzes / bubbles of hydrogen (gas gives a squeaky pop with a lighted splint)\n• cyclohexane: no reaction\n• note: ethanoic acid also fizzes with sodium, so the sodium carbonate test must be done first (or sodium used only on liquids that did not react with sodium carbonate)\nSafety\n• use small pieces of sodium / wear eye protection / no naked flames because the liquids are flammable\nallow other logical orders of testing that would identify all four liquids` }
    ]
  },
  '4.8': {
    name: 'Chemical Analysis',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about pure substances.\n\nA carton of orange juice is labelled '100% pure'. A chemist says the orange juice is not a pure substance.\n\nWhat is meant by a pure substance in chemistry?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `a single element or a single compound (not mixed with any other substance) (1)\nallow contains only one substance\nignore natural / nothing added` },
      { q: `A student made a gas by adding manganese dioxide to hydrogen peroxide solution.\n\nThe student thought the gas was oxygen. The student put a glowing splint into a test tube of the gas.\n\nGive the result if the gas is oxygen.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `(glowing splint) relights (1)\nallow bursts into flame\nignore pops / squeaky pop` },
      { q: `This question is about tests for gases.\n\nElectrolysis of sodium chloride solution produces a gas at the positive electrode. The gas is chlorine.\n\nA student held damp blue litmus paper in the gas.\n\nGive the result of this test for chlorine.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `(litmus paper) is bleached / turns white (1)\nallow turns red then white\nignore turns red unqualified` },
      { q: `A student used paper chromatography to separate the dyes in a black ink.\n\nThe student drew the start line on the chromatography paper in pencil.\n\nGive one reason why the start line should be drawn in pencil and not in ink.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `pencil (graphite) is insoluble (in the solvent) so it will not move up the paper (1)\nallow ink would dissolve / ink would separate / ink would mix with the dyes being tested\nignore pencil can be rubbed out` },
      { q: `This question is about flame tests.\n\nA student carried out a flame test on a sample of calcium chloride.\n\nComplete the sentence. Choose the answer from: crimson / lilac / orange-red\n\nThe colour of the flame is ______.`, marks: 1, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `orange-red (1)` },
      { q: `A water company tests drinking water for very small amounts of lithium ions.\n\nThe water company uses an instrumental method.\n\nWhich instrumental method could be used to identify lithium ions in the water?\n\nChoose the answer from: chromatography / crystallisation / flame emission spectroscopy`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `flame emission spectroscopy (1)` },
      { q: `This question is about aspirin.\n\nA student made a sample of aspirin. The student measured the melting point of the sample.\n\nThe sample melted between 128 °C and 133 °C. Pure aspirin melts at 136 °C.\n\nWhat do these results show about the student's sample?\n\nGive a reason for your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'What', markScheme: `(the sample is) impure / not pure (1)\nbecause it melts over a range of temperatures\nor\nbecause it melts below the melting point of pure aspirin (1)\nallow does not melt at a specific / fixed temperature` },
      { q: `This question is about formulations.\n\nA paint is made by mixing:\n• a pigment to give the colour\n• a binder to stick the pigment to the surface\n• a solvent to make the paint easy to spread.\n\nEach component is added in a carefully measured quantity.\n\nExplain why the paint is described as a formulation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(the paint is) a mixture that has been designed as a useful product (1)\neach component is added in measured quantities so that the paint has the required properties / each component has a specific purpose (1)` },
      { q: `A student used paper chromatography to investigate a food colouring.\n\nThe food colouring produced one spot. The spot moved 2.7 cm from the start line. The solvent moved 7.5 cm from the start line.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the Rf value of the spot.\n\nUse the equation:\nRf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Rf = 2.7 ÷ 7.5 (1)\n= 0.36 (1)\nallow correct answer with no working for 2 marks\ndo not accept an answer with units` },
      { q: `A scientist used paper chromatography to test a sample of ink.\n\nOne of the dyes in the ink has an Rf value of 0.62 in the solvent used. The solvent moved 6.5 cm from the start line.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the distance moved by this dye from the start line.\n\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `distance = 0.62 × 6.5 (1)\n= 4.0 (cm) (1)\nallow 4.03 for 1 mark if not given to 2 significant figures` },
      { q: `A student tested a red food colouring using paper chromatography.\n\nThe student did the experiment three times, using a different solvent each time. Each time the food colouring produced only one spot.\n\nExplain why the student's results suggest that the red food colouring is a pure substance.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `a pure compound produces a single spot (1)\n(a single spot in all) different solvents shows there are no other substances that could have the same Rf value in one solvent (1)\nallow a mixture would produce more than one spot (in at least one solvent)` },
      { q: `A student investigated a green dye using paper chromatography.\n\nThe student found that the Rf value of the dye in water was 0.35, but the Rf value of the same dye in ethanol was 0.80.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nExplain why the dye has a different Rf value in the two solvents.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the dye is more soluble in ethanol (than in water)\nor\nthe dye is more attracted to ethanol (than to water) (1)\nso the dye spends more time in the mobile phase / moves further up the paper in ethanol (1)\nallow the distribution of the dye between the mobile phase and the stationary phase is different in each solvent for 1 mark` },
      { q: `A student added zinc granules to dilute hydrochloric acid in a test tube. Bubbles of gas were produced.\n\nThe student thought the gas was hydrogen.\n\nDescribe the test for hydrogen.\n\nGive the result if the gas is hydrogen.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `(put a) burning / lighted splint (at the mouth of the test tube) (1)\n(burns with a) (squeaky) pop (1)\ndo not accept glowing splint for the first mark\nthe second mark depends on a splint being used` },
      { q: `This question is about the gas produced when marble chips react with dilute hydrochloric acid.\n\nMarble chips are calcium carbonate.\n\nDescribe the test to show that the gas produced is carbon dioxide.\n\nGive the result of the test.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `bubble the gas through limewater\nor\nshake the gas with limewater (1)\nlimewater turns milky / cloudy (1)\nallow calcium hydroxide solution for limewater\nignore white precipitate unqualified` },
      { q: `This question is about testing for halide ions.\n\nA student tested a solution for chloride ions. The student added dilute nitric acid and then silver nitrate solution.\n\nThe student's friend suggested using dilute hydrochloric acid instead of dilute nitric acid.\n\nExplain why the student should not use dilute hydrochloric acid in this test.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `hydrochloric acid contains chloride ions (1)\n(which would) form a white precipitate with silver nitrate (even if there were no chloride ions in the solution) (1)\nallow would give a false positive (result)` },
      { q: `Fertilisers can contain ammonium sulfate.\n\nA farmer wants to check that a fertiliser solution contains sulfate ions.\n\nDescribe a test to show that the solution contains sulfate ions.\n\nGive the result of the test.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `add dilute hydrochloric acid and barium chloride solution (1)\nwhite precipitate (1)\nallow barium nitrate (solution) for barium chloride\nthe second mark depends on barium chloride / barium nitrate being used` },
      { q: `A technician has two bottles of colourless solution. The labels have fallen off.\n\nOne bottle contains aluminium sulfate solution. The other bottle contains magnesium sulfate solution.\n\nDescribe how sodium hydroxide solution can be used to show which bottle contains aluminium sulfate solution.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `add (a few drops of) sodium hydroxide solution: both form a white precipitate (1)\nadd excess sodium hydroxide solution: the precipitate dissolves with aluminium (sulfate) but not with magnesium (sulfate) (1)\nallow aluminium hydroxide dissolves (in excess) for second mark` },
      { q: `A student added sodium hydroxide solution to two solutions, P and Q.\n\nSolution P contains iron(II) ions. Solution Q contains iron(III) ions.\n\nGive the colour of the precipitate formed with each solution.\n\nSolution P (iron(II)): ______\nSolution Q (iron(III)): ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `P: green (1)\nQ: brown (1)\nallow red-brown / orange-brown for brown\nignore shades of green` },
      { q: `When sodium hydroxide solution is added to copper(II) sulfate solution, a blue precipitate of copper(II) hydroxide forms.\n\nCopper(II) hydroxide is Cu(OH)₂.\n\nWrite the balanced ionic equation for the formation of the precipitate.\n\nInclude state symbols.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Cu²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Cu(OH)₂ (1)\nCu²⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq) → Cu(OH)₂(s) (1)\nthe second mark is for correct state symbols on a correct balanced equation\nignore spectator ions if shown correctly on both sides` },
      { q: `A student did a flame test on a white solid. The flame was bright yellow.\n\nThe student's teacher said that the solid contained sodium ions and also contained potassium ions.\n\nExplain why the flame test did not show that potassium ions were present.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(the solid is a) mixture of metal ions (1)\nthe (bright) yellow colour of sodium masks / hides the lilac colour of potassium (1)\nallow some flame colours can be masked for 1 mark` },
      { q: `This question is about instrumental methods of analysis.\n\nA laboratory tests samples of river water for metal ions. The laboratory uses instrumental methods instead of chemical tests.\n\nGive two advantages of using instrumental methods compared with chemical tests.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• (more) accurate\n• (more) sensitive / can detect very small amounts\n• (more) rapid / faster (1 each)\nallow can measure the concentration of ions\nignore cheaper / easier` },
      { q: `A student did a flame test on a solid. The student used a clean metal wire loop and a Bunsen burner.\n\nThe student cleaned the wire loop by dipping it in dilute hydrochloric acid and holding it in the flame.\n\nGive one reason why the student cleaned the loop.\n\nGive one reason why the student used a blue Bunsen burner flame, not a yellow flame.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `cleaning: to remove any other metal ions / substances left on the loop (which would affect the flame colour) (1)\nallow to prevent contamination\nblue flame: a yellow flame would mask the colour (of the metal ions)\nor\nso the colour of the flame can be seen clearly (1)\nignore blue flame is hotter` },
      { q: `This question is about identifying a white solid.\n\nA student did two tests on a white solid.\n\nTest 1: flame test. The flame was crimson.\nTest 2: a solution of the solid was added to dilute nitric acid and silver nitrate solution. A yellow precipitate formed.\n\nName the white solid.\n\nGive a reason for your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `lithium iodide (1)\ncrimson flame shows lithium (ions) and yellow precipitate shows iodide (ions) (1)\nallow LiI\nthe second mark requires both reasons` },
      { q: `A student used paper chromatography to separate the dyes in a brown ink.\n\nAt the end of the experiment, one spot was still on the start line. There were three other spots higher up the paper.\n\nExplain why one spot did not move from the start line.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `that substance / dye is insoluble in the solvent (1)\nso it is not carried up the paper by the mobile phase / it stays attracted to the paper (1)\nallow it has a stronger attraction to the paper than to the solvent` },
      { q: `This is part of a student's method for paper chromatography.\n\n1. Put spots of ink on a pencil start line.\n2. Stand the paper in a beaker of water so that the water level is below the start line.\n\nExplain why the water level must be below the start line.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `otherwise the ink / dyes would dissolve in the water in the beaker (1)\nso the dyes would not move up the paper / would not separate (1)` },
      { q: `A student set up paper chromatography in a beaker of solvent. The solvent was propanone.\n\nThe teacher told the student to put a lid on the beaker while the solvent moved up the paper.\n\nSuggest two reasons for putting a lid on the beaker.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\n• to stop the solvent evaporating (from the paper / beaker)\n• so the solvent front moves further / so the solvent keeps moving up the paper\n• to reduce the risk of breathing in the (harmful / flammable) vapour (1 each)\nallow so the air in the beaker is saturated with solvent vapour` },
      { q: `This question is about flame emission spectroscopy.\n\nA scientist used flame emission spectroscopy to analyse a solution containing a mixture of metal ions.\n\nThe table shows the wavelengths of the lines in the spectra of three metals, P, Q and R, and of the mixture.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nMetal P: 450 nm, 610 nm\nMetal Q: 520 nm, 670 nm\nMetal R: 480 nm, 590 nm\nMixture: 450 nm, 480 nm, 590 nm, 610 nm\n\nWhich metals are in the mixture?\n\nGive a reason for your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Which', markScheme: `P and R (1)\nall the lines of P and R are in the spectrum of the mixture and none of the lines of Q are (1)\ndo not accept answers including Q` },
      { q: `This question is about paper chromatography.\n\nIn paper chromatography the paper is the stationary phase and the solvent is the mobile phase.\n\nExplain how paper chromatography separates the dyes in a mixture.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the dyes are distributed / separate between the stationary phase and the mobile phase (1)\ndifferent dyes have different attractions to the paper and to the solvent\nor\ndifferent dyes have different solubilities in the solvent (1)\ndyes that are more soluble in the solvent / less attracted to the paper move further up the paper (1)` },
      { q: `A student used paper chromatography to identify a dye in a sweet.\n\nThe dye from the sweet produced one spot. The spot moved 4.2 cm from the start line. The solvent front was 7.0 cm from the start line.\n\nThe table shows the Rf values of four known dyes in the same solvent.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nDye A: 0.25\nDye B: 0.45\nDye C: 0.60\nDye D: 0.82\n\nCalculate the Rf value of the dye from the sweet and identify the dye.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Rf = 4.2 ÷ 7.0 (1)\n= 0.60 (1)\n(dye) C (1)\nallow 0.6\nallow identification of the dye consistent with an incorrect Rf value` },
      { q: `A student wants to find out which metal ion is in a solid salt.\n\nThe student has:\n• a nichrome wire loop\n• dilute hydrochloric acid\n• a Bunsen burner.\n\nDescribe how the student should carry out a flame test on the solid.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `clean the wire loop by dipping it in (dilute hydrochloric) acid and holding it in the flame (until there is no colour) (1)\ndip the loop in the solid (after dipping it in acid so the solid sticks) (1)\nhold the loop in a blue Bunsen burner flame and observe / record the colour of the flame (1)\nallow roaring flame for blue flame` },
      { q: `A student tested a solution of an unknown compound, Z.\n\nTest 1: sodium hydroxide solution was added. A green precipitate formed.\nTest 2: dilute hydrochloric acid and barium chloride solution were added. A white precipitate formed.\n\nIdentify compound Z.\n\nExplain your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `iron(II) sulfate (1)\ngreen precipitate (with sodium hydroxide) shows iron(II) ions / Fe²⁺ (1)\nwhite precipitate (with barium chloride) shows sulfate ions / SO₄²⁻ (1)\nallow FeSO₄\ndo not accept iron sulfate without (II) for the first mark` },
      { q: `This question is about a white solid, W.\n\nA student added dilute hydrochloric acid to solid W. The mixture fizzed. The gas produced turned limewater milky.\n\nThe student then did a flame test on solid W. The flame was orange-red.\n\nName solid W.\n\nExplain how the results identify solid W.`, marks: 3, command: 'Name', markScheme: `calcium carbonate (1)\nfizzing and gas turning limewater milky shows carbon dioxide was made so W contains carbonate ions (1)\norange-red flame shows calcium ions (1)\nallow CaCO₃` },
      { q: `A student measured the boiling points of three samples of water, X, Y and Z, at normal atmospheric pressure.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nSample X: boiled at 100 °C\nSample Y: boiled between 101.2 °C and 102.5 °C\nSample Z: boiled at 100 °C\n\nPure water boils at 100 °C.\n\nA student concluded that samples X and Z are pure water and sample Y is impure.\n\nExplain how the data supports the student's conclusion.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `pure substances boil at a specific / fixed temperature (1)\nX and Z boil at the boiling point of pure water / at a fixed temperature of 100 °C (1)\nY boils over a range of temperatures / above 100 °C so contains another substance / is a mixture (1)` },
      { q: `This question is about a paint, which is a formulation.\n\nA 250.0 g tin of paint contains:\n• pigment: 37.5 g\n• binder: 92.4 g\n• solvent: 120.1 g\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the percentage by mass of binder in the paint.\n\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(92.4 ÷ 250.0) × 100 (1)\n= 36.96 (%) (1)\n= 37.0 (%) (1)\nallow correct answer to 3 significant figures with no working for 3 marks\nthe third mark is for giving an answer correctly to 3 significant figures` },
      { q: `A scientist tested a food colouring, X, using paper chromatography.\n\nFood colouring X produced two spots. One spot moved 1.6 cm and the other spot moved 5.6 cm from the start line. The solvent front moved 8.0 cm from the start line.\n\nThe table shows the Rf values of four dyes, E, F, G and H, in the same solvent.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nDye E: 0.20\nDye F: 0.45\nDye G: 0.70\nDye H: 0.85\n\nDetermine which dyes are in food colouring X.\n\nShow your working.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `Rf of first spot = 1.6 ÷ 8.0 = 0.20 (1)\nRf of second spot = 5.6 ÷ 8.0 = 0.70 (1)\n(first spot is dye) E (1)\n(second spot is dye) G (1)\nallow identification of dyes consistent with incorrect Rf values` },
      { q: `A technician has three bottles of colourless solution. The labels have fallen off the bottles.\n\nThe solutions are:\n• sodium carbonate\n• sodium chloride\n• sodium sulfate.\n\nDescribe tests the technician could use to identify each solution.\n\nGive the results of the tests.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `add dilute acid: only sodium carbonate fizzes / produces a gas that turns limewater milky (1)\nadd dilute hydrochloric acid and barium chloride solution: white precipitate shows sodium sulfate (1)\nadd dilute nitric acid and silver nitrate solution (1)\nwhite precipitate shows sodium chloride (1)\nallow any logical order of tests\nallow barium nitrate for barium chloride` },
      { q: `This question is about paper chromatography.\n\nA student used this method to separate the dyes in a green ink.\n\n1. Draw a start line in ink 1 cm from the bottom of a piece of chromatography paper.\n2. Put a spot of the green ink on the start line.\n3. Pour water into a beaker to a depth of 2 cm.\n4. Stand the paper in the beaker.\n5. Remove the paper when the water has nearly reached the top.\n\nThe student's method did not give valid results.\n\nIdentify two mistakes in the student's method.\n\nExplain how each mistake affects the results.`, marks: 4, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `start line drawn in ink (1)\nthe ink of the line would separate / move up the paper and be confused with the dyes (1)\nwater level is above the start line (1)\nthe dyes would dissolve into the water in the beaker / would not move up the paper (1)\nallow should draw the line in pencil / water level should be below the start line for the mistakes\nallow did not mark the solvent front so Rf values cannot be calculated as one mistake with explanation` },
      { q: `A student tested two white solids, X and Y. Both solids are soluble in water.\n\nThe student's results are shown.\n\nSolid X\n• Flame test: lilac flame\n• Sodium hydroxide solution added: no precipitate\n• Dilute nitric acid and silver nitrate solution added: cream precipitate\n\nSolid Y\n• Flame test: no colour seen\n• Sodium hydroxide solution added: white precipitate that dissolves in excess sodium hydroxide solution\n• Dilute hydrochloric acid and barium chloride solution added: white precipitate\n\nIdentify solids X and Y.\n\nExplain how the results identify each solid.\n\nGive the formula of solid Y.`, marks: 5, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `X is potassium bromide because:\nlilac flame shows potassium ions (1)\ncream precipitate (with silver nitrate) shows bromide ions (1)\n\nY is aluminium sulfate because:\nwhite precipitate that dissolves in excess sodium hydroxide shows aluminium ions (1)\nwhite precipitate with barium chloride shows sulfate ions (1)\n\nformula of Y: Al₂(SO₄)₃ (1)\nallow KBr for potassium bromide\ndo not accept formulae with incorrect subscripts or missing brackets for the fifth mark` },
      { q: `A food company makes a red food colouring. The food colouring might contain a dye, E, that is not allowed in food.\n\nA student is given:\n• a sample of the red food colouring\n• a sample of dye E\n• chromatography paper\n• water and a beaker\n• a pencil, a ruler and capillary tubes.\n\nPlan an investigation to find out whether the red food colouring contains dye E.\n\nYour plan should include how the student would use Rf values.`, marks: 6, command: 'Plan', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A coherent, detailed method that would produce valid results, including how Rf values are calculated and compared to reach a conclusion.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A method with most of the key steps that would produce results, but may lack detail of how Rf values are calculated or used.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple, relevant steps that do not form a workable method.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• draw a start line in pencil near the bottom of the paper\n• use a capillary tube to put a small spot of the food colouring on the start line\n• put a spot of dye E on the start line, separate from the food colouring\n• put water in the beaker so the level is below the start line\n• stand the paper in the beaker (with a lid on)\n• leave until the water has nearly reached the top of the paper\n• remove the paper and mark the solvent front in pencil\n• allow the paper to dry\n• measure the distance from the start line to the centre of each spot\n• measure the distance from the start line to the solvent front\n• Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent\n• if a spot from the food colouring has the same Rf value as dye E, the food colouring could contain dye E\n• repeat using a different solvent to check that the Rf values still match\n• if no spot matches dye E, the food colouring does not contain dye E` },
      { q: `A technician has three white solids in unlabelled bottles. The solids are:\n• aluminium bromide\n• calcium chloride\n• magnesium sulfate.\n\nAll three solids dissolve in water to form colourless solutions.\n\nThe technician has these reagents:\n• sodium hydroxide solution\n• dilute nitric acid and silver nitrate solution\n• dilute hydrochloric acid and barium chloride solution.\n\nThe technician can also do flame tests.\n\nDescribe tests that the technician could use to identify each solid.\n\nGive the results of the tests.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Tests are described that identify both the metal ion and the non-metal ion in each solid, with correct results, so that all three solids are identified.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Tests are described with correct results that would identify at least two of the solids, or that identify the metal ions or the non-metal ions in all three solids.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): One or more relevant tests are described, with some correct results.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• dissolve a sample of each solid in water\n• add a few drops of sodium hydroxide solution to each\n• all three form a white precipitate\n• add excess sodium hydroxide solution\n• the precipitate dissolves only for aluminium (bromide)\n• the precipitates for calcium and magnesium do not dissolve\n• flame test: calcium ions give an orange-red flame\n• magnesium ions give no (characteristic) flame colour\n• add dilute nitric acid then silver nitrate solution\n• cream precipitate shows bromide (aluminium bromide)\n• white precipitate shows chloride (calcium chloride)\n• add dilute hydrochloric acid then barium chloride solution\n• white precipitate shows sulfate (magnesium sulfate)\n• only one test of each type needs to be used if the identification is logical` }
    ]
  },
  '4.9': {
    name: 'Chemistry of the Atmosphere',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about the Earth's atmosphere.\nFor about the last 200 million years the proportions of gases in the atmosphere have been much the same as they are today.\n\nWhat fraction of the Earth's atmosphere today is nitrogen?\nTick one box.\none-fifth\nhalf\nfour-fifths\nnine-tenths`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `four-fifths` },
      { q: `This question is about the Earth's early atmosphere.\nDuring the first billion years of the Earth's existence there was intense volcanic activity. The volcanoes released gases, including large amounts of water vapour.\n\nWhat was formed when the water vapour cooled and condensed?`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `the oceans\nallow seas\nignore water / rain / clouds unqualified` },
      { q: `Greenhouse gases in the atmosphere maintain temperatures on Earth high enough to support life.\n\nWhich gas is a greenhouse gas?\nTick one box.\nArgon\nMethane\nNitrogen\nOxygen`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `Methane` },
      { q: `This question is about pollutants from fuels.\nA faulty gas boiler in a house does not get enough air. The natural gas burns with a yellow flame instead of a blue flame.\n\nName the toxic gas produced by the incomplete combustion of natural gas.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `carbon monoxide\nallow CO\ndo not accept carbon dioxide` },
      { q: `Some coal contains sulfur as an impurity. Burning this coal in power stations releases a gas that causes acid rain.\n\nWhich gas is released?\nTick one box.\nCarbon dioxide\nMethane\nSulfur dioxide\nWater vapour`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `Sulfur dioxide` },
      { q: `A student investigated the percentage of oxygen in air.\nMethod:\n1. Put some damp iron wool into a tube connected to a gas syringe containing air.\n2. Record the total volume of air in the apparatus.\n3. Leave the apparatus for one week, recording the volume of air each day.\nThe iron reacted with oxygen in the air. The volume of air decreased each day until day 5. After day 5 the volume did not change.\n\nSuggest why the volume of air did not change after day 5.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `all the oxygen had reacted / been used up\nallow (all) the iron had reacted\nignore reaction finished unqualified` },
      { q: `A student investigated the percentage of oxygen in air by leaving damp iron wool in a sealed tube connected to a gas syringe. The iron reacted with the oxygen in the air.\nThe student's results (data written for this practice question):\nVolume of air at the start = 120 cm³\nVolume of air at the end = 95 cm³\n\nCalculate the percentage of oxygen in the air.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(120 − 95) / 120 × 100 or 25 / 120 × 100 (1)\n= 20.8 (%) (1)\nallow 20.83 for 1 mark if not rounded\nan answer of 20.8 (%) scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `This question is about how the amount of oxygen in the atmosphere increased.\nAlgae first produced oxygen about 2.7 billion years ago. Over the next billion years plants evolved, and the percentage of oxygen gradually increased.\n\nComplete the word equation for the process that produced the oxygen.\ncarbon dioxide + ______ ⟶ ______ + oxygen`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `water (1)\nglucose (1)\nallow sugar for glucose\ndo not accept formulae that are incorrect` },
      { q: `Scientists think the Earth's early atmosphere was mainly carbon dioxide. Today the atmosphere contains only about 0.04% carbon dioxide.\n\nGive two ways in which the amount of carbon dioxide in the early atmosphere decreased.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• (algae and plants) used carbon dioxide in photosynthesis\n• carbon dioxide dissolved in the oceans / water\n• (dissolved carbon dioxide) formed carbonate compounds that were precipitated / formed sedimentary rocks\n• carbon locked up in fossil fuels (coal, crude oil, natural gas)\nallow formed limestone for sedimentary rocks` },
      { q: `Limestone is a sedimentary rock made mainly of calcium carbonate. Much of the carbon dioxide in the Earth's early atmosphere is now locked up in limestone.\n\nExplain how carbon dioxide from the early atmosphere became locked up in sedimentary rocks.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `carbon dioxide dissolved in the oceans (1)\n(which) formed (insoluble) carbonate compounds that were precipitated / deposited (on the sea floor), producing sedimentary rocks (1)\nallow shells / skeletons of marine organisms containing carbonate were deposited for second mark` },
      { q: `Coal is a fossil fuel. It was formed from the remains of plants that grew in swamps millions of years ago. The remains were buried and compressed.\n\nExplain why the formation of coal decreased the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the plants absorbed / took in carbon dioxide (from the atmosphere) for photosynthesis (1)\nthe carbon (compounds) in the plants was then locked up / stored (in the coal) and not returned to the atmosphere (1)\nallow the plants did not decay (so carbon dioxide was not released) for second mark` },
      { q: `Table 1 shows the composition of the atmospheres of Mars and Venus today.\nTable 1\nMars: carbon dioxide 95%; nitrogen 3%; argon 2%; oxygen less than 0.2%\nVenus: carbon dioxide 96.5%; nitrogen 3.5%; oxygen almost none\n\nScientists think that the Earth's early atmosphere may have been like the atmospheres of Mars and Venus today.\nSuggest two reasons why. Use Table 1.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\n• (Mars and Venus atmospheres are) mainly carbon dioxide, as the early Earth's atmosphere is thought to have been\n• (they contain) little or no oxygen, as in the early Earth's atmosphere\n• (they contain) small amounts of nitrogen, as released by volcanoes on the early Earth\n• Mars and Venus (are thought to) have had volcanic activity / no photosynthesising organisms, so their atmospheres have not changed in the way Earth's has` },
      { q: `Scientists have proposed several theories about the composition of the Earth's early atmosphere and how it was formed.\n\nGive two reasons why these theories are difficult to prove.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• the early atmosphere was a very long time ago / about 4.6 billion years ago\n• no one was there to observe / measure it\n• there is limited evidence\n• evidence has been destroyed / changed over time (e.g. by rock movement or weathering)\nignore cannot do experiments unqualified` },
      { q: `This question is about greenhouse gases.\nThe concentration of methane in the atmosphere has more than doubled since the year 1750. Over the same period the world's population has increased greatly.\n\nGive two human activities that increase the amount of methane in the atmosphere.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• (increased) farming of animals / cattle / livestock\n• (increased) rice farming / paddy fields\n• (decomposition of) waste in landfill sites\n• extraction / leaks of natural gas\nignore farming unqualified\nignore burning fossil fuels` },
      { q: `Table 2 shows the mean concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere, measured at an observatory in Hawaii. The values are rounded.\nTable 2\n1960: 317 parts per million (ppm)\n1980: 339 ppm\n2000: 370 ppm\n2020: 414 ppm\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the concentration of carbon dioxide from 1960 to 2020.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(414 − 317) / 317 × 100 or 97 / 317 × 100 (1)\n= 30.6 (%) (1)\nallow 30.599… for 1 mark if not rounded\nan answer of 30.6 (%) scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `Scientists use computer models to predict how the Earth's climate will change in the future. Different models give different predictions.\n\nGive two reasons why it is difficult to predict future climate change.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• the climate / atmosphere is a very complex system\n• there are many different factors / variables that affect climate\n• models have to be simplified\n• (so) predictions are based on incomplete data / uncertain assumptions\n• future human activities / emissions are not known\nallow speculation / opinions presented in the media can be misleading or incomplete` },
      { q: `Most scientists agree that human activities are causing the mean global temperature of the Earth to increase.\n\nGive two potential effects of global climate change.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• sea level rise (from melting ice / thermal expansion), which may cause flooding / coastal erosion\n• more frequent / more severe storms / extreme weather events\n• changes in the amount / timing / distribution of rainfall\n• temperature and water stress for humans and wildlife\n• changes in the food-producing capacity of some regions\n• changes to the distribution of wildlife species\nignore global warming / hotter weather unqualified\nignore ozone layer` },
      { q: `This question is about carbon footprints.\nThe carbon footprint of a school is the total amount of carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases emitted over the full life cycle of everything the school uses and does.\nThe school is heated by burning natural gas and gets its electricity from power stations that burn fossil fuels.\n\nSuggest two ways the school could reduce its carbon footprint.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\n• use alternative / renewable energy, e.g. fit solar panels / buy electricity from renewable sources\n• improve energy efficiency, e.g. better insulation / double glazing / LED lighting\n• turn off lights / heating / computers when not in use\n• encourage students and staff to walk / cycle / use public transport\n• use a heat pump instead of a gas boiler\n• reduce waste / recycle more (so less goes to landfill)\n• plant trees on the school grounds\nignore use less energy unqualified` },
      { q: `Many governments have agreed targets to reduce emissions of carbon dioxide. However, carbon footprints of many countries have not fallen as quickly as planned.\n\nGive two reasons why actions to reduce carbon footprints may be limited.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• economic reasons / cost of new technology\n• people do not want to change their lifestyle\n• lack of public information / education about the problem\n• lack of international agreement / some countries do not take part\n• some countries depend on fossil fuels for economic development\n• alternative technologies are not yet available / reliable` },
      { q: `A coal-burning power station has been fitted with a carbon capture and storage system. Carbon dioxide is removed from the waste gases. It is then pumped deep underground into old oil and gas fields.\n\nExplain how this system helps to reduce global climate change.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `carbon dioxide is not released into the atmosphere / less carbon dioxide is released (1)\n(carbon dioxide is a greenhouse gas so) less (infrared) radiation is absorbed / the enhanced greenhouse effect is reduced (1)\nallow less carbon dioxide in the atmosphere so less global warming for 2 marks` },
      { q: `Diesel is a fuel obtained from crude oil. Diesel may contain sulfur as an impurity.\n\nExplain how sulfur dioxide is produced when diesel is burned in an engine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(sulfur) impurities in the fuel (1)\nreact with / are oxidised by oxygen (from the air) (1)\nallow S + O₂ → SO₂ for second mark\ndo not accept sulfur from the air` },
      { q: `This question is about oxides of nitrogen.\nPetrol does not contain nitrogen compounds. However, the exhaust gases from petrol cars contain oxides of nitrogen.\n\nExplain why oxides of nitrogen are produced in a petrol engine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `nitrogen and oxygen from the air react (1)\nbecause of the high temperature (in the engine) (1)\ndo not accept nitrogen from the fuel\nallow when the fuel burns / sparks for high temperature` },
      { q: `Burning diesel in vehicle engines can release solid particles of carbon (soot) and unburned hydrocarbons into the air. These are called particulates.\n\nGive two effects of particulates.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `global dimming (1)\nhealth problems for humans / damage to lungs (1)\nallow reduces the amount of sunlight reaching the Earth's surface for global dimming\nallow named breathing / respiratory problem e.g. asthma\nignore pollution unqualified` },
      { q: `Carbon monoxide detectors are fitted in homes that have gas boilers. Carbon monoxide is a colourless and odourless gas.\n\nExplain why carbon monoxide is dangerous.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(carbon monoxide is toxic because) it reduces the capacity of the blood to carry oxygen (1)\nit cannot be seen / smelled so is not detected (without a detector) (1)\nallow combines with haemoglobin (in red blood cells) for first mark\nignore poisonous unqualified` },
      { q: `When methane burns in a limited supply of air, incomplete combustion produces carbon monoxide and water.\n\nBalance the equation for this reaction.\n___ CH₄ + ___ O₂ ⟶ ___ CO + ___ H₂O`, marks: 2, command: 'Balance', markScheme: `2CH₄ + 3O₂ → 2CO + 4H₂O\ncorrect CH₄, CO and H₂O (2, 2, 4) (1)\ncorrect O₂ (3) (1)\nallow multiples / allow 1, 1½, 1, 2 for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Table 3 shows the mass of some pollutants released by two new cars travelling 1 km (data written for this practice question).\nTable 3\nPetrol car: carbon monoxide 0.50 g; oxides of nitrogen 0.03 g; particulates 0.001 g\nDiesel car: carbon monoxide 0.25 g; oxides of nitrogen 0.08 g; particulates 0.004 g\n\nCompare the pollutants released by the petrol car with those released by the diesel car.\nUse Table 3.`, marks: 2, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `any two from:\n• petrol car releases more / double the carbon monoxide (0.50 g vs 0.25 g)\n• petrol car releases less oxides of nitrogen (0.03 g vs 0.08 g)\n• petrol car releases less / a quarter of the particulates (0.001 g vs 0.004 g)\nallow converse statements about the diesel car\nmust be comparative` },
      { q: `Thousands of climate scientists publish papers about the evidence for climate change. Before publication, each paper is peer reviewed.\n\nExplain why peer review is important.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `other scientists / experts check the work (before it is published) (1)\nto check the evidence / method / conclusions are valid / to reduce false claims / to prevent bias (1)\nignore so it is correct unqualified\nallow so that the findings are trusted / accepted for second mark` },
      { q: `This question is about the greenhouse effect.\nA diagram shows radiation from the Sun passing through the atmosphere to the Earth's surface. Arrows show radiation leaving the Earth's surface; some arrows pass out into space and some are shown being sent back towards the surface by greenhouse gas molecules.\n\nDescribe how greenhouse gases keep the Earth warm.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `short wavelength radiation from the Sun passes through the atmosphere (1)\nthe Earth (absorbs energy and) emits / radiates long(er) wavelength / infrared radiation (1)\n(some of) the infrared radiation is absorbed (and re-emitted) by greenhouse gases (so energy is kept in the atmosphere) (1)\nallow named greenhouse gas: carbon dioxide / methane / water vapour\ndo not accept answers referring to the ozone layer` },
      { q: `Octane (C₈H₁₈) is a hydrocarbon in petrol. The equation for the complete combustion of octane is:\n2C₈H₁₈ + 25O₂ ⟶ 16CO₂ + 18H₂O\nRelative formula masses (Mᵣ): C₈H₁₈ = 114; CO₂ = 44\n\nCalculate the mass of carbon dioxide produced when 1.00 kg of octane burns completely.\nGive your answer in kg to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `moles of octane = 1000 / 114 = 8.77 (mol) (1)\nmoles of carbon dioxide = 8.77 × 8 = 70.2 (mol) (1)\nmass = 70.2 × 44 = 3088 g = 3.09 (kg) (1)\nalternative method:\n228 g octane produces 704 g carbon dioxide (1)\n1000 / 228 × 704 (1)\n= 3.09 (kg) (1)\nallow 3088 g for 2 marks\nan answer of 3.09 (kg) scores 3 marks` },
      { q: `About 2.7 billion years ago the Earth's atmosphere contained little or no oxygen. Today the atmosphere contains about 21% oxygen.\n\nDescribe how the percentage of oxygen in the atmosphere increased.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `algae (first) produced oxygen (1)\nby photosynthesis (1)\nover the next billion years plants evolved (and also produced oxygen), so the percentage of oxygen gradually increased (to a level that enabled animals to evolve) (1)\nallow ‘algae and plants photosynthesised’ for 2 marks` },
      { q: `Scientists drilled ice cores from the Antarctic ice sheet. Bubbles of air trapped in the ice show the concentration of carbon dioxide in the past. Other measurements in the ice show the temperature at the time.\nTable 4 shows some results (data written for this practice question). Temperature difference is compared with the mean temperature today.\nTable 4\n400 000 years ago: carbon dioxide 280 ppm; temperature difference +1 °C\n350 000 years ago: carbon dioxide 190 ppm; temperature difference −8 °C\n240 000 years ago: carbon dioxide 290 ppm; temperature difference +2 °C\n150 000 years ago: carbon dioxide 190 ppm; temperature difference −9 °C\n125 000 years ago: carbon dioxide 285 ppm; temperature difference +2 °C\n20 000 years ago: carbon dioxide 185 ppm; temperature difference −8 °C\n\nThe concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere is now about 420 ppm.\nWhat can you conclude from Table 4?\nGive three conclusions.`, marks: 3, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any three from:\n• when carbon dioxide concentration is high the temperature is high / higher\n• (there is a) positive correlation between carbon dioxide concentration and temperature\n• carbon dioxide concentration and temperature both rose and fell / varied naturally in the past\n• carbon dioxide concentration in the past was between 185 and 290 ppm\n• the carbon dioxide concentration now (420 ppm) is higher than at any time in Table 4\n• (so) the temperature may rise above any value in Table 4\nignore carbon dioxide causes the temperature change (correlation does not show cause)` },
      { q: `Table 5 shows the annual greenhouse gas emissions of a country, by sector (data written for this practice question).\nTable 5\nTransport: 113 million tonnes\nEnergy supply: 85 million tonnes\nBusiness: 70 million tonnes\nHomes: 68 million tonnes\nAgriculture: 47 million tonnes\nOther: 23 million tonnes\n\nCalculate the percentage of the total emissions that came from transport.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `total = 113 + 85 + 70 + 68 + 47 + 23 = 406 (million tonnes) (1)\n113 / 406 × 100 (1)\n= 27.8 (%) (1)\nallow ecf from incorrect total for marks 2 and 3\nallow 27.83 for 2 marks if not rounded\nan answer of 27.8 (%) scores 3 marks` },
      { q: `Many old stone buildings in cities are made of limestone. In the 1970s the carvings on these buildings were being damaged quickly. Since then, laws have reduced the amount of sulfur allowed in fuels such as diesel and coal.\n\nExplain why the use of low-sulfur fuels reduces the damage to limestone buildings.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `less sulfur dioxide is produced (when the fuel burns) (1)\n(sulfur dioxide dissolves in water in the atmosphere / clouds) so less acid rain (1)\nacid rain reacts with / dissolves limestone / calcium carbonate (1)\nallow converse statements about high-sulfur fuels` },
      { q: `A family of four people plans to travel 300 km to visit relatives. They could travel together in their car, or they could each buy a train ticket.\nCarbon footprint data (data written for this practice question):\nCar: 0.17 kg of carbon dioxide equivalent per km for the whole car\nTrain: 0.035 kg of carbon dioxide equivalent per km for each passenger\n\nCalculate the difference between the carbon footprints of the two ways of travelling for the whole family.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `car = 300 × 0.17 = 51 (kg) (1)\ntrain = 300 × 0.035 × 4 = 42 (kg) (1)\ndifference = 9 (kg of carbon dioxide equivalent) (1)\nallow ecf from one incorrect value\nan answer of 9 (kg) scores 3 marks` },
      { q: `Table 6 shows the concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere and the mean global temperature change, compared with the 1951–1980 mean, for four years (data written for this practice question).\nTable 6\n1960: carbon dioxide 317 ppm; temperature change −0.03 °C\n1980: carbon dioxide 339 ppm; temperature change +0.26 °C\n2000: carbon dioxide 370 ppm; temperature change +0.39 °C\n2020: carbon dioxide 414 ppm; temperature change +0.98 °C\nA student concluded: 'Table 6 proves that the increase in carbon dioxide caused the increase in global temperature.'\n\nEvaluate the student's conclusion.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `any four from:\n• both carbon dioxide concentration and temperature increased (from 1960 to 2020)\n• (there is a) positive correlation\n• use of data e.g. carbon dioxide increased by 97 ppm and temperature by about 1 °C\n• the increases are not proportional / temperature rose faster between 2000 and 2020\n• a correlation does not prove one causes the other\n• other factors could affect temperature, e.g. changes in energy from the Sun / volcanic activity / other greenhouse gases such as methane\n• only four years of data / data over a short time period\n• (but) carbon dioxide is a greenhouse gas, and peer-reviewed evidence shows human activities are likely to be causing the temperature rise\nfor 4 marks there must be a judgement on whether the conclusion is justified, e.g. the data support but do not prove the conclusion` },
      { q: `Table 7 shows the composition of the Earth's early atmosphere, as estimated by some scientists, and the composition of the atmosphere today.\nTable 7\nEarly atmosphere (estimated): mainly carbon dioxide; large amount of water vapour; some nitrogen; small proportions of methane and ammonia; little or no oxygen\nAtmosphere today: about 80% nitrogen; about 20% oxygen; small proportions of carbon dioxide, water vapour and noble gases\n\nCompare the Earth's early atmosphere with the atmosphere today.`, marks: 4, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `any four from:\n• early atmosphere was mainly carbon dioxide, today there is only a small proportion / about 0.04%\n• early atmosphere had little or no oxygen, today about 20% / one-fifth is oxygen\n• early atmosphere had some nitrogen, today about 80% / four-fifths is nitrogen (so nitrogen is now the main gas)\n• early atmosphere had more water vapour\n• early atmosphere contained methane and ammonia, today these are absent / only traces\n• both contain nitrogen / carbon dioxide / water vapour\nmust be comparative to score each mark` },
      { q: `A power station burns 1000 tonnes of coal each day. The coal contains 2.0% sulfur by mass.\nThe sulfur reacts with oxygen to produce sulfur dioxide:\nS + O₂ ⟶ SO₂\nRelative atomic masses (Aᵣ): O = 16; S = 32\nThe power station removes 95% of the sulfur dioxide from the waste gases before they are released.\n\nCalculate the mass of sulfur dioxide released into the atmosphere each day.\nGive your answer in tonnes.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mass of sulfur = 1000 × 2.0 / 100 = 20 (tonnes) (1)\nMᵣ SO₂ = 32 + (2 × 16) = 64 (1)\nmass of sulfur dioxide produced = 20 × 64 / 32 = 40 (tonnes) (1)\nmass released = 40 × 5 / 100 = 2.0 (tonnes) (1)\nallow ecf at each step\nan answer of 2.0 / 2 (tonnes) scores 4 marks` },
      { q: `A household heats its home with a gas boiler that burns methane (CH₄). The household burns 1200 kg of methane each year.\nCH₄ + 2O₂ ⟶ CO₂ + 2H₂O\nRelative formula masses (Mᵣ): CH₄ = 16; CO₂ = 44\nThe household replaces the boiler with an electric heat pump. The heat pump uses 9000 kWh of electricity each year. Generating each kWh of this electricity releases 0.20 kg of carbon dioxide (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage reduction in the mass of carbon dioxide released each year when the household changes from the gas boiler to the heat pump.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `moles of methane = 1200 / 16 = 75 (kmol) or 1 200 000 / 16 = 75 000 (mol) (1)\nmass of carbon dioxide from boiler = 75 × 44 = 3300 (kg) (1)\nmass of carbon dioxide from heat pump = 9000 × 0.20 = 1800 (kg) (1)\npercentage reduction = (3300 − 1800) / 3300 × 100 (1)\n= 45.5 (%) (1)\nalternative for first two marks: 16 kg methane produces 44 kg carbon dioxide (1), so 1200 × 44 / 16 = 3300 (kg) (1)\nallow ecf at each step\nan answer of 45.5 (%) scores 5 marks` },
      { q: `The Earth is about 4.6 billion years old. The composition of its atmosphere has changed greatly over this time.\nFor about the last 200 million years the atmosphere has been about 80% nitrogen and 20% oxygen, with small proportions of other gases.\n\nDescribe how the Earth's atmosphere is thought to have formed and how it changed to produce the atmosphere we have today.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed and coherent description of the formation of the early atmosphere and of how oxygen increased and carbon dioxide decreased, with processes linked to the changes.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A description of the formation of the early atmosphere and of some changes, with at least one process linked to a change.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements about the early atmosphere or the changes, which are not linked.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\nformation\n• intense volcanic activity during the first billion years\n• volcanoes released gases that formed the early atmosphere\n• mainly carbon dioxide with little or no oxygen (like Mars and Venus today)\n• also water vapour, nitrogen and small proportions of methane and ammonia\n• water vapour condensed to form the oceans\ncarbon dioxide decreased\n• carbon dioxide dissolved in the oceans\n• formed carbonate compounds that were precipitated as sedimentary rocks\n• algae and plants absorbed carbon dioxide for photosynthesis\n• carbon locked up in sedimentary rocks such as limestone and in fossil fuels (coal from plant remains; crude oil and natural gas from plankton)\noxygen increased\n• algae first produced oxygen about 2.7 billion years ago by photosynthesis\n• carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen\n• plants evolved and oxygen gradually increased\n• until there was enough oxygen for animals to evolve\nnitrogen\n• nitrogen built up gradually and is unreactive so remained in the atmosphere` },
      { q: `A town has a busy main road used by diesel lorries and petrol cars. Nearby there is an old power station that burns coal.\nAir quality monitors in the town detect carbon monoxide, sulfur dioxide, oxides of nitrogen and particulates.\n\nExplain how these pollutants are produced and describe their effects.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed and coherent explanation of how most of the pollutants are produced, linked to their effects.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): An explanation of how some of the pollutants are produced, with some correct effects.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements about some pollutants or their effects.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\ncarbon monoxide\n• produced by incomplete combustion of the fuel (hydrocarbons) in a limited supply of oxygen\n• toxic: reduces the capacity of the blood to carry oxygen\n• colourless and odourless so not easily detected\nsulfur dioxide\n• sulfur impurities in coal / diesel react with oxygen when the fuel burns\n• causes respiratory problems\n• dissolves in water in the atmosphere to cause acid rain\n• acid rain damages buildings / statues, trees and aquatic life\noxides of nitrogen\n• nitrogen and oxygen from the air react at the high temperatures in engines / furnaces\n• cause respiratory problems and acid rain\nparticulates\n• solid particles of carbon (soot) and unburned hydrocarbons from incomplete combustion, especially in diesel engines\n• cause global dimming\n• cause health problems / damage to lungs` }
    ]
  },
  '4.10': {
    name: 'Using Resources',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about drinking water.\n\nIn the UK, potable water is produced from rain water collected in reservoirs. The water is passed through filter beds. The water is then sterilised.\n\nGive one substance that is used to sterilise water.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `chlorine (1)\nallow ozone\nallow ultraviolet / UV light\nignore filtering / boiling\nignore chloride\ndo not accept fluorine` },
      { q: `This question is about alloys.\n\nBronze and brass are both alloys of copper. Brass is an alloy of copper and zinc.\n\nComplete the sentence. Choose the answer from: iron / lead / tin\n\nBronze is an alloy of copper and ______.`, marks: 1, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `tin (1)` },
      { q: `This question is about gold jewellery.\n\nThe purity of gold is measured in carats. Pure gold is 24 carat.\n\nA ring is made from 18 carat gold.\n\nWhat is the percentage of gold in 18 carat gold?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `75 (%) (1)\nallow 0.75 / ¾\n(18 ÷ 24 × 100)` },
      { q: `This question is about the Haber process.\n\nThe Haber process is used to manufacture ammonia. The raw materials for the Haber process are nitrogen and hydrogen.\n\nGive the source of the nitrogen used in the Haber process.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `(the) air (1)\nallow atmosphere\nignore fractional distillation unqualified\ndo not accept natural gas` },
      { q: `This question is about fertilisers.\n\nPhosphate rock cannot be used directly as a fertiliser because it is insoluble. Phosphate rock is treated with acids to produce soluble salts.\n\nPhosphate rock can be treated with sulfuric acid.\n\nName the fertiliser produced.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `single superphosphate (1)\nallow a mixture of calcium phosphate and calcium sulfate\ndo not accept triple superphosphate` },
      { q: `This question is about corrosion.\n\nAluminium is more reactive than iron. However, aluminium window frames do not corrode away when left outside for many years.\n\nWhy do aluminium window frames not corrode away?`, marks: 1, command: 'Why', markScheme: `(aluminium has a) layer of aluminium oxide (on the surface which) protects / stops (further) reaction (with air and water) (1)\nallow oxide layer acts as a barrier\nignore aluminium is unreactive\nignore aluminium does not rust` },
      { q: `This question is about the Earth's resources.\n\nHumans use the Earth's resources to provide food, warmth, shelter and transport. Some resources are finite and some are renewable.\n\nWhich two resources are finite?\n\nTick two boxes.\n\nCotton\nCrude oil\nIron ore\nTimber\nWool`, marks: 2, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `crude oil (1)\niron ore (1)\nif more than two boxes ticked, deduct one mark for each extra tick` },
      { q: `This question is about water.\n\nIn some countries, there is very little fresh water. Sea water is desalinated to produce potable water. One method of desalination is distillation.\n\nExplain why desalination of sea water is only used in countries where supplies of fresh water are limited.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(desalination) requires large amounts of energy (1)\nso it is expensive (compared with treating fresh water) (1)\nallow reverse osmosis uses (expensive) membranes / requires large amounts of energy\nignore takes a long time` },
      { q: `This question is about pure water.\n\nA student was given two colourless liquids, A and B. One liquid was pure water. The other was a solution of salt in water.\n\nDescribe two tests the student could do to show which liquid was pure water.\n\nGive the result for pure water in each test.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any two from:\n• measure the boiling point – pure water boils at 100 °C\n• evaporate a sample to dryness – pure water leaves no residue / no solid\n• measure the freezing point / melting point – pure water freezes at 0 °C (1 each)\neach mark requires the test and the correct result\nignore pH 7 / universal indicator turns green (salt solution is also neutral)\nignore taste` },
      { q: `This question is about distilling sea water.\n\nA student distilled sea water. The student heated sea water in a flask. The vapour passed through a condenser and the liquid formed was collected in a beaker.\n\nExplain why the liquid collected in the beaker is pure water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(only) water evaporates / boils (from the sea water) (1)\n(dissolved) salts are left in the flask (because they have much higher boiling points) (1)\nallow the water vapour condenses (to form pure water) for 1 mark if neither of the above is given\nignore salt is filtered out` },
      { q: `This question is about waste water treatment.\n\nSewage is treated before it can be safely released into the environment. The first stage of sewage treatment is screening and grit removal.\n\nGive two types of material removed by screening and grit removal.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• large objects / rags / plastics / wood\n• grit / sand / stones\n• solid objects (unqualified) (1 each)\nmax 1 for solids alone\nignore microbes / bacteria\nignore dissolved substances` },
      { q: `This question is about sewage sludge.\n\nAfter sedimentation, sewage is separated into an effluent and a sludge. The sludge is then broken down by bacteria in the absence of oxygen.\n\nGive the name of this process.\n\nGive one use of a product of this process.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `anaerobic digestion (1)\n\nany one from:\n• biogas / methane used as a fuel\n• (digested waste) used as a fertiliser (1)\nallow biogas used to generate electricity\nignore compost` },
      { q: `This question is about waste water from industry.\n\nA factory produces waste water. The factory must treat the waste water before it is released into a river. The waste water needs more treatment than domestic sewage.\n\nSuggest why waste water from industry needs more treatment than domestic sewage.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `(industrial waste water) may contain harmful chemicals / toxic substances (1)\nallow named example, e.g. heavy metal compounds\n(as well as) organic matter (which must also be removed) (1)\nallow so the harmful chemicals do not pollute the river / harm living organisms\nignore it is dirtier` },
      { q: `(Higher tier) This question is about phytomining.\n\nNickel can be obtained from land that contains low concentrations of nickel compounds. Plants are grown on the land, then harvested and burned.\n\nThe ash from one hectare of plants has a mass of 15 tonnes. The ash contains 1.2% nickel by mass. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the mass of nickel in the ash from one hectare.\n\nGive your answer in kilograms (kg).\n\n1 tonne = 1000 kg`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `15 × 1.2 ÷ 100 = 0.18 (tonnes) (1)\n0.18 × 1000 = 180 (kg) (1)\nallow 15 000 × 0.012 = 180 (kg) for 2 marks\nallow 0.18 (kg) for 1 mark\nan answer of 180 (kg) scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `(Higher tier) This question is about extracting copper.\n\nA solution containing copper sulfate is produced from low-grade copper ore. Scrap iron is added to the solution to obtain copper metal.\n\nWrite the ionic equation for the reaction between iron atoms and copper ions.\n\nUse the formula Fe²⁺ for iron ions.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Fe + Cu²⁺ → Fe²⁺ + Cu\n\nall formulae correct (1)\ncorrect reactants and products on the correct sides, balanced (1)\nallow state symbols: Fe(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) → Fe²⁺(aq) + Cu(s)\nignore incorrect state symbols\ndo not accept sulfate ions in the equation unless they appear on both sides` },
      { q: `(Higher tier) This question is about life cycle assessments (LCAs).\n\nA supermarket carried out an LCA to compare paper shopping bags with plastic shopping bags. The supermarket published a report that included only some stages of the life cycle.\n\nGive two reasons why the conclusions of this LCA might not be reliable.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• effects of some pollutants are difficult to measure / need value judgements / are subjective\n• a shortened / selective LCA can be misused (to support claims)\n• the supermarket may be biased / may want to show one bag in a favourable way\n• some stages were left out so the full environmental impact is not shown (1 each)\nignore cost of the bags` },
      { q: `This question is about shopping bags.\n\nTable 1 shows the energy needed to make one shopping bag and the number of times each bag is used. (data written for this practice question)\n\nType of bag | Energy needed to make one bag in MJ | Number of times bag is used\nPaper | 1.8 | 1\nPlastic | 1.2 | 4\n\nCalculate how many times more energy is needed per use for the paper bag than for the plastic bag.\n\nUse Table 1.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(energy per use for plastic bag =) 1.2 ÷ 4 = 0.30 (MJ) (1)\n1.8 ÷ 0.30 = 6 (times) (1)\nallow 6.0\nan answer of 6 scores 2 marks\nallow 1 mark for 1.5 (1.8 ÷ 1.2)` },
      { q: `This question is about recycling metals.\n\nScrap aluminium drinks cans are collected, melted and made into new aluminium products. Aluminium is extracted from its ore, bauxite, by electrolysis.\n\nSuggest two reasons why recycling aluminium is more sustainable than extracting aluminium from bauxite.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\n• uses less energy (than electrolysis)\n• conserves bauxite / ore (which is finite)\n• less mining / quarrying needed (so less damage to habitats / landscape)\n• reduces waste going to landfill\n• produces less carbon dioxide (from burning fossil fuels for energy) (1 each)\nignore cheaper unqualified\nignore it is better for the environment unqualified` },
      { q: `This question is about rusting.\n\nA student set up three test tubes, each containing an iron nail.\n\nTube A: nail in tap water, open to the air\nTube B: nail in water that had been boiled, with a layer of oil on top of the water\nTube C: nail in dry air with calcium chloride to absorb water vapour, with a stopper in the top\n\nAfter one week, only the nail in tube A had rusted.\n\nExplain why the nail in tube B did not rust.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `boiling removes dissolved air / oxygen from the water (1)\nthe (layer of) oil stops air / oxygen dissolving back into the water / reaching the nail (1)\nallow rusting needs both air / oxygen and water, so 1 mark for no oxygen present if neither marking point given\nignore oil stops water reaching the nail` },
      { q: `This question is about preventing corrosion.\n\nIron buckets are often galvanised. Galvanised iron is coated with a layer of zinc. Zinc is more reactive than iron.\n\nThe zinc coating on a galvanised iron bucket is scratched so that some iron is exposed. The iron does not rust.\n\nExplain why the iron does not rust.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `zinc is more reactive (than iron) so zinc reacts / corrodes (with oxygen and water) instead (of the iron) (1)\nsacrificial protection (1)\nallow zinc loses electrons / is oxidised instead of iron\nignore the zinc acts as a barrier (the barrier is broken)` },
      { q: `This question is about gold alloys.\n\nPure gold is too soft to make jewellery. Gold used for jewellery is often an alloy of gold with copper. Copper atoms are a different size from gold atoms.\n\nExplain why the alloy of gold and copper is harder than pure gold.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(different sized atoms) distort the layers (of atoms) (1)\nso the layers cannot slide over each other as easily (1)\nallow disrupts the regular arrangement\nignore molecules\ndo not accept reference to bonds between layers breaking\nmax 1 if reference to ions sliding` },
      { q: `This question is about steels.\n\nTable 2 shows information about three types of steel. (data written for this practice question)\n\nType of steel | Elements in addition to iron | Property\nLow carbon steel | about 0.1% carbon | soft and easily shaped\nHigh carbon steel | about 1.0% carbon | strong but brittle\nStainless steel | chromium and nickel | hard and resistant to corrosion\n\nA company makes kitchen sinks.\n\nWhich steel should the company use to make kitchen sinks?\n\nGive a reason for your answer. Use Table 2.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `stainless steel (1)\n(because it is) resistant to corrosion / does not rust (when in contact with water) (1)\nthe second mark is dependent on the first mark\nallow hard (wearing) as a second reason only alongside corrosion resistance\nignore strong` },
      { q: `This question is about polymers.\n\nPoly(ethene) is a thermosoftening polymer. Melamine is a thermosetting polymer. Melamine is used to make heat-resistant kitchen worktops.\n\nExplain why melamine does not melt when heated.\n\nRefer to the structure of the polymer chains in your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(polymer chains in melamine have) cross-links (between the chains) (1)\n(which are strong covalent bonds so) the chains cannot move / slide over each other (when heated) (1)\nallow chains are held in fixed positions\ndo not accept intermolecular forces break / weak bonds between chains for the second mark` },
      { q: `This question is about poly(ethene).\n\nLow density (LD) poly(ethene) and high density (HD) poly(ethene) are both produced from the monomer ethene. LD poly(ethene) is used for carrier bags. HD poly(ethene) is used for water pipes.\n\nExplain why LD and HD poly(ethene) have different properties.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(they are produced using) different reaction conditions / different catalysts (1)\nallow different temperature / pressure\n(so the polymers have) different structures / arrangement of chains (1)\nallow LD has branched chains and HD has (mostly) straight / unbranched chains (so HD chains pack more closely)\nignore different monomers` },
      { q: `This question is about ceramics.\n\nGlass is a ceramic. Most of the glass we use is soda-lime glass. Borosilicate glass is used for laboratory glassware.\n\nBorosilicate glass is made by heating a mixture of sand and boron trioxide.\n\nName the two substances, other than sand, that are heated to make soda-lime glass.`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `sodium carbonate (1)\nlimestone (1)\nallow calcium carbonate for limestone\nallow soda / soda ash for sodium carbonate\nignore sand\ndo not accept sodium chloride / lime` },
      { q: `This question is about composites.\n\nFibreglass is used to make boat hulls. Fibreglass is a composite. It consists of glass fibres embedded in a polymer resin.\n\nDescribe what is meant by a composite.\n\nUse fibreglass as an example.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `(a composite is made of) two (or more) materials combined (1)\none material is the reinforcement (glass fibres) and the other is the matrix / binder (polymer resin) that surrounds it (1)\nallow the composite has properties that are different from / better than the separate materials for 1 mark\nignore mixture unqualified` },
      { q: `(Higher tier) This question is about the Haber process.\n\nThe equation for the reaction in the Haber process is:\n\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)\n\nA pressure of about 200 atmospheres is used.\n\nExplain why a high pressure increases the yield of ammonia.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `there are fewer molecules of gas on the right-hand side / product side (1)\nallow 4 molecules / moles of gas form 2 molecules / moles of gas\n(so increasing pressure) shifts the position of equilibrium to the right (1)\nallow equilibrium moves towards ammonia / the products\nignore reference to rate` },
      { q: `This question is about analysing water.\n\nA student investigated the mass of dissolved solids in a sample of sea water.\n\nThis is the method used.\n1. Measure the mass of an empty evaporating basin.\n2. Measure 50.0 cm³ of filtered sea water into the evaporating basin.\n3. Heat the evaporating basin until all the water has evaporated.\n4. Measure the mass of the evaporating basin and solid.\n\nMass of empty evaporating basin = 45.62 g\nMass of evaporating basin and solid = 47.12 g\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the mass of dissolved solids in 1 dm³ of the sea water.\n\n1 dm³ = 1000 cm³`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `47.12 − 45.62 = 1.50 (g) (1)\n1.50 × (1000 ÷ 50.0) (1)\n= 30 (g) (1)\nallow 30.0\nallow ecf from an incorrect mass of solid\nan answer of 30 (g) scores 3 marks` },
      { q: `This question is about drinking water in the UK.\n\nPotable water is water that is safe to drink. Potable water is not pure water because it contains dissolved substances.\n\nDescribe how rain water is treated to produce potable water in the UK.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `an appropriate source of fresh water is chosen (1)\nallow collected in reservoirs / lakes / rivers / ground water (aquifers)\n(water is) passed through filter beds (to remove solids) (1)\nallow filtered\n(water is) sterilised (to kill microbes) (1)\nallow using chlorine / ozone / ultraviolet light\nignore distillation / desalination\nignore boiling` },
      { q: `This question is about fertilisers.\n\nAmmonium nitrate (NH₄NO₃) is used as a fertiliser to supply nitrogen to plants.\n\nCalculate the percentage by mass of nitrogen in ammonium nitrate.\n\nRelative atomic masses (Aᵣ): H = 1  N = 14  O = 16`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Mᵣ = (2 × 14) + (4 × 1) + (3 × 16) = 80 (1)\n(2 × 14) ÷ 80 × 100 (1)\n= 35 (%) (1)\nallow 35.0\nallow ecf from an incorrect Mᵣ\n17.5 (%) (one N atom only) scores 2 marks\nan answer of 35 (%) scores 3 marks` },
      { q: `This question is about the Haber process.\n\nIn the Haber process, nitrogen and hydrogen are passed over a catalyst. Only some of the nitrogen and hydrogen react to form ammonia. The gases leaving the reactor are a mixture of ammonia, nitrogen and hydrogen.\n\nDescribe how ammonia is separated from the mixture and what happens to the unreacted nitrogen and hydrogen.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `the mixture is cooled (1)\nthe ammonia liquefies / condenses (and is removed) (1)\nallow ammonia has the highest boiling point\nthe unreacted nitrogen and hydrogen are recycled / returned to the reactor (1)\nignore filtering\ndo not accept nitrogen and hydrogen liquefy` },
      { q: `(Higher tier) This question is about phytomining.\n\nCopper-rich ores are becoming scarce. A mining company plans to obtain copper from land containing low-grade copper ores using phytomining.\n\nDescribe how phytomining is used to produce copper compounds.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `plants are grown on land containing (low-grade) copper ore / copper compounds (1)\nthe plants absorb copper compounds (through their roots) (1)\nthe plants are harvested and burned (1)\nthe ash contains copper compounds (1)\nmax 3\nallow ash is dissolved in acid to produce a solution of copper compounds\nignore extracting copper from the ash (the question asks about copper compounds)` },
      { q: `This question is about producing ammonium sulfate.\n\nA student made ammonium sulfate in the laboratory. The student reacted ammonia solution with dilute sulfuric acid, then crystallised the ammonium sulfate.\n\nAmmonium sulfate is also manufactured on a large scale in industry.\n\nGive three differences between the laboratory preparation and the industrial production of ammonium sulfate.`, marks: 3, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any three from:\n• laboratory is small scale / batch and industry is large scale / continuous\n• laboratory uses a titration and crystallisation; industry uses several stages\n• in industry the raw materials are made on site: ammonia from the Haber process and sulfuric acid made from sulfur\n• in the laboratory the reactants are bought / supplied as dilute solutions\n• laboratory uses dilute solutions in glassware; industry uses large reaction vessels / plant (1 each)\neach difference must compare both processes\nignore cost unqualified` },
      { q: `This question is about recycling aluminium.\n\nThe energy needed to produce 1 kg of aluminium is:\n\n• 210 MJ from bauxite (aluminium ore)\n• 11 MJ from recycled aluminium\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in energy needed when 1 kg of aluminium is produced from recycled aluminium instead of from bauxite.\n\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `210 − 11 = 199 (MJ) (1)\n199 ÷ 210 × 100 = 94.761 9 (1)\n= 94.8 (%) (1)\nthe third mark is for the correct answer to 3 significant figures\nallow ecf for the 3 s.f. mark\n5.24 (%) scores 2 marks (11 ÷ 210 × 100)\nan answer of 94.8 (%) scores 3 marks` },
      { q: `(Higher tier) This question is about the Haber process.\n\nThe equation for the reaction in the Haber process is:\n\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)\n\nThe forward reaction is exothermic.\n\nThe Haber process uses a temperature of about 450 °C and an iron catalyst.\n\nExplain why these conditions are used.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `temperature:\na lower temperature would give a higher yield (because the forward reaction is exothermic) (1)\nbut a lower temperature would make the rate (of reaction) too slow (1)\nso 450 °C is a compromise between rate and yield (1)\n\ncatalyst:\nthe iron catalyst increases the rate (of reaction) (1)\nallow reaches equilibrium faster\nallow catalyst does not affect the yield / position of equilibrium\nignore catalyst lowers activation energy unqualified\ndo not accept a higher temperature increases the yield` },
      { q: `This question is about producing potable water.\n\nIn the UK, most potable water is produced from fresh water. In some countries, potable water is produced from sea water.\n\nCompare the production of potable water from fresh water with the production of potable water from sea water.`, marks: 4, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `any four from:\n• both sources need to be sterilised (e.g. with chlorine / ozone / UV)\n• both may be filtered (to remove solids)\n• fresh water has low levels of dissolved salts but sea water has high levels of dissolved salts\n• sea water must be desalinated but fresh water does not need desalination\n• desalination is by distillation or reverse osmosis (membranes)\n• producing potable water from sea water needs much more energy\n• so producing potable water from sea water is more expensive (1 each)\nmaximum of 3 marks if only differences or only similarities are given\nignore reference to taste` },
      { q: `(Higher tier) This question is about extracting copper.\n\nThe Earth's resources of copper-rich ores are being used up. Traditional mining involves digging, moving and disposing of large amounts of rock.\n\nCopper can be extracted from low-grade ores using bioleaching.\n\nDescribe how copper metal is obtained from low-grade ore using bioleaching.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `bacteria are used (on the ore) (1)\nthe bacteria produce leachate solutions (1)\nthe leachate contains copper compounds / copper ions (1)\ncopper is obtained from the solution by displacement using scrap iron (1)\nor\ncopper is obtained from the solution by electrolysis (1)\nmax 4\nignore smelting\ndo not accept plants absorb copper compounds (phytomining)` },
      { q: `This question is about the manufacture of ammonia.\n\nThe equation for the reaction in the Haber process is:\n\nN₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃\n\nIn one hour, 1400 kg of nitrogen is passed into a reactor. The mass of ammonia produced in that hour is 255 kg. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the percentage yield of ammonia.\n\nRelative atomic masses (Aᵣ): H = 1  N = 14\n\nAssume that all the nitrogen could react.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Mᵣ N₂ = 28 and Mᵣ NH₃ = 17 (1)\nmoles of N₂ = 1 400 000 ÷ 28 = 50 000 (mol) (1)\nmoles of NH₃ = 50 000 × 2 = 100 000 (mol) (1)\nmaximum (theoretical) mass of NH₃ = 100 000 × 17 = 1 700 000 g = 1700 kg (1)\npercentage yield = 255 ÷ 1700 × 100 = 15 (%) (1)\nallow 15.0\nallow working in kg throughout\nalternative method using masses:\n28 kg N₂ produces 34 kg NH₃ (2)\n1400 kg N₂ produces 1400 × 34 ÷ 28 = 1700 kg NH₃ (2)\n255 ÷ 1700 × 100 = 15 (%) (1)\nallow ecf at each step\nan answer of 15 (%) scores 5 marks` },
      { q: `This question is about life cycle assessments (LCAs).\n\nA drinks company is deciding whether to sell lemonade in glass bottles or in plastic (PET) bottles.\n\nTable 3 shows information from an LCA for one bottle of each type. (data written for this practice question)\n\nStage / factor | Glass bottle | Plastic (PET) bottle\nRaw materials | sand, limestone, sodium carbonate | crude oil\nMass of bottle in g | 380 | 25\nEnergy to make one bottle in MJ | 7.5 | 2.4\nCarbon dioxide released during transport of 1000 full bottles in kg | 32 | 18\nNumber of times the bottle can be reused | about 20 | 1\nDisposal | recycled by melting, or landfill (does not decompose) | recycled into new products, or landfill (non-biodegradable)\n\nEvaluate the use of glass bottles compared with plastic bottles.\n\nUse Table 3 and your own knowledge.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed and coherent evaluation is given, with relevant comparisons of both types of bottle across several stages of the life cycle, using data from the table, leading to a justified conclusion.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): An attempt is made to compare both types of bottle at more than one stage, with some use of data. A conclusion may be given but may not be fully justified.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements are made about one or both types of bottle. The logic may be unclear.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content\nRaw materials:\n• raw materials for glass (sand, limestone) are plentiful\n• crude oil for PET is a finite resource / non-renewable\nManufacture:\n• glass needs 3 times more energy per bottle (7.5 vs 2.4 MJ)\n• energy is often from fossil fuels, so more carbon dioxide released for glass\nUse / transport:\n• glass bottles are much heavier (380 g vs 25 g) so more fuel is used in transport\n• glass releases more carbon dioxide in transport (32 kg vs 18 kg per 1000 bottles)\n• glass can be reused about 20 times so energy per use is lower (7.5 ÷ 20 = 0.375 MJ compared with 2.4 MJ)\n• reuse needs energy / water for washing and collection\nDisposal:\n• both can be recycled\n• neither decomposes in landfill; PET is non-biodegradable and lasts a long time\n• plastic waste can pollute oceans / harm wildlife\nConclusion:\n• a justified conclusion, e.g. glass is better overall if bottles are reused many times, or plastic is better if bottles are used only once\n• allow either conclusion if justified\nTo access Level 3 a conclusion must be given and justified` },
      { q: `This question is about analysing and purifying water.\n\nA student was given a sample of water from a stream. The student was asked to analyse the water and then produce pure water from it.\n\nThe student had access to normal laboratory apparatus.\n\nDescribe a method the student could use to:\n• find the pH of the water\n• find the mass of dissolved solids in the water\n• purify the water by distillation.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A coherent method is described with relevant detail for all three parts, in a logical sequence, which would lead to valid results.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): The bulk of a method is described with mostly relevant detail. At least two parts are covered. There may be steps missing or in the wrong order.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements are made about at least one part. The response may lack a logical structure.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content\npH:\n• filter the water (to remove insoluble solids)\n• add universal indicator (or dip universal indicator paper) and compare the colour with a colour chart\n• or use a (calibrated) pH meter / pH probe\nDissolved solids:\n• measure the mass of an empty evaporating basin\n• measure a known volume of filtered water, e.g. 50 cm³ with a measuring cylinder, into the basin\n• heat (gently) to evaporate the water\n• allow to cool and measure the mass of the basin and solid\n• reheat and reweigh until the mass is constant\n• subtract to find the mass of dissolved solids\nDistillation:\n• heat the water in a flask (with anti-bumping granules)\n• the water boils / evaporates\n• the vapour passes into a (cold water) condenser\n• the vapour condenses and pure water is collected in a beaker\n• the thermometer should read 100 °C\n• dissolved solids remain in the flask\nSafety:\n• wear eye protection / take care with hot apparatus` }
    ]
  }
};
